-- Clio: full abstracts, part 7 of 7 (172 studies). Run after 01-05 and the seed files. Safe to re-run.

-- 04_hardening.sql forces row level security on studies, so even the SQL Editor (the table owner) can only
-- read it. This file lifts that for the owner inside one transaction and puts it back before committing.
begin;
alter table public.studies no force row level security;

update public.studies s
   set abstract = v.abstract
  from (values
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930287$q$, $q$Unmanned Surface Vehicle (USV) swarms face significant challenges during dynamic target encirclement in complex marine environments, particularly regarding strong target maneuverability and strict safety constraints. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel encirclement and capture method. The proposed approach integrates a bio-inspired pursuit-evasion dynamic game with fixed-time safety control. Drawing on the behavioral mechanism of dynamic cooperative blocking and escape space compression in group predation in nature, a multi-pursuer single-evader dynamic game model is established. The payoff functions of pursuers and the evader are constructed to characterize the strategic interaction between both sides, and adaptive cooperative encirclement strategies for the USV swarm are generated based on dynamic game equilibrium. To address environmental disturbances and the requirement of rapid response in complex marine environments, a fixed-time safety controller is designed. Taking the desired encirclement positions generated by the dynamic game as control objectives, a safety constraint mechanism is constructed by introducing control barrier functions, which achieves rapid convergence and cooperative collision avoidance of the USV swarm. Furthermore, the fixed-time convergence of position errors and the satisfaction of safety constraints for the control system are proven via Lyapunov stability theory. Finally, dynamic target encirclement numerical simulations under ocean current disturbances verify the effectiveness of the proposed method. The results demonstrate that the USV swarm can adapt to continuous target maneuvering, realize stable encirclement and capture, and satisfy safety constraints.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930290$q$, $q$Classifying blue basin trap captures of Tropinota hirta according to environmental conditions can support timely and data-driven management of this flower-feeding pest in almond orchards; however, imbalanced class distributions pose important challenges for machine-learning models. This study proposes an interpretable PSO-XGBoost with Partial SMOTE framework for classifying T. hirta abundance levels derived from blue basin trap captures using temporal and meteorological variables. From a biomimetic perspective, Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) is a bio-inspired optimization algorithm that emulates the collective search and information-sharing behavior observed in natural swarms, translating principles of biological collective intelligence into a computational optimization process. The dataset consisted of 256 trap observations categorized into High (H), Low (L), and Medium (M) abundance classes, with 180, 55, and 21 observations, respectively, indicating substantial class imbalance. Eleven temporal and meteorological variables were used as predictors, while PestCount, representing the observed number of T. hirta adults captured in the blue traps and used to derive the target classes, was excluded from the predictor set to prevent target leakage. The proposed framework combines controlled minority-class oversampling using Partial SMOTE, bio-inspired PSO for XGBoost hyperparameter optimization, and rule extraction for model interpretability. A Partial-SMOTE ratio ablation analysis identified L = 0.80 and M = 0.40 as the most favorable class-specific oversampling configuration, avoiding complete class balancing while improving overall and class-sensitive predictive performance. Performance was evaluated using stratified five-fold cross-validation, with oversampling applied exclusively to the training folds to prevent information leakage. The proposed method achieved a mean Accuracy of 89.45% and a mean Weighted F1-score of 88.79%, representing the best overall performance among the evaluated methods. The ablation analysis showed that PSO-based optimization improved conventional XGBoost, while the proposed Partial-SMOTE configuration outperformed both class-weighted XGBoost and PSO-XGBoost without oversampling. Robustness was further assessed using 10-times repeated stratified five-fold cross-validation, where the proposed method achieved 87.51 ± 3.75% Accuracy, 86.94 ± 3.71% Weighted F1-score, and 71.60 ± 7.40% Macro F1-score. Friedman and post-hoc Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction confirmed statistically significant performance differences in favor of the proposed method. In addition, human-readable IF-THEN rules with bounded temporal and meteorological conditions were extracted from the optimized model to improve interpretability. The results indicate that integrating a biomimetic swarm-intelligence optimization strategy with controlled oversampling, blue-trap monitoring, and interpretable machine learning provides a promising framework for predicting T. hirta abundance levels and supporting environmentally compatible monitoring and pest-management decision-making in almond orchards.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930295$q$, $q$Anticancer peptides (ACPs) have emerged as a promising class of therapeutic molecules due to their low toxicity and high specificity. However, experimentally identifying ACPs is labor-intensive and low-throughput, making virtual screening with intelligent models an attractive alternative. Despite recent progress, the number of ACP samples available in existing databases remains limited, which may cause predictive models to overfit the labeled information and exhibit suboptimal generalization. To address this issue, we propose a semi-supervised prediction framework with bio-inspired data augmentation for ACP identification, called AugACP. Specifically, we incorporate semantic knowledge into masked Transformer pretraining to guide ACP sequence augmentation and provide more reliable supervised signals. Based on the augmented data, we adopt a semi-supervised learning strategy that selects high-confidence pseudo-labels from augmented peptides to improve prediction performance. In the computational experiments, AugACP outperforms state-of-the-art baseline methods under various experimental configurations, particularly in generalizing to unseen oligopeptides. Moreover, t-SNE visualizations indicate that AugACP learns more discriminative embeddings for separating ACPs from non-ACPs, while external validation on novel ACPs also achieves an F1-score of 0.9272. These results highlight the predictive power of AugACP and its potential value in promoting anticancer drug discovery.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930307$q$, $q$The South African automotive industry is under increasing pressure to improve the operational efficiency and sustainability of its aftersales services. Biomimicry was applied to enhance maintenance, repair and waste management processes at a medium-sized Western Cape dealership. Although overall customer satisfaction scores were high, significant gaps were identified between customer treatment ratings and satisfaction with repair quality, indicating inefficiencies in service delivery. Repeat customer complaints persisted due to diagnostic errors, inconsistent repair quality, and lack of innovative sustainable waste practices. The Biomimicry Challenge to Biology methodology guided the research. Root cause analysis tools (Business Process Mapping, Why-Why trees, and Fishbone Diagrams) identified seven functional requirements, mapped to the Biomimicry Taxonomy to derive nature-inspired design principles. Three biomimicry concepts were generated and evaluated using the Analytic Hierarchy Process; the highest-ranking concept, Decentralised Resource Distribution Inspired by Ant Colonies, was emulated into AntLink, a digital system that replaces rigid hierarchy with a collaborative, real-time "digital pheromone trail" linking managers, service advisors and technicians. Verification confirmed that AntLink satisfied all functional requirements, including first-time repair resolution targets, standardised diagnostic checklists for recurring processes, and waste category monitoring. Validation combined stakeholder surveys across operational roles, showing overall positive consensus with good agreement on functional requirement alignment, and an assessment against Biomimicry 3.8's Life's Principles confirmed conceptual alignment with resilience, adaptability and sustainability. AntLink illustrates a practical route for translating nature-inspired design into measurable operational and environmental gains in the automotive aftersales sector.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930317$q$, $q$Upper-limb rehabilitation robots should provide adaptive assistance while avoiding excessive robotic intervention when users can contribute to movement. A key challenge is that measured interaction forces contain both voluntary and passive mechanical components, making direct force based participation assessment unreliable. This study proposes an Assist-as-Needed control framework based on a learned passive interaction force reference for a four degree of freedom upper-limb rehabilitation robot. Passive experiments with five healthy participants were conducted during eating and drinking tasks, and interaction forces were recorded at the upper-arm and forearm interfaces. The results revealed clear task, interface, and directional dependence in passive interaction. A Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) model was then developed to estimate the expected passive interaction force from robot kinematics, movement phase, and task information, achieving a maximum (R 2 ) of approximately 0.92. The deviation from the estimated passive reference was used to construct a control-oriented participation estimate, which was combined with trajectory tracking performance to regulate adaptive impedance. Controller-level simulation showed that robotic assistance decreased with increasing prescribed interaction contribution and increased again when tracking performance deteriorated. These results support the feasibility of using a learned passive interaction reference for Assist-as-Needed regulation and provide a basis for subsequent active human validation of the participation estimator.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930339$q$, $q$Introduction: Hearing loss and cognitive impairment are among the most common chronic conditions of aging, and a growing body of evidence links the two. The 2020 and 2024 Lancet Commissions identified midlife hearing loss as one of the largest potentially modifiable risk factors for dementia, raising the possibility that auditory rehabilitation could help preserve cognition. Methods: This narrative review provides a lifespan perspective by summarizing current evidence on the relationship between hearing loss and cognitive decline, the cognitive domains most affected, and the mechanisms proposed to explain the association, including the cognitive load, common cause, cascade, and overdiagnosis hypotheses, together with their neurobiological correlates. Results: We then examine the effect of auditory interventions on cognition in adults, considering hearing aids and cochlear implants separately, and discuss landmark trials such as ACHIEVE, which suggest that cognitive benefits may be greatest in older adults at increased risk of decline. Finally, we address the distinct situation of children, in whom early auditory deprivation may shape the development of working memory and executive function and in whom the timely fitting of hearing devices is critical. Conclusions: Overall, the evidence supports hearing loss as a modifiable contributor to cognitive decline and highlights early detection and rehabilitation as promising, though not universally effective, strategies for protecting cognitive health.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930443$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: Few studies have examined the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on the clinical readiness of dental students in the provision of care. This study examined whether dental students who graduated during the pre-pandemic period (2019 and before) would have higher self-reported clinical skills confidence than those who graduated during the post-pandemic period (2025 and after) at the only two Historically Black Colleges and Universities (HBCUs) in the United States that teach dentistry. The study hypothesized that the change in educational methods that resulted from the pandemic would result in a reduction in self-reported confidence during the senior year. Methods: A cross-sectional online survey was sent to dental graduates from two Historically Black Colleges and Universities in the United States. The questionnaire included a validated 40-item instrument to measure self-reported confidence during senior year in clinical procedures and questions about relevant demographic variables. Results: In fixed prosthodontics, confidence decreased significantly for tooth preparation, impressions, try-in of ceramic-fused-to-metal restorations, and cementation of fixed prostheses ( p p p Conclusions: The findings provide further evidence of the adverse effect of the COVID-19 pandemic on the African American dental workforce. Given the limited access to care that patients traditionally treated by HBCU dentists have, this study provides evidence that the underserved community may be more adversely affected by the pandemic than previously described.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930516$q$, $q$Background/objectives: Orally administered herbal preparations have gained attention as complementary approaches in supportive cancer care; however, their clinical efficacy and safety remain uncertain. This systematic review critically evaluated evidence from randomized clinical trials investigating these interventions in patients with cancer. Methods: The review followed Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) 2020 guidelines and was prospectively registered in PROSPERO. PubMed/MEDLINE, ScienceDirect, Springer Nature Link, CENTRAL, Scopus, LILACS, ClinicalTrials.gov, and WHO ICTRP were searched for English-language studies published from January 1990 through 8 September 2026. Risk of bias was assessed using RoB 2, and outcome-level certainty was evaluated using GRADE. Results: Eight randomized clinical trials met the eligibility criteria and were included in the qualitative synthesis. The studies evaluated Gamiguibi-tang, Rikkunshito, holy basil ( Ocimum tenuiflorum ), Alcea digitata - Malva sylvestris, Annona muricata, Malva sylvestris - Althaea digitata, Jing Si herbal tea, and Renshen Yangrong Tang across heterogeneous cancer populations. Individual trials reported potentially favorable findings for sleep, fatigue, chemotherapy-induced anorexia, anxiety, salivary function, xerostomia, gastrointestinal symptoms, and quality of life. However, the Jing Si herbal tea trial found no statistically significant between-group differences in fatigue or quality of life, and exploratory ex vivo cytotoxicity findings did not demonstrate clinical antitumor efficacy. Meta-analysis was not feasible because of substantial clinical and methodological heterogeneity. All evaluated outcomes were rated as very-low-certainty evidence, primarily because of risk of bias and serious imprecision. No serious intervention-related adverse events were identified in the published reports, but adverse-event monitoring was inconsistent and no trial was adequately powered to establish safety. Conclusions: The available evidence is insufficient to establish definitive efficacy or safety or to support routine clinical implementation. Adequately powered multicenter trials using botanically authenticated and chemically characterized preparations, clinically meaningful outcomes, interaction assessment, and long-term pharmacovigilance are required.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930635$q$, $q$Chest X-ray (CXR) imaging remains a widely used diagnostic modality for the detection of thoracic diseases, including pneumonia, tuberculosis, pleural effusion, lung cancer and cardiomegaly. Despite the extensive use of CXR examinations in clinical practice, the development of robust multi-label artificial intelligence (AI) systems remains constrained by limitations in dataset availability, annotation consistency and reproducible labeling methodologies. Existing public datasets frequently rely on heterogeneous labeling protocols, limited pathology coverage or annotations that inadequately account for linguistic negation and uncertainty within radiology reports. In this paper, we introduce MultiCaRe-Thorax, a curated thoracic benchmark dataset derived from the publicly available MultiCaRe repository, with a reproducible negation-aware natural language processing (NLP) framework for automated generation of multi-label thoracic disease annotations. Unlike existing CXR benchmarks that are distributed in their pre-curated form, MultiCaRe-Thorax provides a fully reproducible pipeline from raw case reports to curated dataset, establishing a new resource that is both scalable and transparent. Our pipeline performs thoracic case identification, image-report matching, duplicate removal, data cleaning, disease-specific label extraction and quality-control verification to produce a curated cohort comprising 5252 chest radiographs from 3415 unique patients. Sixteen clinically relevant thoracic pathologies were automatically annotated using a dictionary- and pattern-based NLP framework incorporating explicit negation detection and n-gram mining, achieving 95% accuracy against 500 manually reviewed reports (Cohen's κ=0.89). Sixteen clinically relevant thoracic pathologies were automatically annotated using a dictionary- and pattern-based NLP framework incorporating explicit negation detection and n-gram mining, achieving 95% agreement against 500 manually reviewed reports. Five ImageNet-pretrained convolutional neural network (CNN) architectures (ResNet50, DenseNet121, EfficientNetB0, EfficientNetB7 and InceptionV3) with two hybrid ensemble configurations were evaluated under a patient-level data partitioning protocol. Among individual architectures, DenseNet121 attains the best AUC on tuberculosis (0.757) and pleural effusion (0.767) with roughly one-third the parameters of ResNet50, while ResNet50 attains the higher mean AUC across all sixteen pathologies (0.688 vs. 0.682) and on interstitial lung disease (0.769) and lung mass/cancer (0.777); we report this as an accuracy-efficiency trade-off rather than a single best architecture. The DenseNet121-InceptionV3 ensemble achieves the highest reported AUC of 0.821 for lung mass/cancer detection. We report 95% confidence intervals for the evaluated ensemble and remaining backbones and note substantial estimation uncertainty for the rarest classes (e.g., congenital heart disease, n=8 test cases). Grad-CAM visualizations were also employed to provide clinically interpretable explanations of model predictions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930712$q$, $q$Fungal infection is a major cause of hatching failure in amphibian embryos. However, studies in Hynobiidae remain limited. Across four breeding seasons (10 November 2022-15 April 2026), we combined field monitoring of hatching rates and mould-affected egg proportions, lesion phenotyping, 18S and 16S rRNA gene sequencing, and a conservation intervention to characterise egg-capsule mould-like deterioration in the Anji salamander ( Hynobius amjiensis ), identify candidate pathogenic microorganisms, and evaluate the effect of the intervention on the survival rate of Hynobius amjiensis. The proportion of mould-affected eggs differed significantly among breeding seasons (2.05%, 4.02%, 8.24%, and 7.09%; p p = 0.010; 16S: p = 0.045). The relative abundances of Ascomycota, Zoopagomycota, and Basidiomycota were higher in affected embryos, and those of Chytridiomycota and Mucoromycota were higher in affected intestinal samples, but no bacterial taxon was consistently highly abundant. The intervention group had a significantly higher hatching rate (65.47% vs. 46.57%; p p < 0.001) than the natural-condition group. These findings can inform disease prevention and control in hynobiid salamanders and other amphibians.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930943$q$, $q$Transferring gaze estimation models from mobile terminals to in-vehicle driving scenarios faces challenges from differences in device form, viewpoint, and imaging conditions. To address this, this study proposes a cross-scene adaptation framework that integrates composite multi-stage transfer learning with distributed multi-camera fusion for personalized in-vehicle gaze estimation. The framework builds upon a multi-level information element fusion baseline (MIEF) and explores three transfer paths (CMP1-CMP3) for progressive personalized adaptation from a large-scale mobile source domain to an in-vehicle target scenario. A distributed multi-camera platform is established, producing gaze calibration data (GCD) for cross-session training and driving gaze and road scene data (DRD) for visualization validation. A Multi-Camera Fusion Model (MCF) is designed with two paradigms, MCF-Lite and MCF-Plus, to balance accuracy improvement and computational complexity. Experimental results demonstrate that the composite multi-stage transfer learning path CMP3 achieves an average error of 0.277 across camera views, with a best single-camera mean error of 0.244. Building on this, the distributed MCF-Plus model with the full C1 + C2 + C3 configuration further reduces the mean error to 0.160 and the standard deviation to 0.127, with 96.88% of predicted gaze points falling within the target calibration block (Error < 0.5). The proposed method provides a feasible technical pathway for personalized adaptation of gaze estimation models from mobile-terminal source domains to in-vehicle multi-camera target scenarios, and offers preliminary experimental evidence for gaze behavior perception and driving gaze heatmap generation in real environments.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930961$q$, $q$Generative artificial intelligence can enhance the quality and visibility of advanced learners' work while making it harder to determine what the resulting performance reveals about their independent competence. This theoretical paper develops the Relational Epistemic Agency Model for Advanced Learners (REAM-AL) through a structured database search and an integrative synthesis of scholarship on talent development, extended and distributed cognition, cognitive offloading, metacognition, epistemic agency, intellectual ownership, transfer, and assessment validity. The model organizes five focal constructs across four analytical layers: developmental epistemic readiness, cognitive task distribution, epistemic decision distribution, intellectual ownership, and the relationship among supported performance, independent competence, and transfer. Their configuration may produce relational cognitive extension, in which AI broadens thinking while epistemic authority remains with the learner, or developmental substitution, in which the system performs operations the learner is expected to develop. REAM-AL treats these as interaction patterns rather than fixed student categories. It offers a framework for task design and assessment that distinguishes joint human-AI performance from independently sustained and transferable competence. The proposed constructs and relationships remain theoretical and require direct testing with advanced children and adolescents across varied tasks, developmental periods, and AI conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930968$q$, $q$Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) allows university teachers to delegate cognitive work while deciding how students should engage with it. Using codebook thematic analysis, we examined multi-source accounts from 25 teachers recruited through an AI-focused learning community and its professional networks in China. Materials comprised interviews with 22 teachers, three documentary cases, self-narratives and reflective essays. Three themes organize the findings: reconstructing the teacher role, defending professional judgment, and managing teachers' and students' cognitive participation. Participants described disciplinary expertise as a basis for evaluating AI outputs and reported practices intended to retain human engagement. Instrumental and partnership orientations overlapped; descriptive comparison with later use profiles did not establish a simple relationship between partnership language and usage intensity. Because the protocol introduced several focal topics, we distinguish prompted positions from volunteered formulations. The proposed belief-strategy-practice cycle is a heuristic interpretation of retrospective accounts, not a demonstrated mechanism. The study contributes an account of how teachers connect their own cognitive offloading with their reported organization of students' learning opportunities. Its claims concern this AI-engaged sample rather than university teachers generally.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42930978$q$, $q$Digital assessments record item-visit durations and revisiting. These traces can reveal responses to task difficulty, but achievement associations do not establish stable person-level measures. We linked digitalPIRLS 2021 process, response, and achievement data across 25 education systems, obtaining 121,908 students with usable process records and 116,206 complete cases for the primary achievement models. Item difficulty was estimated from other students in five groups, excluding each target student's own responses. We measured how each student's first-visit time and revisiting changed with difficulty. More difficult items elicited longer first visits in all 25 systems, whereas the average difficulty effect on revisiting was weak. A one-standard-deviation increase in time adaptation was associated with 19.92 overall-reading points, compared with 3.19 points for revisit adaptation. Effect magnitudes varied substantially across systems. Repeated analyses of separate item halves showed limited stability for time adaptation (0.317) and moderate stability for revisit adaptation (0.529), after correction for test length. Time adaptation estimated from four fifths of the items remained associated with performance on the remaining fifth in all 25 systems. These findings support time adaptation as a task-responsive behavioral indicator within digitalPIRLS. They do not establish a highly reliable trait score or identify a single cognitive mechanism.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931084$q$, $q$Periploca chevalieri ( Apocynaceae Juss.) is an endemic medicinal plant from Cabo Verde that has long been used in traditional medicine, particularly for the management of diabetes and cough. However, to date, no studies focusing on potential adverse effects compromising its safe use have been performed. The present study aimed to investigate the safety profile of a standardized 70% hydroethanolic extract of Periploca chevalieri aerial parts (PcEE) using complementary in vivo and in vitro toxicological approaches. Repeated oral toxicity of PcEE at doses of 170, 600, and 1000 mg/kg body weight was evaluated in CD-1 mice over 14 days. Clinical signs, body weight, and food intake were monitored, as well as important biochemical parameters (hepatic, renal, and pancreatic biomarkers). Genotoxicity was investigated using the bacterial reverse mutation assay (Ames test) with five different strains of Salmonella Typhimurium in the presence and absence of a metabolizing system. The results obtained showed no significant variations ( p p P. chevalieri aerial parts in traditional medicine.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931118$q$, $q$Traditional herbal preparations are frequently used alongside prescription and non-prescription medicines, yet conventional medication reconciliation may not preserve the preparation details needed to interpret interaction and safety evidence. We propose the community-contextualized translational ethnopharmacy framework (CTEF) as a testable conceptual architecture for pharmacy practice. Developed through a problem-oriented conceptual synthesis and a targeted, non-systematic comparator mapping, CTEF treats the exact reported preparation as the clinical exposure and embeds it within a review of the patient's complete medication and clinical context. The revised framework is iterative and comprises five functions: exposure characterization; comprehensive medication and clinical reconciliation; appraisal of the evidence profile and transferability to the reported preparation; consequence-sensitive action selection using patient vulnerability and explicit escalation/de-escalation rules; and culturally responsive communication, documentation, and follow-up. The central hypothesis is that, compared with a prespecified standard medication-reconciliation workflow, CTEF will improve exposure-characterization completeness, inter-rater reproducibility, and concordance with blinded external adjudication without unacceptable increases in consultation burden, unnecessary escalation, or patient-reported communication harm. CTEF is not a scale, treatment guideline, or validated clinical decision rule. Staged co-design, simulation, reliability, feasibility, comparative, and cross-cultural studies are required before clinical adoption.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931129$q$, $q$This study evaluated educational satisfaction among pharmacists who graduated from Umeå University's online pharmacy programs between 2019 and 2023, including the potential impact of the COVID-19 pandemic. Satisfaction levels were compared with those reported by graduates from 2015-2018, as well as between educational and job satisfaction. Data were collected through alumni surveys. Overall educational satisfaction among the pharmacy graduates was high. However, satisfaction levels were significantly lower than in the 2015-2018 cohort, with a smaller proportion reporting being very satisfied with the education (82.1% vs. 96.0%, p = 0.011). No statistically significant differences in educational satisfaction were observed between graduation groups defined in relation to the COVID-19 pandemic. Most respondents reported that the knowledge and skills acquired during the education were useful in their current job (77.4%), that they felt well prepared for professional practice (74.4%), that they would recommend the program to a prospective student (82.9%), and that the education provided them with a clear professional identity (80.8%). No significant differences between cohorts were observed for these outcomes. Higher educational satisfaction was associated with greater job satisfaction. Assessing educational satisfaction may provide valuable insights for the ongoing development of pharmacy education and support graduates' preparedness for professional practice.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931171$q$, $q$Dengue remains the most important arboviral disease in the Americas and a growing global public health threat. The region experienced the largest dengue epidemic in recorded history during 2023-2024, with more than 13 million reported cases in 2024 alone, reflecting the combined effects of rapid urbanization, climate variability, population mobility, persistent social inequities, and weakened vector-control programs. This review summarizes and expands on the principal discussions of the First Dengue Summit of the Dengue Initiative of the Americas, held in Cartagena, Colombia, in September 2025. Major themes included the changing epidemiology of dengue; implementation and development of new-generation vaccines, including TAK-003 and Butantan-DV; advances in digital surveillance, outbreak forecasting, and clinical management; and integrated vector-control strategies. Particular attention is given to Wolbachia -mediated population replacement, supported by evidence from a cluster-randomized trial in Indonesia and large-scale quasi-experimental evaluations in Colombia and Brazil, which show substantial and sustained reductions in dengue incidence and hospitalization. In the Aburrá Valley, Colombia, city-wide deployment in Bello, Medellín, and Itagüí was associated with reductions exceeding 90% in notified dengue incidence where Wolbachia became established. The review also examines mathematical modeling, artificial intelligence, climate-informed surveillance, and integrated public health approaches. Persistent challenges include financial sustainability, healthcare inequities, climate change, insecticide resistance, and heterogeneous surveillance and health-system capacity. Coordinated, multisectoral, sustainable strategies that integrate vaccination, vector control, surveillance, community participation, and healthcare strengthening are essential to reduce the dengue burden and improve regional resilience.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931413$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: This study aims to determine university students' knowledge and attitudes in Cyprus regarding human papillomavirus (HPV) and the HPV vaccine. Methods: We conducted a cross-sectional study of 601 university students in Cyprus between March 2025 and June 2025 using quota-based convenience sampling. Undergraduate students in the faculties of Medicine, Nursing, Midwifery, and Engineering completed a detailed questionnaire assessing HPV and HPV vaccine knowledge and attitudes, as well as variables including gender, age, year of study, field of study, and self-reported racial/ethnic categories as operationalised in the survey, age at first sexual intercourse, sexual orientation, smoking, and history of number of sexual partners. The questionnaire assessed knowledge about HPV and the HPV vaccine, as well as attitudes and the self-reported reasons behind them. Results: Females and health-care students showed higher awareness of HPV and the HPV vaccine. Knowledge also increased with higher year of study and age. Among the variables examined, students aged over 24 had the highest vaccination rate. The most cited self-reported reasons for receiving the vaccine were to prevent cervical cancer and genital warts. Attitudes towards the HPV vaccine varied markedly across variables. Conclusions: HPV knowledge is directly related to preventive efforts, and young adults are key actors. Further research into attitudes towards the HPV vaccine and the reasons behind them may increase vaccination and decrease HPV-related cancers globally.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42931530$q$, $q$Microcystins (MCs) produced by cyanobacteria can contaminate aquatic foods, but drinking water surveillance does not directly characterize dietary exposure. This critical narrative and framework review evaluates environmental transfer, tissue residues, analytical coverage, toxicity, exposure assessment, and management for fish, bivalves, crustaceans, and related products, with a separate assessment of algal dietary supplements. Evidence is organized by species, consumed tissue, concentration basis, analytical fraction, congener coverage, and consumption scenario. Reported concentrations are not directly comparable when studies analyze different organs or use different extraction procedures and mass bases. Paired analyses of tilapia muscle illustrate how measurements limited to free congeners can miss a substantial additional burden, while shellfish studies show why clearance requires verification in the edible tissue. Structural and toxicological evidence explains why MC-LR provides a reference for assessment but does not fully characterize every mixture. We propose a framework linking environmental warnings to product sampling, validated quantification, explicit exposure scenarios, and proportionate management. Priorities are comparable occurrence reporting, interlaboratory validation, characterization of the oral relevance of bound fractions, and congener-specific toxicity data. Neither a positive screening result nor an exceedance of a chronic intake benchmark alone establishes the probability of illness from an individual meal.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932014$q$, $q$Pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) are an increasing class of environmental pollutants of concern in aquatic ecosystems owing to their persistence and biological activity at low concentrations. Correct and selective monitoring of these substances is necessary to assess their ecological impacts and preserve water quality. The high conductivity, large surface area and tunable surface chemistry of two-dimensional transition metal carbides and nitrides (MXenes) have recently opened new avenues for electrochemical sensing. In this study we explain the state of the art of MXene nanoparticles, their characteristics, production and functionalization procedures that allow the integration of these materials in electrochemical and bioinspired sensing systems. Design advances such as biomimetic recognition elements, molecular imprinting, enzymes and nanocomposites are critically reviewed in terms of their contribution towards improving selectivity and operational stability of sensors. This study examines the analytical performance of MXene-based sensors for the ultra-trace detection of PPCPs in complex aquatic matrices, focusing on advances in sensitivity, antifouling strategies, and multiplexed detection. It also discusses future perspectives such as smart sensor networks and AI-driven data analytics, as well as issues in material scalability, sensor durability and real-world deployment. This study opens the door for the new potential of MXene-enabled platforms for the development of environmental analytical chemistry and shows the future research pathways towards sustainable and effective aquatic pollution monitoring.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932264$q$, $q$Municipalities increasingly pilot gamified physical activity (PA) interventions, yet few progress beyond pilots. This qualitative study examined decision-making pathways through which Dutch municipalities adopt, implement, and scale digital gamified PA interventions, using the case of 'Missie Master'. A two-phase design was applied, combining exploratory interviews with policymakers, health professionals, and a company (phase 1), followed by two multi-stakeholder workshops and follow-up interviews with municipalities new to gamification (phase 2). Thematic analysis showed that adoption was driven by informal processes, enthusiastic individuals, trust in product reliability, and peer recommendations, while generic company outreach was ignored. Implementation relied on early preparatory capacity, such as designated coordinators, marketing plans, and timely app updates. Scaling was rare and cyclical, dependent on political support, cross-domain alignment, and short communication lines. The study offers a practical decision guide and highlights monitoring needs. Policy implications include transparent evaluation pathways, structured early implementation, and strengthened public-private co-development.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932280$q$, $q$This study examined the effects of elevation gradients on on soil and plant stoichiometry and heavy metals distribution in Mixed Coniferous Forests (MCF) and Oak Forests (OF). Soil organic carbon (SOC), total nitrogen (TN), and total phosphorus (TP) were significantly higher at the 10 cm soil depth at elevation EL-2, followed by EL-3, in both forests; whereas the lowest values were observed at EL-1. MCF exhibited significantly higher SOC, TN, and TP concentrations than OF. Similarly, leaf carbon, nitrogen, and phosphorus concentrations were highest EL-2. In oak forests, leaf carbon, nitrogen, and phosphorus concentrations reached 458.4 mg kg - ¹, 4.42 mg kg - ¹, and 2.54 mg kg - ¹, respectively. The Corresponding values in MCF were 472 mg kg - ¹, 16.1 mg kg - ¹, and 1.9 mg kg - ¹, respectively, indicating greater nutrient accumulation at the mid-elevation site (EL-2). The leaf C:N ratio was significantly higher at EL-2, whereas the C:P and N:P ratios did not differ significantly among elevations. A similar pattern was observed in branches, bark, and litter. At EL-2 site, soil nickel (Ni), lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), copper (Cu), and iron (Fe) concentrations at the 10 cm soil depth were significantly lower in MCF than in OF. Conversely, plant tissues in MCF exhibited higher concentrations of Ni, Pb, zinc (Zn), Cd, Cu, and Fe than those in OF at EL-1. Soil and plant stoichiometry are fundamental components of ecosystem functioning and nutrient cycling, and elevation gradients also play a critical role in regulating these processes. These findings offer valuable insights for developing sustainable forest management strategies that aim to mitigate heavy metal pollution in high-altitude ecosystems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932301$q$, $q$Long-term disposal and management of nuclear waste is one of the major hurdles for nuclear energy. Deep geological disposal, an idea originating in the 1970s, is currently the preferred path being followed in many countries. However, acceptance of this approach is hindered by societal concerns around the safety of the "one-million years" geological disposal and passing the waste burden to future generations. iMAGINE, an integrated nuclear system based on molten salt fast reactor technology with self-sustained iso-breeding, online clean-up and reverse reprocessing, has been proposed as an innovative way to eliminate the demand for a long-term high-level waste disposal. In this work, we investigate the use of iMAGINE as a technological approach for reimagining high-level nuclear waste management by overcoming the challenges of classical partitioning and transmutation (P&T). After discussing the current status and limitations of P&T along with an overview of the nuclear waste classification and management framework existing in major nuclear power producing countries, we demonstrate the potential impact of iMAGINE in simplifying the disposal of high-level waste. The results indicate that among the key long-lived radionuclides, many lie well below the criteria for requiring deep geological disposal. Besides Finland, India and Russia where most (or all) of the important long-lived fission products exceed current high-level waste thresholds, only two to three fission products (generally with half-lives from 30 to 100 years) exceed the national limits in Canada, China, France, Germany, Japan, and South Korea, while concrete conclusions can't be drawn for the UK and US based on available information. The results presented here do not aim to make inflated claims but should be seen as the scientific basis for deeper discussions amongst all nuclear waste disposal stakeholders to explore a novel technological solution for rethinking high-level waste management and possibly eliminating the need for a "one-million years" geological disposal.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932364$q$, $q$Reliably capturing transient animal behavior in the field and laboratory remains a logistical and financial challenge, especially for small ectotherms. Here, we evaluate home security cameras as affordable, accessible and suitable alternatives for monitoring small, cold-blooded animals historically overlooked by commercial camera traps. These cameras are often lightweight, weather-resistant, operate offline or online, store data continuously for up to four weeks and can use phone notifications over local Wi-Fi to alert users to behaviorally relevant events. We first benchmark a representative home security camera against an established trail camera under matched laboratory conditions in both continuous and motion-triggered modes, comparing event detection, recording duration, and missed-event rates. We then validate the system during a 1.5-month deployment in a tropical primary rainforest, assessing weather durability, sensitivity-versus-data-volume trade-offs, and whether behavioral measurements obtained in the laboratory are reproducible under natural conditions. We discuss the relative affordability of our system for researchers in the Global South, home to the largest ectotherm diversity.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932367$q$, $q$Olive mill wastewater (OMW) is a major environmental concern in olive-producing regions because of its high organic load and phytotoxic phenolic content, which limit its safe discharge or reuse. This study aimed to develop and evaluate an integrated electrocoagulation-adsorption (ECAD) treatment using olive pomace (OP) biochar and to determine how electrode material influences the performance and cost of the combined process. Specifically, the study characterized OP biochar produced by pyrolysis and evaluated its adsorption behavior toward total phenols (TPh) and soluble chemical oxygen demand (CODsoluble), compared Al- and Fe-based EC and their integrated ECAD performance, and theoretically determined biochar dosages and estimated the operational cost of both configurations. The produced biochar exhibited a BET surface area of 135.23 m2/g, a mesoporous structure with an average pore width of 21.95 Å, a negative zeta potential of -29.89 mV, and a carbon content of 67.21%, confirming the formation of a carbon-rich and porous adsorbent. Isotherm analysis showed that TPh adsorption was best described by the Langmuir model, with a maximum adsorption capacity of 84.74 mg/g, whereas CODsoluble was best fitted by the Dubinin-Radushkevich (D-R) model. Standalone EC achieved comparable TPh and CODsoluble removal using Al and Fe electrodes (about 52-55%). However, the integrated systems showed a strong electrode-dependent response. The Al-ECAD system achieved maximum removals of 70.69% TPh and 71.39% CODsoluble, while the Fe-ECAD system reached 67.40% and 66.33%, respectively, at the lowest biochar dosage, with removal declining at higher dosages. The estimated operational costs were 4.25 and 5.30 US$/m3 for Al- and Fe-based systems, respectively. The novelty of this work lies in demonstrating that the effectiveness of biochar-assisted EC is strongly dependent on electrode-biochar compatibility, identifying electrode selection as a key design factor for ECAD treatment of OMW.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932371$q$, $q$Purpose: To evaluate patient satisfaction and preferences for portable versus table-mounted visual field (VF) devices in a rural telemedicine setting and identify influencing factors. Methods: We conducted a sequential explanatory mixed methods study at three Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs) within the Alabama Screening and Intervention for Glaucoma and eye Health through Telemedicine (AL-SIGHT) study. Participants completed VF testing with table-mounted Humphrey Field Analyzer (HFA), tablet-based Melbourne Rapid Fields (MRF), and virtual reality (VR)-based VisuALL perimeters. Participants rated satisfaction, comfort, ease of use, and future testing preference. Chi-square tests assessed differences in device preferences. Twelve participants completed semi-structured interviews to explore reasons underlying preferences. Qualitative data were analyzed in NVivo 14 using reflexive thematic analysis. Results: Among 271 respondents (mean age 60.4 years; 62.4% women), 50.6% preferred VR-based, 35.1% tablet-based, and 14.4% table-mounted for future testing (χ2 (2) = 53.52, p 0.05). Qualitative themes identified four key drivers: comfort and physical experience, visual experience, ease of use and interaction, and psychological and motivational factors. Portability and community suitability were valued. Conclusion: Rural underserved patients strongly preferred portable visual field devices, particularly VR-based, over table-mounted HFA. Comfort, ergonomic flexibility, immersive visual experience, and simplicity of interaction were central determinants of preference. Portable perimetry may enhance patient-centered glaucoma monitoring within telemedicine programs and access in resource-limited settings.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42932440$q$, $q$Background: Although the Dominican Republic has achieved considerable progress in under-five mortality (U5M) over the past decades, there still exists considerable socioeconomic and geographic inequality. Children from poorer households and disadvantaged communities continue to face disproportionately higher mortality risks. Nevertheless, research on the multilevel impact of individual- and community-level determinants on child survival in the country remains limited. We aimed to quantify the associations between individual- and community-level socioeconomic and demographic determinants and under-five mortality in the Dominican Republic, using a multilevel analytic approach applied to nationally representative survey data. Methods: We conducted a cross-sectional multilevel analysis using nationally representative birth history data from the 2013 Dominican Republic Demographic and Health Survey. We included approximately 13,100 children nested within 453 community clusters. We used multilevel logistic regression models with random effects to examine the relationship between socioeconomic, demographic, and community-based factors and U5M. We used the Akaike Information Criterion, Bayesian information criterion, and likelihood ratio tests to assess model fit. We quantified community-level variation using clustering measures such as the intraclass correlation coefficient and median odds ratio (OR). Results: Significant socioeconomic gradients in U5M were observed. Children in the poorest wealth quintile had nearly twice the odds of mortality compared to those in the richest quintile (adjusted OR (aOR) = 1.98; 95% confidence interval (CI) = 1.44-2.71). Maternal education was strongly associated with child survival, with children of mothers with no education having higher odds of mortality (aOR = 2.14; 95% CI = 1.58-2.89) compared to those with tertiary education. Unimproved sanitation (aOR = 1.38; 95% CI = 1.16-1.64), unimproved water sources (aOR = 1.27; 95% CI = 1.05-1.54), short birth intervals (<24 months) (aOR = 1.39; 95% CI = 1.18-1.64), and fewer than four antenatal care visits (aOR = 1.61; 95% CI = 1.32-1.96) were significant predictors. At the community level, rural residence (aOR = 1.51; 95% CI = 1.19-1.92), higher regional poverty (aOR = 1.43; 95% CI = 1.09-1.87), and lower healthcare access (aOR = 1.38; 95% CI = 1.05-1.82) were associated with increased mortality risk. Community-level variance decreased from 12.9% in the null model to 4.2% in the fully adjusted model, indicating meaningful contextual effects. Conclusions: Both individual socioeconomic conditions and broader community-level factors shape U5M in the Dominican Republic. Persistent inequalities in wealth, maternal education, and access to basic services highlight the need for integrated policy approaches targeting both household and structural determinants. Strengthening maternal education, expanding access to water and sanitation, and improving rural healthcare infrastructure are critical for reducing child mortality and advancing progress toward Sustainable Development Goal 3.2.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42934301$q$, $q$Differences in visual perception mechanisms between biological and artificial intelligence make it difficult to design camouflage that is both visually natural and effective against modern detectors. Existing adversarial approaches often create conspicuous patterns and are typically static, limiting their use in dynamic real-world scenarios. Here we present CephCam, an embodied closed-loop camouflage system that integrates environment-aware color extraction, optimization-based texture generation that jointly balances visual naturalness and detector evasion, and angle-aware display compensation for physical deployment. This system involves an eye-brain-skin framework of sensing, computation, and display, motivated by the naturally evolved cephalopod camouflage, but implemented with specialized cameras, computing modules, and OLED screens on a model vehicle. CephCam targets simultaneous evasion of both human observers and modern object detectors across diverse digital and physical environments, significantly outperforming strong baselines targeting either alone. These findings underscore shared vulnerabilities in human and artificial visual processing systems, showcasing CephCam's potential for robust, adaptive, and embodied camouflage applications.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42934874$q$, $q$Greenhouse gas emissions from temporarily flooded upland croplands remain poorly characterized in the global greenhouse gas budget. We combined field measurements from 11 maize-field sites temporarily flooded across the North China Plain with a global synthesis of 23 flooded upland cropland sites to quantify diffusive emissions. Flooded croplands release high fluxes of nitrous oxide, carbon dioxide, and methane, with emission factors exceeding relevant Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) defaults for rice paddies and newly flooded lands. Extrapolating site-level fluxes from limited observations over the estimated global area of flooded upland cropland and mean flooding duration yields a mean of 63.3 teragrams (Tg) of carbon dioxide-equivalent (CO 2 -eq) emissions per year during the standing-water flooding periods, comprising 7.2 (1.7-17.2), 34.6 (9.0-83.7), and 21.5 (6.2-45.6) Tg from nitrous oxide, carbon dioxide, and methane, respectively. Temporarily flooded upland croplands are therefore identified as a previously overlooked component of the global greenhouse gas budget, providing an empirical basis for incorporating flood-driven emissions into future agricultural inventories.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42935137$q$, $q$The rapidly evolving use of artificial intelligence (AI)-powered intelligent transportation systems (ITS) and other emerging technologies in the transportation sector has the potential to deliver unprecedented improvements in safety and efficiency. However, recent research has also raised concerns about the implications of using AI and automation, as these technologies can introduce new, understudied risks to such systems. This perspective article evaluates AI-powered ITS through three lenses: risk assessment, concerning whether current conceptual and methodological tools remain sufficient for these systems; the human element, relating to how analysts and operators interact with AI outputs, including explainable AI (XAI) and human-in-the-loop oversight; and governance, concerning frameworks needed to manage these systems. Across these lenses, the article uses risk characterization as a diagnostic tool for locating where established risk practice must adapt and translates the resulting gaps into specific research directions. Although the practical interpretation and application of fundamental risk concepts may change when viewed through the lens of AI-powered ITS, the work of this article provides a basis for research directions and requirements needed for risk practice to adapt to new and emerging technologies and to support future research and training within the risk analysis community. This perspective will be of interest to risk researchers, practitioners, and policymakers.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42935564$q$, $q$Introduction: Meteorological variability influences diamondback moth (DBM; Plutella xylostella ) infestation and the resulting damage to Brassica crops. Accurate multi-horizon forecasting requires models that preserve short-lived meteorological exposures while integrating thermal timing, spatial dependence, and recent infestation dynamics within a transparent framework. Methods: The Temporal-Geospatial Pest Network (TG-PestNet) forecasts the weekly percentage of inspected plants classified as DBM-infested 1 to 4 weeks ahead at four Guangdong stations, from origin-time information only. It preserves hourly exposure, encodes lookback weeks on a thermal-time axis, couples stations through a wind-informed graph, and protects infestation history in a gated anchor. Evaluation used 344 synchronized weeks, 66 later test origins, 13 history-only comparators, retraining ablations, matched-width controls, and input-matched generic encoders. Results and discussion: TG-PestNet achieved the lowest observed root-mean-square error, mean absolute error, and mean absolute percentage error in all 12 station-metric combinations, with mean reductions of 5.9%-13.0%. Year-ahead forecasts outperformed persistence in all four test years. Removing the history anchor increased error by 14.3% and remained significant after multiplicity correction, demonstrating its central contribution to forecast accuracy. Meteorological information provided complementary predictive value: its occlusion increased the error by 9.3%, and retraining without it increased the macro RMSE by 3.6%. TG-PestNet also substantially outperformed the two input-matched generic encoders, whose errors were approximately one quarter higher. These results establish TG-PestNet as an accurate, reproducible and auditable framework for multi-station pest forecasting, with component-level analyses that identify the dominant contribution of recent infestation history and characterize the predictive roles of high-resolution meteorology and structured spatial information.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42935598$q$, $q$This study examined students' geometric reasoning, geometric problem-solving errors, and the association between demonstrated Van Hiele reasoning levels and Newman error categories. A quantitative approach employing a cross-sectional descriptive-correlational design was adopted. The study involved 300 final-year students from a selected senior high school, who were selected through proportionate stratified random sampling. Data were collected using an adapted 20-item Geometry Test developed by Usiskin (1982) and a 12-item researcher-designed Geometry Problem-Solving Test aligned with the Ghanaian core mathematics curriculum. Descriptive statistics and category-specific binary logistic Generalized Estimating Equations (GEE) models were used for data analysis. Most students demonstrated lower levels of geometric reasoning, with Level 0 (visualization) and Level 1 (analysis) accounting for the largest proportions, while relatively few demonstrated Levels 2 (informal deduction) and 3 (deduction). Transformation errors were the most frequent, followed by comprehension errors, whereas reading errors were least frequent among the erroneous responses. The GEE analysis showed significant associations between demonstrated Van Hiele reasoning levels and comprehension, transformation, processing skill, and encoding errors, but not reading errors. The findings indicate that error patterns varied across demonstrated reasoning levels, highlighting the value of examining geometric problem-solving errors in relation to both the level of reasoning explicitly demonstrated and the stage at which difficulty was manifested. The study contributes an initial empirical examination of the proposed GRaPSE framework, which integrates these two dimensions for analyzing geometric problem-solving responses.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42935730$q$, $q$Food rescue organizations act as hubs for food services, redistributing surplus food from their operations to recipients in need of food assistance to prevent food waste and reduce hunger. Ensuring the food is nutritious is the priority; however, this aspect is sometimes neglected. This study aimed to estimate the nutritional content and food group of redistributed surplus food against dietary recommendations in Indonesia. Data on the quantities, menus, and types of surplus food managed by the Food Rescue Organization were collected from July 2024 to October 2025 in Yogyakarta, Indonesia. We estimated nutritional content by calculating macronutrients using the Indonesian Food Composition Table (TKPI) database. Among all the redistributed food ( n = 156 donation meals), the amounts of energy, total fat, and carbohydrate in all population groups did not meet 30% of the recommended dietary allowance (RDA) per meal, partly reflecting that some meals consisted of a single donated dish rather than a full combination of foods. Significant differences across donors were observed for protein ( p p p < 0.05); however, the variance in the number of recorded meals across donors should be considered. Although the redistributed surplus food comes from various food groups, the dominant sources are grains and cereals and animal-based protein. At the same time, high-fiber food groups such as fruits, plant-based proteins, and beans are in short supply. This study highlights the importance of improving nutrition in food rescue organizations' operations.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42935788$q$, $q$The digital transformation of society creates new opportunities for the involvement of older population in physical fitness and sports activities, however, the nature and the power of the impact exerted by various aspects of digitalisation on the physical activity level remain understudied. The goal of the study is to identify digital factors and to assess their impact on the physical activity of older population. The study was conducted using a sample of 27 European Union countries for the period 2015-2024 with the use of physical activity indicators of population aged 60-74 years, the level of internet usage by this age group, digital skills of population, the e-Governance development index, and gross domestic product per capita. Correlation analysis was used to identify relationship between variables under study. Statistical methods were selected taking account of the results of data distribution normality testing with the use of the Shapiro-Wilk criterion. The Granger causality test was used to measure the direction of identified relationship. Estimates were made using the Stata software. The findings of the study confirmed the existence of statistically significant positive correlation between digitalisation indicators and the level of physical activity of older population. The strongest relationship was discovered between the digital skills of population and the level of physical activity of people aged 60-74 years ( r = 0.781; p r = 0.712; p r = 0.637; p < 0.01). The Granger causality test's results showed that digital skills precede changes in the level of physical activity in 18 European Union countries, while the internet usage in 16 countries, and the e-Governance development in 13 countries. The findings suggest that the digitalisation can be regarded as one of factors for the development of physical activity of older population. The practical significance of the study consists in the opportunity to use the findings in the development of active longevity programs, digital inclusion of older citizens, and digital transformation projects in the field of physical fitness and sports.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936016$q$, $q$Background: The teaching of anatomy is often perceived as one of the most challenging components of medical education. Augmented reality (AR) has emerged as a transformative educational tool. These applications superimpose digital information onto the physical world, offering students interactive, immersive experiences for understanding complex anatomical structures. This research investigates medical students' perceptions and self-reported use of AR applications on smartphones to assess medical students' familiarity with AR technology, engagement levels, usability challenges, and preferences between AR and traditional learning methods. Methods: A descriptive cross-sectional study and a structured questionnaire were employed to collect data from medical students at Fatima Memorial College of Medicine & Dentistry (FMHCM&D), Lahore, Pakistan. Data analysis was performed using SPSS 27.00, employing descriptive statistics and chi-square tests. Kruskal-Wallis (with Shapiro-Wilk and Dunn's post hoc, Bonferroni) was used to examine associations between the variables. Results: This study surveyed 496 medical students from FMHCM&D, Lahore, Pakistan. The mean age of participants was 21.33 ± 1.66 years. 32% were males and 68% were females. One hundred and forty-two of 638 consenting students (22.3%) had no prior AR app use and were removed before data entry. Whereas 77.7% (496/638) reported prior use of AR apps. Only 70% of students agreed about the frequent use of AR apps. 31% did not experience general usability difficulties (e.g., navigation problems), but 46.8% reported performance issues (e.g., app crashes, slow running, or battery drain) while using AR applications. These apps were perceived by 76.4% of students as helpful for securing good marks in anatomy, and 70% agreed that AR apps reduced their reliance on traditional learning methods. Furthermore, 81.7% of students agreed that AR enhanced their enjoyment of learning anatomy. Conclusion: The findings of this study suggest that medical students perceive AR as a valuable tool that enhances their engagement and enjoyment, highlighting the need for controlled, outcome-based research to establish AR's actual impact on teaching methodology.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936043$q$, $q$Quality assurance (QA) in pharmacy education concerns both educational standards and the competence of graduates. This structured narrative review examines entry-to-practice pharmacy education in the eight East African Community (EAC) Partner States. The initial search focused on PubMed-indexed literature and official sources. During revision, the search was expanded to Scopus, PubMed, Google Scholar, and English- and French-language institutional sources, with final retrieval on October 4, 2026. Publications from 2015 onward were prioritized, with relevant earlier educational studies, legislation, and foundational frameworks also included. Selection focused on accreditation, competencies, curriculum, educational capacity, assessment, graduate outcomes, and continuous improvement. Evidence availability differs among Partner States. Institutional studies document curriculum reform, clinical training, assessment, and short-term learning outcomes, but evidence on graduate competence and the effects of QA systems remains limited. Shared core competencies, pharmacy-specific benchmarks, outcome monitoring, and documented improvement cycles are proposed within existing national and regional arrangements. The proposed framework is a conceptual synthesis requiring stakeholder validation and empirical pilot testing before operational use.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936092$q$, $q$Plant-derived polyphenols possess broad anti-inflammatory activities and may benefit chronic inflammatory diseases. XuanBi is a traditional Chinese medicine formula containing orally absorbable polyphenolic constituents and is used to treat rheumatoid arthritis (RA). This study investigated its absorbable components and potential mechanisms associated with synovial inflammatory hyperplasia. XuanBi was evaluated in collagen-induced arthritis (CIA) rats and rheumatoid arthritis synovial fibroblasts (RASFs). Constituents in the decoction and rat serum were characterized by high-performance liquid chromatography coupled with quadrupole-Orbitrap mass spectrometry (HPLC-Q-Orbitrap-MS). Predicted targets were intersected with synovial differentially expressed proteins (DEPs), followed by network pharmacology, Gene Ontology (GO), Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) enrichment analyses, and experimental validation. Twenty-six absorbable constituents were identified, including catechin, quercetin, baicalein, and wogonin, together with 14 candidate proteins associated with RA synovial pathology. Enrichment analysis implicated extracellular matrix (ECM)-receptor interaction, focal adhesion, and interleukin-17 (IL-17) signaling. Medium- and high-dose XuanBi reduced joint swelling, synovial hyperplasia, and inflammatory infiltration in CIA rats. In RASFs, XuanBi inhibited proliferation, migration, and invasion. It also downregulated fibronectin 1 (FN1) and caveolin 1 (CAV1), reduced phosphorylation of SRC, focal adhesion kinase (FAK), and FYN, decreased integrin alpha 2b (ITGA2B) + /integrin beta 1 (ITGB1) + cells, and reduced interleukin-1 beta (IL-1β), interleukin-6 (IL-6), tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α), matrix metallopeptidase 3 (MMP3), and matrix metallopeptidase 9 (MMP9). Overall, XuanBi exerted anti-inflammatory and anti-synovial-hyperplasia effects in experimental RA, accompanied by changes in proteins associated with these pathways, supporting the potential involvement of these pathways and warranting further mechanistic and clinical investigation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936235$q$, $q$Countries with limited water and land resources confront a significant problem due to the growing demand for medicinal plants. These plants are used worldwide to extract and isolate therapeutically important bioactive molecules, known as secondary metabolites. Many medicinal plant species are currently facing significant conservation challenges due to excessive, unregulated harvesting, driven by rapidly growing demand for plant-derived therapies. Soilless farming techniques like hydroponics, aeroponics, and vertical farming offer a sustainable alternative for generating high-quality planting material on a large scale in a considerably shorter period and with less space. Soilless farming revolutionises agriculture and food production, making it particularly well-suited for urban agriculture by reducing maintenance requirements and maximizing crop yields. Soilless farming of numerous medicinal herbs, fruit crops, and vegetables has become increasingly popular in recent years, particularly for its ability to produce large amounts of biomass, high yields, and a wide range of pharmacologically active metabolites. These high-tech systems have significant promise for increased crop yields, cleaner produce, season-independent cultivation, and reduced environmental impact, particularly in water use and disease, compared to traditional soil-based systems. This review article rigorously evaluates recent advancements in soilless agriculture, including LED spectral regulation, nano-elicitation, microbial biostimulants and precursor-feeding strategies, reusable substrates, computational technologies, and CRISPR/Cas-based editing, with respect to their efficacy in generating high-quality plant biomass, improving automation, and optimizing shoot architecture. Implementing soilless farming to produce and synthesize bioactive molecules is expected to be crucial in enhancing sustainability and human well-being.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936290$q$, $q$Textile dye-infused industrial effluents are toxic and recalcitrant and impart a negative environmental impact. The aim of this study is to develop and characterize a ZnO-coated coconut shell biochar-filled polylactic acid (PLA) ternary composite membrane for the effective removal of methylene blue (MB) from aqueous solutions. The coconut shell biochar was prepared by oxygen-limited pyrolysis in a muffle furnace at 500 °C and further functionalized with ZnO using the wet impregnation method. The ZnO-coated biochar was introduced into the PLA matrix at different feedings (0.25 g, 0.5 g and 0.75 g). Characterisation studies, such as field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, zeta potential measurement, water contact angle and porosity measurements were performed to confirm the successful incorporation of ZnO and to assess the changes in surface properties. The 0.5 g filled membrane, which had the highest pure water flux (PWF) (565 LMH), exhibited a maximum MB rejection of 93% at an initial concentration of 5 mg L -1 and a flux recovery ratio of up to 79.5%. This study highlights the use of agro-waste-derived composite membranes as a potential sustainable alternative for textile dye remediation applications.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936344$q$, $q$Plagiarism is one of the most persistent ethical problems in academic and scientific publishing. Once limited to copying sentences from books, it has grown into a far more complicated challenge, driven by the internet, intense research competition, and the rapid spread of generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools. This article provides a thorough overview of plagiarism - definition, historical roots, types, contributing factors, detection tools, and regulatory frameworks with particular focus on recent developments through 2025/2026. The article pays special attention to India's regulatory approach under the University Grants Commission 2018 Regulations, the global role of the Committee on Publication Ethics, and the newly published Generative AI Transparency 2024 guidelines. The central argument is that addressing plagiarism today demands a combination of good ethical education, clear institutional policies, honest AI disclosure, and a genuine commitment to original thinking.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936410$q$, $q$Active tuberculosis remains a major global health problem, and distinguishing active from non-active disease on chest radiographs could accelerate triage where radiology resources are limited. We developed ShuffleNetV2 using 4,613 radiographs from two centers and evaluated it in retrospective external (1,318 patients) and prospective (560 patients) cohorts. The model achieved AUCs of 0.917 and 0.896, respectively, and performance was stable across prespecified demographic and scanner subgroups. In the prospective cohort, artificial-intelligence assistance increased radiologist AUCs to 0.897-0.937 across experience levels. These findings support ShuffleNetV2 as a rapid decision-support tool for prioritizing confirmatory clinical and microbiological assessment; positive predictions should not be used alone to initiate treatment.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936720$q$, $q$Foodborne disease remains a major global health and economic burden, with recent World Health Organization estimates attributing roughly 866 million illnesses and 1.52 million deaths annually to 42 major foodborne hazards. Conventional preservation and decontamination strategies are increasingly challenged by antimicrobial-resistant pathogens, chemical residues, and mycotoxin contamination, creating demand for complementary nanotechnology-based interventions. Zinc oxide (ZnO) nanoparticles and their heterojunctions with graphitic carbon nitride (g-C 3 N 4 ) have emerged as versatile platforms combining broad-spectrum antimicrobial activity with visible-light-driven photocatalytic detoxification. This review synthesizes recent literature on the synthesis of ZnO nanoparticles by conventional and green/biogenic routes, including microbially mediated synthesis using actinobacteria such as Streptomyces species, and on the design of g-C 3 N 4 /ZnO S-scheme heterojunctions that suppress charge-carrier recombination while preserving strong redox potentials. We examine the principal antimicrobial and photocatalytic mechanisms-reactive oxygen species generation, Zn 2+ ion release, and direct membrane interaction-and their translation into food-relevant applications, including antimicrobial active packaging films and coatings, photocatalytic degradation of mycotoxins and pesticide residues, and nanomaterial-based biosensors for pathogen and spoilage detection. Toxicological and regulatory considerations, including nanoparticle migration from food-contact materials and evolving European Food Safety Authority and United States Food and Drug Administration frameworks, are discussed alongside persistent gaps in standardized safety testing, scale-up reproducibility, and life-cycle assessment. We conclude by outlining research priorities-particularly the systematic evaluation of biogenic ZnO and g-C 3 N 4 /ZnO composites under real food matrices and regulatory-relevant conditions-needed to advance these materials from laboratory demonstration toward safe, standardized, and sustainable adoption across the food value chain.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936721$q$, $q$Electrochromic technology, which enables dynamic modulation of optical properties in smart materials, holds significant promise for applications such as energy-efficient buildings and intelligent displays. In recent years, a novel electrochromic system based on the electrodeposition and dissolution of silver ions (Ag + ) has attracted considerable attention owing to its unique optical modulation capabilities and excellent electrochemical reversibility. By controlling the deposition morphology of Ag on various electrode surfaces to form uniform films, nanoparticles, or dendritic structures, this system achieves reversible switching among transparent, black, mirror-like, and even multicolor states. It offers advantages such as high optical contrast, fast response times, and good cycling stability. This review provides a systematic overview of Ag-based electrodeposition electrochromic systems, covering their fundamental principles, key performance metrics, critical materials (electrolytes and electrodes), device architectures, and application progress in smart windows. Future research directions and development prospects are also discussed.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42936777$q$, $q$Modern biomedical research depends on an extensive ecosystem of shared infrastructure that enables investigators to access specialized technologies, expertise, data resources, and biospecimens. Less attention has been given to the operational infrastructure that provides reliable access to the reagents and consumables required for everyday experimental work. Although traditionally viewed as an administrative or procurement function, research supply infrastructure directly influences the cost, timeliness, continuity, and sustainability of scientific activity. This article examines centralized research supply as a form of shared scientific infrastructure through the experience of the Genetic Resources Core Facility at the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine. What began as a small reagent-access program evolved into an institution-wide research supply infrastructure serving more than 5000 researchers and 846 principal investigators. In 2025, the program provided access to approximately 664,000 products and distributed more than 106,000 laboratory reagents while consolidating purchasing and distribution through a coordinated institutional framework. Over an eleven-year evaluation period, the model also generated substantial estimated institutional economic value through negotiated pricing, reduced shipping costs, and consolidation of purchasing activity. The experience demonstrates that the value of centralized research supply extends beyond procurement efficiency. Integration within a shared-resource environment can improve access to research materials, reduce administrative and logistical barriers, support continuity of experimental workflows, and connect purchasing decisions with scientific expertise and evolving research needs. These findings suggest that research supply can function as shared scientific infrastructure when organized around scientific access, integrated with the research ecosystem, and responsive to evolving investigator needs. Recognizing and strategically integrating this infrastructure within institutional research ecosystems provides an opportunity to strengthen scientific productivity, resilience, and long-term sustainability.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938225$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: Organised colorectal cancer screening is still being introduced in Romania, and the yield of opportunistic colonoscopy outside it is poorly documented. Our primary aim was to describe the histopathological spectrum and the yield of advanced neoplasia in an opportunistic colonoscopy service; a secondary, exploratory aim was to describe self-reported lifestyle items in a questionnaire subgroup. Methods: This study represents a retrospective cross-sectional analysis of consecutive colonoscopies (June 2023-June 2024) at a county hospital outside organised screening, restricted to patients from whom material reached histopathology; the comparison group therefore comprises patients whose sampled lesions were benign, not patients without neoplasia. Advanced neoplasia was defined histologically as adenocarcinoma or adenoma with high-grade dysplasia, without the 10 mm size criterion. A convenience subgroup self-completed a locally developed, unvalidated questionnaire before the procedure; its items were tested against advanced neoplasia and adenocarcinoma with false discovery rate correction. Results: Among 274 patients, 45 (16.4%) had adenocarcinoma, 139 (50.7%) adenoma and 72 (26.3%) advanced neoplasia; per examination ( n = 978), detection was 4.6% for adenocarcinoma and 7.4% for advanced neoplasia. Forty-four of 45 carcinomas were graded as moderately differentiated, and no serrated lesion was separately reported. Age was associated with advanced neoplasia (OR 1.34 per decade, 95% CI 1.01-1.79). Questionnaire data were available for only 79 patients (28.8%), among whom adenocarcinoma was more frequent than among non-respondents (26.6% vs. 12.3%). In this subgroup, obesity was inversely (OR 0.07, 95% CI 0.02-0.28) and reported weight loss positively (OR 6.77, 2.26-20.30) associated with advanced neoplasia, and two coffee items were inversely associated; processed meat, dairy, aspirin and supplement use were not associated after correction. Conclusions: Opportunistic colonoscopy at this centre recovered a high proportion of carcinoma, consistent with symptom-driven presentation, and the reporting of serrated lesions and tumour grade requires standardisation. The questionnaire findings rest on a small, selected subgroup and are hypothesis-generating only. The inverse obesity association is most plausibly explained by selection of the comparison group and of respondents, and none of the associations identifies a risk factor.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938464$q$, $q$Competition remains common but under-theorized in early childhood education (ECE), and its developmental implications are fragmented across distinct research traditions. This systematic review synthesized 56 peer-reviewed empirical studies published between 2000 and 2025 and identified through PsycINFO, Scopus, Web of Science, ERIC, PubMed, and citation searching. Eligible studies examined competitive experiences in children from birth through age 8 across socio-emotional, socio-cognitive, and socio-moral domains. Methodological quality was appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT), and findings were integrated through a typological narrative synthesis followed by an ecological interpretation. Across heterogeneous experimental, correlational, longitudinal, observational, and qualitative evidence, competition was neither uniformly beneficial nor harmful. Reported developmental findings varied with children's capacities and relational positioning, the demands and social architecture of competitive activities, and the sociocultural meanings through which competition was interpreted and socialized. Methodological concerns included limited representativeness, variable control of confounding, and sparse direct examination of teacher mediation and cross-cultural mechanisms. Drawing on this synthesis, the review proposes the Safe Zone Framework-Assess, Adapt, and Support-as a preliminary evidence-informed conceptual framework for considering how competitive experiences may be understood, structured, and supported in ECE. The framework re-quires empirical validation before it can be considered a classroom protocol.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938549$q$, $q$Sudden natural disasters pose growing challenges to emergency governance. Understanding public response behaviors is critical to effective risk communication. Social media comments provide valuable data for examining such behaviors during disasters. To investigate the characteristics and influencing factors of public response behaviors, this study used Typhoon Ragasa in 2025 as a case and analyzed 8365 valid comment samples from the Douyin platform. Qualitative coding and text analysis revealed public response behaviors varied across disaster phases. Information behavior predominated during the warning phase, as people relied on information to assess risks and adjust daily routines. Emotional behavior increased during the impact phase, with regional empathy and cross-verification of information. During the recovery phase, responses shifted toward restorative emotional expression and reflection on disaster governance. Action and supportive behaviors remained relatively infrequent. Logistic regression results further showed that the cognitive risk perception index and reply-based conversational engagement were positively associated with information behavior and negatively associated with emotional behavior. Neither was significantly associated with action or supportive behavior. Like-based social endorsement was positively associated with information behavior during the warning phase and emotional behavior during the impact phase. Based on these findings, several recommendations are proposed.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938589$q$, $q$Family-school partnership is often discussed in relation to student support, yet adult interviews primarily illuminate how educators interpret family knowledge and organize professional action. This interpretivist qualitative study examined how 13 educators in Arab education in Israel constructed partnership when interpreting students' learning, emotional, social, or behavioral difficulties. The educator interviews were analyzed using topic-focused reflexive thematic analysis. Four unmatched mother interviews, collected to explore school communication, transparency, and decision inclusion, received a separate, bounded contextual reading; they neither generated themes nor triangulated educator accounts. Three educator-derived themes concerned changing the terms of engagement before crisis, treating family knowledge as capable of revising professional attributions and informing response, and attributing participation- and belonging-related meanings to coordinated adult support. A descriptive cross-case display showed how reattribution-and-response accounts were distributed across the educator interviews, including partial, ambiguous, and negative cases; these counts describe this dataset rather than estimate prevalence. The mother accounts complicated school-centered constructions by describing contact that could remain delayed, one-directional, or without influence over decisions. The findings support an adult-centered account of contact, epistemic authority, communicative accessibility, and decision inclusion; they do not demonstrate a causal pathway or student outcomes.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938594$q$, $q$This study addresses the gap in existing social media discourse research, which often treats time, space, users, topics, and sentiment in isolation. It proposes an integrated analytical framework to capture the interplay among these five dimensions, using an online controversy involving Nongfu Spring as an illustrative case to demonstrate the framework's applicability. A multi-method approach combines temporal, spatial, and user classification analysis; deep learning-based sentiment analysis via a fine-tuned Chinese-BERT-wwm-ext model; and influential keyword identification using Graph Attention Network and improved TextRank algorithms. The framework is applied to 12,674 Weibo posts related to this controversy. Posts clustered temporally around key discussion periods, spatially concentrated in economically developed regions, and, while ordinary users produced the majority of posts, verified institutional and high-influence individual accounts became increasingly prominent in the later phases of the discussion. The integrated analysis reveals joint patterns across dimensions, such as the marked shift in user composition over the course of the crisis, that single-dimension analyses alone would not reveal. The study contributes a reusable framework for the multidimensional analysis of online discourse and illustrates its applicability through a single case; its generalizability across platforms and event types remains to be tested in future research.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938615$q$, $q$Children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) are at heightened risk during earthquakes, yet behavioral interventions targeting disaster preparedness in this population remain scarce. This study evaluated the effects of a multicomponent, technology-supported behavioral intervention package on the acquisition, maintenance, and generalization of earthquake safety skills-declarative knowledge of pre- and post-earthquake actions and a five-step drop-cover-hold chain under stimulus control-in children with ASD. Three male children with ASD (aged 8-12 years) took part in a probe-condition multiple-probe across-participants single-case experimental design. The package combined video modeling, slide-based concept presentation, conditional discrimination training, and in vivo rehearsal with least-to-most prompting and reinforcement schedule thinning. All three participants reached the maximum score after three to four instructional sessions and met the mastery criterion (three consecutive maximum scores) within six to seven daily probes. Performance was maintained at 2-, 4-, and 8-week follow-up probes, with one score of 4 at 4 weeks, and generalized to a novel setting, materials, and implementer. Visual analysis indicated three within-study replications of effect, one per participant, produced by the staggered introduction of the intervention across the three tiers of the design, and Tau-U effect sizes were 0.80 to 0.87 (large to very large). Within the limits of a three-participant design meeting What Works Clearinghouse standards with reservations, these findings indicate that structured behavioral instruction can establish earthquake safety responding in children with ASD; replication is required before wider instructional use.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938645$q$, $q$Although student teachers often report low confidence in writing assessments, empirical evidence regarding the behavioral and cognitive mechanisms affecting their evaluative judgments remains limited. This study investigated the evaluative behavior of 161 student teachers, yielding 1932 scoring observations and 2415 coded feedback segments. Fluctuations in scoring accuracy and feedback quality were examined as a function of text proficiency levels, scoring criteria, and feedback dimensions. Participants evaluated three Grade 9 argumentative texts (low-, mid-, and high-level) using a four-criterion analytic rubric. Participants' writing quality and empathy (cognitive and affective) were analyzed as potential moderators. Linear mixed-effects models revealed that scoring accuracy is a function of the interaction between text proficiency level and criterion: accuracy was highest for focus in high-level texts and organization in low-level texts. Feedback quality also varied significantly across dimensions: evaluation quality and rubric-aligned explanations were weakest for mid-range texts, reflecting potential evaluative strain when navigating text ambiguity. While student-teachers' writing quality showed no significant effects, their cognitive empathy moderated text-level effects and positively predicted feedback quality. These findings illuminate the psychological underpinnings of rater cognition, providing a foundation for targeted pedagogical interventions in teacher education programs.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938654$q$, $q$Empathy is regarded as a key affective driver of individual well-being. In the context of a performance-oriented, hyper-mobile society, the target of empathetic concern has shifted "from humans to nature," and non-human life is increasingly treated as a new anchoring point onto which emotions are projected and well-being restored. Extant literature, however, usually reduces empathy to a unidimensional emotional amplifier, overlooking the positive-negative bipolarity inherent in empathic responses and the differentiated effects this polarity exerts on well-being. Yet, the gain-and-loss pathways through which empathy with nature influences tourist well-being, together with their boundary conditions, remain unexplored. Adopting an exploratory sequential mixed-methods design, the study deconstructs the mechanism through which empathy with nature affects tourist well-being by means of in-depth interviews and grounded-theory analysis. Three follow-up experiments corroborate the qualitative findings. Results reveal that empathy with nature in wildlife tourism bifurcates into positive and negative strands, exerting asymmetric impacts on tourists' hedonic and eudaimonic well-being. Restorative experience operates as a vicarious mechanism linking empathy with nature to well-being outcomes. Furthermore, wildlife anthropomorphism asymmetrically moderates these affective pathways-amplifying the harm of negative empathy on hedonic well-being and restoration-while leaving eudaimonic well-being unaffected. Based on mainland Chinese participants in scenario-based zoo encounters, these findings offer an initial, culturally situated account of how empathy with nature shapes tourist well-being in wildlife tourism.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938657$q$, $q$Immersive virtual reality (IVR) has shown positive effects on second language (L2) learning, yet the mechanisms underlying these effects remain unclear. Based on the Cognitive Affective Model of Immersive Learning (CAMIL), the present study used structural equation modeling to examine how affective (situational interest, intrinsic motivation, emotions) and cognitive variables (cognitive load, embodied learning, self-efficacy) contribute to receptive L2 vocabulary learning through IVR. A group of native Chinese speakers was asked to learn Korean words in an IVR environment. They were then administered a lexical recognition task that measured learning outcomes. The results revealed two sequences of associations through which IVR affected vocabulary recognition performance: a cognitive path that intersected with presence and self-efficacy; and an affective path that intersected with presence and emotions. Situational interest and intrinsic motivation also demonstrated a sequence of associations with receptive L2 vocabulary recognition. Although further independent confirmation is needed, these findings put forth theoretically meaningful patterns regarding the cognitive-affective mechanisms of IVR-based L2 learning.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42938671$q$, $q$While prolific research exists on campus gender-based violence (GBV), less focuses on theoretical frameworks that shape how the problem is understood and what forms of institutional response are possible. Guided by PRISMA 2020 procedures, this systematic review synthesized 52 peer-reviewed studies, between 2020 and 2025, on GBV in higher education in the United States, Nigeria, and South Africa. Findings reveal that most studies approach GBV through behavioral, psychological, or compliance-oriented frameworks, with limited research that engaged intersectional, decolonial, or African feminist perspectives. Where these frameworks are used, they illuminated dimensions of power, colonial legacies, institutional culture, and the uneven experiences of minoritized students that dominant approaches often overlook. The review also found a striking convergence across contexts: despite significant differences in policy environments, universities in both the United States and Nigeria exhibit similar patterns of underreporting, institutional mistrust, reputation management, and limited accountability. Overall, findings underscore that the marginalization of feminist theory is a research limitation that constrains how campus GBV is understood and addressed across diverse contexts. By bringing U.S. and African scholarship into conversation, this review demonstrates how theory shapes knowledge production, institutional accountability, and the possibilities for transformative responses to GBV in higher education.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939028$q$, $q$The COVID-19 pandemic exposed persistent weaknesses in health-data interoperability, cybersecurity, provenance, cross-organizational governance, and continuity of digital care. This short communication presents an updated focused narrative synthesis and a five-layer governance framework for integrating blockchain and the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) in patient-centered digital health. The literature was updated through 10 August 2026, with recent peer-reviewed evidence complemented by authoritative standards and European regulatory sources. The synthesis positions blockchain primarily as a distributed governance, provenance, and audit layer rather than a clinical database or stand-alone security solution, while IoMT provides heterogeneous sensing infrastructure for continuous and distributed care. The framework separates: (1) device and data acquisition; (2) network and edge orchestration, including 5G/SDN where clinically justified; (3) blockchain-based governance; (4) privacy-enhancing data, analytics, and AI; and (5) application and service delivery. Homomorphic encryption, zero-knowledge proofs, secure aggregation, differential privacy, and federated learning are treated as complementary controls with distinct threat models and computational trade-offs. The revised framework also incorporates the European Health Data Space, the EU AI Act and its medical-device interplay, and NIS2 as a regulatory overlay. Across six use-case domains, evidence is strongest for technical feasibility, provenance, and programmable access control; evidence for clinical effectiveness, cost-effectiveness, equity, sustainability, and large-scale deployment remains limited. The proposed model therefore emphasizes standards-based interoperability, minimization of on-chain personal data, endpoint trust, auditable human oversight, and prospective real-world validation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939050$q$, $q$Characterizing multi-parameter physiological, acoustic, and environmental changes before acute chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbations (AECOPD) is crucial for improving home telemonitoring and enabling timely clinical intervention. In a 12-month prospective observational exploratory pilot study (N=16), a multimodal Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystem collected daily data on symptoms, lung function, cardiorespiratory dynamics, actigraphy, sleep, fine motor skills, bioacoustics, and indoor air quality. From 427 daily features across 4738 patient-days, collinearity mitigation retained 245 independent features. Linear mixed-effects models (LMM), nonparametric paired tests, and multi-timescale exponential moving average smoothing quantified prodromal trajectories and univariate discrimination during the 7-day pre-exacerbation window. Engagement analysis showed a dual hierarchy: passive background monitoring had high retention (>72 %), while demanding active tasks like peak flow spirometry dropped to 26.9 % compliance. Patients with frequent exacerbations showed higher reactive adherence. LMM frameworks identified a multisystem prodromal footprint marked by coordinated increases in cough frequency, sputum purulence, dyspnea, and resting heart rate. Acoustic measures showed notable pre-event changes in higher-order speech cepstral coefficients, voice-intensity variability, and cough spectral dispersion, providing objective proxies for airway limitation. Indoor environmental signals suggested domestic confinement but weakened after adjustment for macro-seasonal confounders. Multi-temporal kinetics demonstrated that acute physiological symptoms peaked within 72 h, whereas acoustic features required 3-to-7-day temporal smoothing (EMA3-EMA7) to optimize signal-to-noise ratios. In conclusion, these preliminary observations in the exploratory small cohort suggest that the transition from a stable baseline to the prodromal AECOPD phase is marked by a quantifiable, multi-systemic digital footprint across cardiorespiratory, bioacoustic, and symptom domains. Although requiring validation in broader populations, findings highlight the potential of multi-tiered digital health architectures that pair continuous passive sensing with low-friction active reporting to overcome task attrition and secure the uninterrupted longitudinal baselines needed for automated early-warning systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939056$q$, $q$Intelligent tongue diagnosis demands high-precision segmentation on resource-constrained mobile devices. All deep learning models outperform conventional approaches, improving mean intersection over union (mIoU) by approximately 9% and accelerating inference by more than two orders of magnitude. Among the evaluated models, UNet++ achieves the highest test mIoU (0.9543) and Dice (0.9764), with paired bootstrap analysis confirming statistically significant advantages over BiSeNet, PP-LiteSeg, and HRNet. BiSeNet achieves the fastest inference speed (310.03 FPS) and the highest mPA (0.9766), making it the most efficient model for real-time deployment. HRNet and PP-LiteSeg provide a favorable accuracy-efficiency balance, suitable for mobile-based tongue diagnosis. Deployment of PP-LiteSeg on three Android devices achieved inference latency of 233-262 ms and throughput of 3.2-3.4 FPS, demonstrating the feasibility of lightweight models on commodity mobile hardware. These findings establish an evidence-based framework for model selection in mobile tongue diagnosis, supporting AI deployment on portable devices for clinical assistance and remote monitoring.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939059$q$, $q$Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming bioengineering, with applications ranging from diagnosis and biomedical imaging to wearable technologies, rehabilitation, biomaterials, biofabrication, and digital twins. This narrative review aims to examine the major innovations, challenges, and future directions of AI-enabled bioengineering, with particular attention to its clinical and translational development. The literature was searched in Scopus and PubMed, with emphasis on systematic reviews and meta-analyses and consideration of other relevant secondary literature across the main bioengineering domains. A total of 57 secondary publications were included in the principal evidence base. The review process was additionally informed by the ANDJ Narrative Review Checklist and an established qualification and scoring methodology applied to the selected secondary literature. The reviewed evidence shows a broad expansion of AI applications and increasing integration of intelligent technologies into bioengineering systems. However, important challenges remain, particularly regarding data quality and representativeness, validation and generalizability, reproducibility, safety, human oversight, ethical responsibility, equity, and regulatory requirements. Future developments are expected to increasingly involve multimodal, adaptive, connected, and human-centered systems integrating AI with sensing technologies, robotics, digital twins, and longitudinal data. Overall, AI-enabled bioengineering is progressing from promising technological applications toward increasingly integrated and translational systems. Its successful evolution will depend on combining technological innovation with robust clinical evidence, responsible development, and appropriate ethical and regulatory frameworks.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939240$q$, $q$This study aimed to analyze the relationship between buffalo health and the health of participating swamp buffalo keepers under the One Health framework in the Thale Noi wetland, Phatthalung Province, Thailand. An exploratory cross-sectional survey was conducted among 25 participating swamp buffalo keepers using a structured questionnaire covering socio-demographic characteristics, buffalo production systems, and knowledge, attitudes, and practices (KAP) related to zoonotic disease prevention and One Health. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics. The results showed that most respondents were male (68.0%), had completed primary education (68.0%), and were engaged primarily in agriculture (96.0%). The average age was 57 ± 12 years, with an average of 32 ± 14 years of buffalo-raising experience and 31 ± 41 buffaloes per household. Most buffaloes were raised in wetland grazing systems (92.0%), reflecting local livelihoods' close dependence on the Thale Noi wetland ecosystem. Respondents demonstrated a moderate-to-high level of knowledge about zoonotic diseases and buffalo health, with an overall knowledge score of 76.4%. However, only 40.0% correctly understood the One Health concept. Attitudes toward disease prevention and integrated health management were highest (mean = 4.53 ± 0.66), while preventive practices were also high (mean = 4.07 ± 1.16). Practices such as consulting veterinarians and maintaining hygiene were widely adopted, although consistent use of personal protective equipment remained relatively low. These findings provide preliminary exploratory evidence of positive attitudes and the adoption of appropriate preventive practices but need greater awareness of the One Health concept. Strengthening integrated education, disease surveillance, and collaborative management among the livestock, public health, and environmental sectors will help sustainably improve the health of buffaloes, humans, and the wetland ecosystem.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939254$q$, $q$Accurate estimation of climate-altering gases, such as methane and nitrous oxide, from livestock waste treatment is critical for establishing national inventories. This study aims to monitor and analyze the emission characteristics of these gases during the treatment process at an operational Hanwoo (Korean cattle for beef) farm in Republic of Korea to develop country-specific metrics. A full-scale compost shed was modified into a 2400 m 3 flux chamber equipped with fans to control airflow at 183 m 3 ·min -1. Real-time gas concentrations were measured using enhanced-performance laser-based analyzers across summer, autumn, and winter. The results indicated that mean emissions for both methane and nitrous oxide were highest in summer, followed by winter, and lowest in autumn. Daily patterns revealed higher hourly mean emissions during the morning and afternoon. The final estimated emission factors were 1.28 kgCH 4 ·head -1 ·yr -1 and 0.039 kgN 2 O· head -1 ·yr -1. These values are relatively lower than those reported in previous studies. These values were lower than those reported in several previous studies. Differences in facility scale and operating conditions may partly explain this result; however, potential underestimation due to non-uniform airflow should also be considered.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939337$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: Preoperative antithrombotic therapy is common among patients undergoing subdural hematoma (SDH) evacuation. However, the association with postoperative outcomes remains uncertain. We evaluated this potential association using a multicenter electronic health record cohort and a single-center cohort. Methods: We performed a retrospective cohort study using TriNetX, a multicenter electronic health record (EHR) database, and a single-center cohort. TriNetX was used to identify adults undergoing evacuation of nontraumatic subdural hematoma between 2005 and 2025. The exposed cohort was defined as documented oral anticoagulant or antiplatelet exposure within 7 days before surgery, after which patients were propensity-score matched 1:1. The single-center cohort included surgically treated subdural hematoma patients with clinical, radiographic, laboratory, and operative data. Postoperative outcomes included 30-day mortality, short-term thrombotic complications, and 90-day repeat subdural hematoma surgery. Results: In TriNetX, 15,651 eligible patients were identified. Matched cohorts included 1455 exposed and 1455 unexposed patients. Preoperative antithrombotic exposure was associated with higher 90-day repeat surgery (9.7% vs. 6.5%; hazard ratio [HR] 1.52; p = 0.001), whereas 30-day mortality and 14-day thrombotic complications did not differ significantly. The single-center cohort included 164 surgically treated patients, including 103 exposed and 61 unexposed patients. The reoperation association was not reproduced. However, exposed patients had higher unadjusted 30-day and overall mortality that attenuated after multivariable Cox adjustment. Conclusions: Preoperative antithrombotic exposure was associated with repeat surgery in the TriNetX cohort but not in the single-center cohort. Together, these findings suggest that antithrombotic exposure may identify a medically complex surgical SDH population requiring individualized postoperative surveillance.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939395$q$, $q$This study used typical chestnut soil from Huangyuan County, Qinghai Province as the experimental soil, and selected three oat varieties, "Baiyan 7", "Qingyan 2", and "Qinghai 444", for mixed sowing. A blank control (CK) and five treatments with different ratios of exogenous nutrient bag waste (PF-R-1 to PF-R-5) were established. Through soil physicochemical analysis, microbial biomass determination, RNA sequencing (RNA-Seq), and weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA), the effects of exogenous nutrient bag waste with different ratios soil nutrient availability, microbial activity and root gene expression in oats were systematically evaluated. The results showed that most treatments enhanced soil organic matter, nitrogen content, and microbial activity. Transcriptomic analysis revealed that the comparison between PF-R-3 and PF-R-2 treatments exhibited the highest total number of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), with enrichment in pathways related to ribosomal protein translation, oxidative phosphorylation energy metabolism, and DNA damage repair. These enrichment patterns suggest potential changes in energy metabolism and protein synthesis, although direct functional validation was not performed. Moreover, DEGs uniquely identified in PF-R-3 were significantly enriched in nitrogen metabolism and secondary metabolite biosynthesis pathways; WGCNA identified the coral2 module as the core regulatory module, with its genes predominantly enriched in functions related to transmembrane transport and plant hormone signal transduction, which were highly consistent with efficient root nutrient acquisition and the maintenance of metabolic homeostasis. Fluorescence staining confirmed the successful colonization of oat root tissues by Morchella mycelium. Taken together, based on the integration of soil nutrient supply stability, root gene expression regulation, and fungal colonization evidence, the PF-R-3 treatment (wheat + corncob + oat) emerged as a promising candidate among the tested formulations under the experimental conditions. However, further field trials incorporating yield and quality data are needed before a definitive recommendation can be made. This study provides a theoretical basis and technical support for the resource utilization of exogenous nutrient bag waste and the green cultivation of oat in alpine regions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939400$q$, $q$Habitat degradation caused by anthropogenic disturbance is a major threat to global biodiversity, yet the spatiotemporal dynamics of the ecological intactness of giant panda habitat and the associated population responses remain poorly quantified. We focused on the Sichuan section of the Giant Panda National Park and the Liangshan Mountains. Using multi-source anthropogenic disturbance data and three national giant panda surveys, we integrated fuzzy overlay analysis, Theil-Sen trend analysis, Geodetector, and the Mann-Whitney U test to analyze the spatiotemporal dynamics, contributing disturbance components, and population responses of habitat intactness. The mean habitat intactness in 2020 was 6.70. Intactness declined slowly during 1980-2020, and 73.7% of the habitat remained stable. Among the disturbance components of the intactness index, land-use type and road construction contributed most to its spatial variation, and the joint contribution of any two interacting components exceeded that of either component alone. The intactness of giant panda occurrence sites was higher than that of systematic background points (significant in five of the six survey-by-region comparisons) and was higher in the Liangshan Mountains than in the national park. However, intactness was not significantly correlated with population density. These results indicate that wild giant pandas are concentrated in high-intactness habitat, although intactness alone does not predict population density, and they provide empirical evidence for intactness-oriented conservation management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939888$q$, $q$Polysaccharides play crucial roles in medicine and nutrition, serving as dietary additives that help maintain fish health. This study systematically evaluated the effects of dietary supplementation with plant-derived Lycium barbarum polysaccharides (LBP) and Seaweed-derived polysaccharides (SPs) on growth, metabolism, and immune function in rainbow trout ( Oncorhynchus mykiss ). A total of 270 juvenile rainbow trout (15.50 ± 0.71 g) were randomly divided into three groups (control group, CG; 1.5 g/kg LBP; 1.5 g/kg SPs), with 90 fish in each group. The feeding trial lasted 56 days, with sampling at days 28 and 56. Compared with CG, SPs increased WGR by approximately 39.4%, decreased FCR by approximately 44.7%, and elevated muscle Ala, Glu, and Gly contents. SPs also improved intestinal microbiota balance and promoted the synthesis of amino acids and short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs). In addition, SPs significantly enhanced CAT and SOD activities and upregulated IL-10 expression by approximately 2.3-fold. Compared with the CG, LBP can reduce the total cholesterol content by 33.3%, inhibit lipid synthesis, and promote fatty acid oxidation. It also upregulates the expression of the PPARα gene by approximately 2.4-fold. In the Aeromonas salmonicida challenge test, survival rates of the SPs and LBP groups were 30% and 20% higher, respectively, than that of CG. These results indicate that SPs were more effective in promoting growth and enhancing immunity, whereas LBP showed superior performance in metabolic regulation. This study provides a basis for the targeted application of these two substances.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42939906$q$, $q$Soil salinity and drought can restrict oxygen availability in the root zone, adding hypoxic stress to the ionic, osmotic, and hydraulic constraints experienced by crops. Nanobubble (NB) water offers a chemical-free approach for delivering highly dispersed oxygen and sustaining dissolved oxygen during irrigation. This review connects the physicochemical behavior of NBs with plant responses to salinity- and drought-associated hypoxia. It examines restoration of aerobic respiration and ATP homeostasis, modulation of ethylene response factor group VII and hypoxia-responsive gene signaling, recovery of ion transport and aquaporin function, redox regulation, hormonal cross-talk, and rhizosphere microbiome responses. Evidence from rice, maize, wheat, cotton, and controlled-environment systems is evaluated alongside opportunities for nanoparticle co-delivery, nanofertilizers, seed priming, and sensor-guided irrigation. The review also outlines how transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics, epigenomics, ionomics, and phenomics can resolve NB-mediated mechanisms across the plant-soil continuum. Current evidence supports NB irrigation as a promising precision-aeration platform, but field-scale validation, standardized NB characterization, dose-response studies, energy and cost assessments, and long-term environmental safety analyses remain necessary before broad agricultural deployment.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940069$q$, $q$Cardiometabolic risk changes as metabolic state, vascular injury, behavior and treatment evolve. Multi-omics assays characterize the underlying molecular state, whereas wearable sensors and continuous glucose monitoring capture physiological responses during daily life. This review asks whether combining these measurements improves a defined clinical decision beyond established clinical information in type 2 diabetes, atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and heart failure. We distinguish biological association, prediction and clinical utility, and define dynamic risk prediction as repeated estimation of future event probability over a specified horizon using only information available at each prediction time. Large cohorts link proteins and metabolites with future disease, and intensive studies connect molecular variation with activity, nutrition and glycemic responses. However, evidence for joint molecular-digital integration remains predominantly observational. Trials of glucose monitoring and structured telemanagement show that monitoring can improve outcomes when it is embedded in an effective care pathway, but they do not establish the incremental value of multi-omics. We therefore organize the evidence around three requirements: temporal alignment of stable genomic information, intermittent molecular assays and dense digital trajectories; validation against a strong clinical comparator; and prospective assessment of patient outcomes, cost and burden. Emerging sensors, dynamical network biomarkers and digital twins may eventually extend this framework, but they remain experimental or conceptual for integrated cardiometabolic event prediction.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940228$q$, $q$Artificial intelligence-enabled computer vision can increase the frequency and spatial coverage of food inspection, but high predictive accuracy does not establish reliable production decisions. This critical narrative review examines the evidence connecting image measurements with grading, rejection, and process control. Representative studies across produce, grains and nuts, meat and seafood, microbiology, and packaging are compared by sensing modality, reference endpoint, validation unit, and operational action. The synthesis distinguishes visible quality from specific hazards, prepared defects from natural variation, and image-level performance from verified product outcomes. An author-proposed evidence map organizes analytical feasibility, independent-sample testing, operational challenges, external transfer, and prospective impact as partly independent dimensions. It is unvalidated and is not a readiness score or regulatory standard. Task-dependent reporting considerations address uncertainty, prevalence, false rejection, and abstention. Safety-related uses require separate hazard-specific validation, sampling, and acceptance criteria. Process integration additionally requires acquisition-to-actuation testing, accepted-product verification, and controlled updates. The purposive evidence base cannot establish field-wide effectiveness or rank modalities. Research priorities include traceable datasets, mechanistic challenges, and prospective intervention studies. Progress toward closed-loop control should be judged by stable measurements and demonstrated effects on defined product and process outcomes within a stated operating domain.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940255$q$, $q$Sudan dyes (SDs), synthetic azo dyes used exclusively in industrial applications, are frequently detected as illegal adulterants in spice products due to their vivid color and low cost. This review systematically assesses the health risks posed by SD exposure and characterizes global contamination patterns across complex spice supply chains. These supply chains are inherently complex, presenting risks such as contamination, adulteration, and food fraud (FF). Mitigating these risks demands a coordinated approach: integrating advanced technology, enforcing regulatory compliance, and embedding sustainability across the entire chain. Key actions include deploying scalable detection tools, delivering targeted training, and aligning with international standards to strengthen transparency and traceability. Regulatory challenges stem from supply chain complexity, varying international standards, and enforcement limitations in producing regions. However, adopting cutting-edge tools, enhancing supply chain visibility, and promoting sustainable practices can reduce contamination, adulteration, and fraud, strengthening food safety and resilience. In summary, this review emphasizes the need for integrated prevention strategies combining blockchain-based traceability, Internet of Things (IoT) monitoring, harmonized global regulations, and strengthened supplier oversight to safeguard food authenticity and public health.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940258$q$, $q$Spoilage due to microbial proliferation is the primary cause of the decline in the quality and nutritional value of aquatic products. Current research largely focuses on various antimicrobial methods, yet each antibacterial preservation technology has certain limitations in maintaining storage quality and prolonging the shelf life of aquatic products. This paper categorizes the spoilage microorganisms of aquatic products and highlights that Shewanella spp. and Pseudomonas spp. are common spoilage organisms. The multiple antimicrobial mechanisms of hurdle technology are examined: combined hurdles are classified into five patterns (physical-physical, physical-chemical, physical-biological, biological-chemical, and biological-biological), and their aquatic applications are reviewed together with emerging intelligent controlled-release antibacterial packaging. Aims to provide theoretical references for the iterative optimization of aquatic product preservation technologies.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940260$q$, $q$Fruit quality assessment is an integral part of food safety, loss reduction after harvest and consumer satisfaction. In modern agricultural production systems, where quality control is essential for ensuring both domestic and export markets, it continues to play a vital role. Manual grading, which is widely used, is poorly time-efficient, imprecise, and prone to human error, and is reliant upon the human eye and human skill. Such limiting practices can result in variability and, in large-scale production, high operating expenses. In this paper, we propose a hybrid classification model using deep learning features extracted from ResNet50V2 along with traditional handcrafted features such as Histogram of Gradients (HOG), Local Binary Patterns (LBP), and color, texture and shape descriptors. The purpose of this study is to develop a model to combine high-level and low-level feature representation to gain a comprehensive understanding of fruit quality characteristics. The proposed preprocessing technique will be followed by data augmentation, ANOVA-based feature selection, and classification using K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Forest (RF) and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost). This multi-stage course of action is specifically designed so that the extracted features are appropriate, relevant and discriminative for correct classification. For experimentation, standard datasets were chosen as FruitNet and tomato. There are six fruit types included in the FruitNet dataset with three different quality classes, and another tomato dataset with three ripeness-based classes. XGBoost always outperforms other classifiers on the FruitNet dataset with an accuracy of 97.3% and on the tomato dataset with an accuracy of 92%. The findings validate the validity of the proposed model and its use in all crops under all grading systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940274$q$, $q$Researchers are developing egg alternatives to match the nutritional, sensory, and techno-functional properties of conventional eggs, but no single alternative ingredient currently replicates the full multifunctionality of eggs. This review critically evaluates recent advances in egg-alternative systems, with an emphasis on alternative proteins and functional ingredients. Comparative evidence indicates that several legume proteins, like adzuki and cowpea, can provide strong egg-like functionality. Protein concentrates can also provide improved functional performance at lower concentrations than corresponding isolates, whereas cereal proteins generally face limitations related to solubility, foaming, and emulsification. Hydrocolloids, starches, fibers, and aquafaba can further improve gelation, emulsion stability, and foam structure, although their effects depend strongly on formulation and processing conditions. Despite these advances, the principal functional gap remains the simultaneous replication of egg functionalities within a single formulation. Nutritionally, plant-based systems commonly remain limited by incomplete amino acid profiles and lower levels or bioavailability of micronutrients such as vitamins. The evidence therefore supports a design strategy based on complementary protein blends, controlled protein-polysaccharide-lipid interactions, targeted fortification with attention to bioavailability, and processing technologies that improve functionality without compromising sensory quality. Future development should prioritize multifunctional formulations validated against egg benchmarks using standardized functional, nutritional, sensory, and digestibility measurements.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940276$q$, $q$A Kolmogorov-Arnold network (KAN) learns a univariate function on every edge, so a trained network can be written as a closed-form equation, unlike a multilayer perceptron (MLP). A KAN was compared with five models, with selection nested within each withheld-lot or withheld-season fold, on tahini adulteration by attenuated total reflectance Fourier-transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) spectroscopy (1554 spectra, released here) and mango dry matter by near-infrared (NIR) spectroscopy (across-season test). No accuracy advantage was found: the KAN's coefficient of determination (R 2 ) was comparable to a parameter-matched MLP's but never higher on average, and on withheld lots no difference between the leading models was significant. Most of the accuracy lost in converting the network to an equation was due to the conversion procedure; correcting it raised the median external R 2 of the tahini equation from 0.952 to 0.988. Repeated conversions of one network gave equations scoring -0.76 to 0.96, whereas an elementary edge basis makes the printed equation identical to the network up to rounding. With every lot serving as the blank, detection limits for samples resembling the calibration population were 8.9-21.6% adulterant, and blends at 4-12% were not quantified. The KAN provides an exact, inspectable screening calibration rather than higher accuracy.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940299$q$, $q$This study evaluated the fitness for purpose of official Korean Food Code LC-MS/MS methods for five prohibited veterinary drugs and one major metabolite-pyrimethamine, dapsone, monoacetyldapsone, roxarsone, chloramphenicol, and vancomycin-in bovine, porcine, and chicken muscle and applied the evaluated methods to nationwide monitoring of production-stage samples. In 2024, dapsone, monoacetyldapsone, pyrimethamine, and roxarsone were analyzed in 202 muscle samples, whereas in 2025, chloramphenicol and vancomycin were analyzed in 300 muscle samples (total n = 502). Analytical performance was assessed for selectivity, linearity, limits of detection (LOD) and quantification (LOQ), recovery, repeatability, inter-day precision, and between-analyst performance; all analyte-matrix combinations met the predefined acceptance criteria. For chloramphenicol, an LOQ of 0.15 μg/kg was achieved in all three matrices, equal to the European Union reference point for action. An external proficiency test for chloramphenicol in bovine muscle yielded a satisfactory z-score of -0.30. No sample met the regulatory reporting criterion for any target analyte included in the respective annual survey. These findings support the fitness for purpose of the evaluated official methods and provide baseline analytical and occurrence data for surveillance of selected prohibited substances in production-stage meat in Korea.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940311$q$, $q$Mycotoxigenic fungi contaminate cereals, nuts, fruits, and other food commodities, threatening human and animal health. This review focuses on Aspergillus flavus, Fusarium verticillioides, and Alternaria alternata as representative mycotoxigenic fungi because of their importance as food contaminants and their production of major food-relevant mycotoxins. Some methods detect fungal presence or toxigenic potential, whereas others directly quantify toxins. Fungal identification or detection of biosynthetic genes indicates presence or toxin-producing potential, respectively, but neither replaces direct mycotoxin measurement. The diversity of targets, toxins, and food matrices remains a challenge for accurate and practical analysis. Particular attention is given to immunodiagnostic approaches, including enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays (ELISAs), as well as complementary formats such as lateral flow immunoassays (LFIAs), and immunosensors, for laboratory and on-site food safety monitoring. The advantages and limitations of these approaches, with emphasis on sensitivity, specificity, matrix effects, and sample preparation, are discussed. Future aspects will focus on integrated, matrix-validated workflows combining robust sample preparation, multiplex recognition, portable readout, and appropriate quality controls to enable faster and more reliable monitoring of fungal contamination and mycotoxins throughout the food and feed chain.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940427$q$, $q$From a medico-legal perspective, AI functions as a clinical decision support tool without legal autonomy. Responsibility for AI-assisted clinical decisions remains subject to the applicable legal and regulatory framework and depends on the degree of human oversight. The use of artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming intensive care medicine, offering advanced tools for data processing, prediction, and clinical decision support. This narrative review explores current applications of AI in the intensive care unit (ICU), along with associated ethical, medico-legal, and regulatory challenges. AI systems, particularly those based on machine learning and deep learning, have demonstrated promising predictive performance across several intensive care applications. Systematic reviews of machine learning models for ICU prediction tasks have reported AUROC values generally ranging from approximately 0.80 to over 0.90 for early sepsis prediction and ARDS risk stratification, although performance decreases in external validation cohorts. AI has also shown encouraging performance in predicting weaning success and supporting mechanical ventilation management, while evidence of improved patient-centered clinical outcomes remains limited A narrative literature search was conducted in PubMed and Scopus for studies published between January 2015 and December 2025. Additionally, AI contributes to continuous patient monitoring, reduction in false alarms, and improved workflow efficiency. Despite these advances, most models are derived from retrospective and single-center datasets, limiting generalizability and real-world applicability. Moreover, only a limited number of AI systems have undergone prospective clinical evaluation or demonstrated improvements in patient-centered outcomes following real-world implementation. A key limitation of current AI systems is the "black box" nature of many algorithms, which reduces interpretability and may undermine clinician trust. Furthermore, algorithmic bias, data quality issues, and limited external validation raise concerns regarding safety, equity, and reliability. Importantly, improved predictive accuracy does not necessarily translate into better clinical outcomes, highlighting the need for prospective validation studies. From a medico-legal perspective, AI functions as a clinical decision support tool without legal autonomy. Responsibility for AI-assisted clinical decisions remains subject to the applicable legal and regulatory framework and depends on the degree of human oversight, the intended use of the AI system, and the relevant jurisdiction. The emergence of regulatory frameworks, such as the European Union Artificial Intelligence Act and the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), reflects the increasing regulation of healthcare AI according to the intended purpose, level of autonomy, and potential impact of each system, emphasizing requirements for transparency, human oversight, and data protection.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940477$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: Diabetes prevention programs (DPPs) reduce type 2 diabetes (T2D) risk, yet their reach often remains misaligned with the populations most affected by social and structural diabetes risk. This study examined inclusivity barriers within Small Steps for Big Changes (SSBC), a Canadian DPP developed at the University of British Columbia, through an in-depth qualitative case study of a participant whose age, ethnicity and gender identity differed from the program's typical participant profile. Methods: The participant was purposively selected for the present case analysis from individuals enrolled in SSBC and completed the standard 4-week intervention, consisting of one-on-one motivational interviewing-informed counseling, supervised exercise, fitness-tracker- and mobile-app-supported self-monitoring, and planned long-term follow-up. Data were collected from two semi-structured Zoom interviews, six session-day journal entries and two optional non-session journal entries between July and September 2022. Data were analyzed in NVivo using interpretive description. Results: The participant was a young South Asian adult who self-identified as male. Five themes were identified: lack of access, limitations and barriers, lack of support, mental health implications, and SSBC-related takeaways. Barriers included culturally irrelevant nutrition resources, the university food environment, cost constraints, a one-year wait for dietetic care, isolation from peers managing prediabetes, and prediabetes-related guilt and stress. Supportive, non-judgmental coaching strengthened accountability and motivation but did not fully address culture-, age-, student-life-, and mental-health-related needs. Conclusions: This case identifies practical equity targets for DPP redesign, including culturally adaptable dietary resources, peer support, inclusive coaching training and mental health-responsive prevention care.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940542$q$, $q$Background: Digital health technologies can support asthma self-management through symptom tracking, medication reminders, environmental alerts, and communication with healthcare providers. However, evidence regarding patients' perceived needs and acceptance of an integrated smart asthma management application in Saudi Arabia remains limited. Objective: This study aimed to assess perceived need for and intended acceptance of a Saudi-based smart asthma management application among adults with asthma and identify factors associated with their likelihood of using the proposed application. Methods: A cross-sectional questionnaire-based study was conducted among adults with asthma in Saudi Arabia. The questionnaire assessed asthma self-management experiences, environmental challenges, previous digital health application use, and preferences for proposed application features. Descriptive statistics, Spearman's rank correlations, and ordinal logistic regression were performed. Internal consistency of the five core feature-needs items was evaluated using Cronbach's alpha. The Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate procedure was applied to adjust for multiple correlation comparisons. Results: A total of 68 adults were included in the final analysis. Overall, 77.9% reported a moderate-to-high need for the proposed application, 58.8% expressed moderate-to-high willingness to use it, and 66.2% indicated that they were likely or very likely to use a Saudi-based smart asthma application. The five core feature-needs items demonstrated high internal consistency (Cronbach's α = 0.899). Overall perceived need showed the strongest positive correlation with likelihood of use (ρ = 0.709, 95% CI: 0.545-0.844, FDR-adjusted q q p p < 0.001) were independently associated with greater likelihood of intended application use. Conclusions: Adults with asthma demonstrated substantial perceived need for and willingness to use a Saudi-based smart asthma management application. Perceived environmental burden and need for additional asthma-management support were particularly associated with intended use. These findings support user-centered development and prospective evaluation of an integrated application incorporating contextually relevant asthma self-management and digital health features.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940772$q$, $q$Wireless sensor networks (WSNs) are pervasively employed in critical domains such as smart healthcare, industrial automation, and environmental surveillance, where secure access control is essential. User authentication protocols are vital for thwarting unauthorized intrusions, yet the vast majority of existing schemes rely on conventional public-key cryptography (such as ECC, RSA), which is provably insecure in the post-quantum era due to Shor's algorithm. This motivates the design of post-quantum authentication that is also lightweight for resource-limited sensors. To address this challenge, we propose an efficient authentication and key agreement protocol based on Number Theory Research Unit (NTRU), a lattice-based cryptosystem offering proven resistance to quantum attacks. We have proven the correctness and security of our scheme through BAN logic and random oracle model and demonstrated through informal analysis that our scheme can resist various attack methods and possesses high security features. Moreover, we thoroughly evaluate our protocol against the most recent state-of-the-art schemes. The results indicate that our scheme has the lowest computational cost, and its communication cost is superior to that of most other schemes. Overall, our proposed protocol provides a robust, efficient, and future-proof authentication solution for WSNs, well-suited for deploying WSNs environments.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940783$q$, $q$As global energy systems pursue lower-carbon transition pathways, the rapid expansion of shale-gas development has increased subsurface fluid injection, intensifying concerns about induced seismicity and associated seismic risk. Although regional studies have documented specific induced-seismicity sequences, global patterns, regional variability, forecasting approaches, and governance practices have rarely been examined together within an integrated framework. This review synthesizes shale-gas-related induced seismicity across major production regions, including North America, Europe, and China, with particular emphasis on common patterns and contrasting seismic responses to hydraulic fracturing and wastewater disposal. Across diverse geological and operational settings, the reviewed evidence indicates that wastewater disposal is generally associated with larger-magnitude and more spatially widespread seismicity, whereas hydraulic fracturing typically produces localized seismicity but can also be associated with significant events where critically stressed faults are hydraulically connected to the stimulated zone. Building on this regional synthesis, we compare statistical, physics-based, hybrid, and machine-learning forecasting methods in terms of their operational applicability, predictive capabilities, data requirements, and limitations. However, translating these methodological advances into effective risk reduction requires their integration with robust monitoring and adaptive governance practices. Current governance measures can reduce risk, but their effectiveness depends on sufficiently sensitive monitoring, timely data exchange, regionally calibrated and adaptive operational thresholds, and continued post-injection surveillance. Future risk management should therefore move toward dynamic, physics-constrained geospatial frameworks that integrate seismic observations, injection histories, fault architecture, and regional stress conditions to support adaptive operational decision-making.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940792$q$, $q$In next-generation wireless networks, communication systems are expected to go beyond simple data transmission and simultaneously provide high data rates, efficiency, and security. This requirement has motivated the extensive adoption of machine learning methods to develop intelligent and real-time network management frameworks, enabling the system to continuously monitor and react to channel variations and user behavior while maintaining efficient information delivery. In this context, the integration of machine learning with beamforming enables adaptive and data-driven beam direction selection, improving both the efficiency and security of wireless links. In this work, a 3GPP-based system model is first implemented under a no-attacker scenario, and an exhaustive search is employed as a reference to identify the best beamforming configurations. The proposed framework is then evaluated in the presence of an attacker and under different network scalability conditions. We demonstrate that the reinforcement learning-based approaches, namely Q-learning and State-Action-Reward-State-Action (SARSA), consistently outperform random selection in terms of total channel capacity, attacker-detection accuracy, and performance stability. Among the evaluated reinforcement learning methods, Q-learning achieves the best overall trade-off between detection accuracy and computational efficiency. Our results indicate that the proposed framework provides a stable, scalable, and effective solution for joint beamforming and security-aware decision-making in dynamic and adversarial wireless environments.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940837$q$, $q$Urban bus networks are exposed to cascading disruptions whose propagation depends not only on network topology but also on temporally varying traffic states and passenger-flow distributions. Existing vulnerability analyses often rely on static topology or hypothetical flows, limiting their ability to characterize traffic-dependent failure dynamics. This study develops a data-driven vulnerability framework for urban bus networks by integrating a coupled road-bus network, user-equilibrium assignment and a weighted Coupled Map Lattice (CML) model. Smart-card data are used to estimate passenger origin-destination demand, while road traffic states at four representative periods are mapped onto bus links to characterize travel-speed-dependent capacity reductions. The resulting weighted networks are evaluated using degree, strength, conventional betweenness, and traffic-flow betweenness. The improved CML model jointly incorporates topological connectivity, passenger-flow characteristics, external perturbations, and flow redistribution to simulate cascading failures under targeted and random attacks. The Harbin trunk bus network is used as an empirical case study. Results show that cascading-failure patterns are strongly traffic-state dependent: under more congested conditions, failures reach their peak propagation rate earlier. Across the tested parameter settings, perturbation intensity and coupling strength influence the timing and extent of cascade propagation, with responses varying by attack strategy and traffic condition. These findings demonstrate that incorporating time-varying traffic states and actual passenger flows can reveal vulnerability patterns that static topology-based analyses may overlook, providing a basis for time-sensitive disruption management and resilience-oriented bus operations.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940849$q$, $q$This study applies Transfer Entropy (TE) to floating car data from the urban road network of Catania (Italy) to investigate directional dependencies in traffic flows and identify relevant patterns of information transfer. Unlike traditional correlation-based approaches, TE captures asymmetric directed information dependencies between road segments, enabling the identification of predictive relationships among congestion states across the network. We constructed TE-based influence networks using traffic speed data collected over 15 min intervals across 86 road segments over six weeks. The analysis examined information transfer using history lengths of 1, 2, and 3 observations, corresponding to 15, 30, and 45 min of past information, revealing distinct patterns between time windows. Key findings include the identification of recurring information-transfer relationships across multiple history lengths, and road segments with comparatively high outgoing or incoming information strength. Notably, the morning and evening time windows differ in network density, total strength and highest-ranked nodes, although some of these patterns depend on the discretisation and history length adopted, highlighting the dynamic nature of urban traffic systems. This approach shifts focus from traditional traffic prediction to network prioritisation, allowing the identification of candidate locations for enhanced traffic monitoring with minimal data requirements. The methodology provides practical insights for optimising sensor placement and informing further traffic-management analysis based on predictive influence patterns rather than congestion levels alone.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940919$q$, $q$Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping insect pest identification, monitoring, and decision support, yet field maturity is often judged from algorithmic accuracy on curated or internally split datasets. Such evidence is insufficient for integrated pest management (IPM), where decisions depend on pest abundance, crop phenology, natural enemies, weather, economic thresholds, treatment costs, and asymmetric error consequences. This critical review synthesizes the literature on computer vision, automated counting, smart traps, Internet of Things (IoT), edge AI, multimodal sensing, population forecasting, uncertainty-aware AI, decision support, and precision intervention. We examine the laboratory-to-field generalization gap, small-object and long-tailed recognition, ecological incompleteness of pest-only datasets, sensor and trap bias, domain shift, calibration, human oversight, and operational constraints. We propose layered evaluation metrics spanning technical, quantitative, ecological, generalization, operational, decision, and outcome performance, and introduce a six-level Pest-AI Readiness Framework (PARF), from laboratory recognition to closed-loop precision management. We argue that future pest-AI systems should integrate visual observations with weather, crop phenology, trap history, and natural-enemy information, quantify uncertainty, support expert escalation, and undergo prospective external validation to generate timely, selective, and ecologically sound IPM decisions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940928$q$, $q$The emerald ash borer, Agrilus planipennis Fairmaire, 1888 (Coleoptera: Buprestidae), is an invasive pest in Europe and North America, responsible for extensive mortality of ash ( Fraxinus spp.). This study reports its first confirmed occurrence in Slovakia and summarises the subsequent delimiting survey. On 25 June 2026, specimens morphologically resembling A. planipennis were collected from a monitoring trap in a park in Streda nad Bodrogom, south-eastern Slovakia. Eighteen adults were identified as A. planipennis by the national quarantine diagnostics laboratory, with confirmation by the European Union Reference Laboratory in France. Initial field inspections revealed characteristic galleries and D-shaped exit holes on ash trees at the detection site, in nearby forests, and along roads within approximately 7 km. By 31 July 2026, 2250 survey points had been recorded, of which 149 showed evidence of A. planipennis, including adults, larvae, galleries, or exit holes. Old galleries together with living larvae indicate that the pest had probably been established in Slovakia for several years before detection in 2026. Symptom assessment was complicated by widespread ash dieback caused by Hymenoscyphus fraxineus (T. Kowalski) Baral, Queloz & Hosoya, 2014.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940944$q$, $q$Information on the seasonal dynamics of major maize pests and naturally occurring parasitoids and predators in agroecosystems of southeastern Kazakhstan remains limited. Regional data on the seasonal occurrence of phytophagous insects and their natural enemies are needed to improve phytosanitary monitoring and support the development of environmentally oriented approaches to maize protection. The study was conducted from 2022 to 2025 in commercial maize fields in the Almaty and Zhetysu regions of southeastern Kazakhstan. Phytosanitary surveys included visual monitoring of pests and their developmental stages on selected plants, assessment of overwintering Ostrinia spp. populations in post-harvest maize residues, pheromone monitoring of the seasonal activity of Ostrinia spp. and Helicoverpa armigera, and recording of naturally occurring parasitoids and predators. The major lepidopteran pests recorded were Ostrinia spp. and H. armigera. Pheromone monitoring revealed year-to-year differences in the timing and magnitude of seasonal trap catches of both taxa. For Ostrinia spp., several periods of increased trap catches were recorded during the growing season, primarily from June to August, with the highest mean catch of 23 males per trap recorded in the third ten-day period of June 2024. For H. armigera, the timing of maximum trap catches varied among years, with the highest mean catch of 23 males per trap recorded in the first ten-day period of August 2025. Viable overwintering larvae of Ostrinia spp. were found in all 11 surveyed farms. Maize residues also contained larvae parasitized by Braconidae, tachinid pupae, and individuals showing signs of bacterial infection. During the growing season, representatives of Braconidae, Trichogrammatidae, Chrysopidae, Coccinellidae, and Tachinidae were recorded in maize agroecosystems. The findings provide a regional baseline for improving phytosanitary monitoring of maize in southeastern Kazakhstan. The observed year-to-year differences in seasonal trap catches highlight the importance of accounting for variation among individual growing seasons, while the naturally occurring parasitoids and predators recorded in this study warrant further investigation to assess their potential role in environmentally oriented maize pest management programs.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42940978$q$, $q$The tobacco cutworm, Spodoptera litura, is a globally important agricultural pest that relies on a female-produced sex pheromone blend for mate location. Here, we investigated the electrophysiological and behavioral responses of S. litura to its sex pheromone components and structurally related compounds using gas chromatography-electroantennogrm detection (GC-EAD) and field trapping assays. Most of the 17 compounds tested elicited antennal responses in both sexes, although response magnitude and sensitivity differed markedly among compounds and between males and females. Male responses were generally stronger, but females also detected several pheromone-related compounds with high sensitivity, demonstrating previously unrecognized pheromone autodetection in S. litura. In field assays, four compounds, Z7-12:Ac, Z9-14:Ac, Z9-14:OH, and Z9E12-14:Ac, strongly inhibited male attraction to the conspecific pheromone. Other structurally related compounds either had weaker or inconsistent inhibitory effects. Our findings suggest that broad sensitivity to structurally related and heterospecific pheromone compounds may contribute to discrimination among chemically similar odor signals and thereby facilitate species recognition and reproductive isolation in Spodoptera. The potent behavioral antagonists identified here also provide promising candidates for the development of pheromone-based, environmentally sustainable approaches to S. litura management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941047$q$, $q$Maintaining temperature stability within the cold chain is critical to preventing food spoilage and financial loss. Insulated containers (coolboxes) using eutectic plates are widely employed to maintain passive cooling during transport. However, mechanical wear, seal damage, or improper latching can lead to microclimatic fluctuations inside the container, compromising product safety. This paper presents a non-invasive method for the automatic detection and classification of the technical state of coolboxes (fully functional, conditionally functional, and faulty) based on internal temperature and humidity measurements collected via wireless IoT sensors. Experimental trials conducted in a controlled climatic chamber simulating real-world operations demonstrate that micro-temperature gradients and heating rates allow for the early detection of seal degradation and incomplete door closures before critical thermal thresholds are violated. Specifically, the results confirm that uncompressed or damaged seals accelerate internal heating rates by up to 60%, and the diagnostic algorithm successfully classifies technical states with high sensitivity based on a degradation index threshold (∆ Q ≤ 10% for fully functional, 10% Q ≤ 30% for conditionally functional, and ∆ Q > 30% for faulty units). The proposed diagnostic framework supports predictive maintenance and improves overall cold-chain traceability.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941051$q$, $q$Purpose: To evaluate the effectiveness of immediate implant placement and loading combined with autologous dentin grafting in reducing post-extraction tissue remodelling, with particular emphasis on peri-implant soft tissue stability in the esthetic zone. Materials and Methods: This retrospective observational study included 13 patients (14 implant sites) with Type 1 extraction sockets in the anterior maxilla. Following atraumatic tooth extraction, implants were immediately placed and grafted with autologous dentin processed from the extracted tooth using the Tooth Transformer device. Screw-retained provisional restorations were immediately delivered through a digital workflow, and definitive restorations replicated the provisional transmucosal profile. Soft tissue dimensional changes were evaluated by superimposing stereolithography (STL) files from intraoral scans acquired before treatment and after at least 12 months. Vertical changes were measured as a single value, while horizontal changes were assessed 2 and 4 mm apical to the gingival margin. Results: All implants remained clinically stable, with no biological or prosthetic complications. Mean vertical soft tissue collapse was 0.69 mm, while mean horizontal collapse was 0.71 mm at 2 mm and 0.57 mm at 4 mm apical to the gingival margin. In most cases, dimensional changes were below 1 mm, with greater contour alterations at the more coronal level. Conclusions: Within the limitations of this study, immediate implant placement and loading combined with autologous dentin grafting using the Tooth Transformer device resulted in limited peri-implant soft tissue alterations after 12 months. This protocol may represent a biologically favourable, minimally invasive approach for ridge preservation while avoiding donor site morbidity and heterologous grafting materials.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941134$q$, $q$This study evaluated the feasibility of using two hazardous residue streams from municipal solid waste incineration (MSWI) as partial substitutes for conventional constituents of cement mortars. A mixture of solid residues from flue gas treatment and fly ash (MSWI-SW/FA; 19 01 07* and 19 01 13*) was used as a partial replacement for natural sand, whereas boiler dust containing hazardous substances (MSWI-BD; 19 01 15*) was used as a partial cement replacement. The residues were incorporated separately and simultaneously at different replacement levels. Mortar consistency, compressive and flexural strength after 7 and 28 days, water absorption, freeze-thaw resistance, and microstructure were evaluated. At low replacement levels, mechanical properties remained close to those of the reference mortar. After 28 days, the best mixture containing both residues reached compressive and flexural strengths of 50.68 and 9.01 MPa, compared with 52.23 and 9.36 MPa for the reference mortar. Increasing MSWI-BD content reduced strength and increased water absorption, whereas MSWI-SW/FA improved freeze-thaw resistance. The most favorable freeze-thaw performance was obtained for MIX 2.93/17.07, with only a 3.20% reduction in compressive strength after 25 cycles. Mortar performance depended strongly on residue type, replacement level, and relative proportion.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941322$q$, $q$Leadless pacemakers have reshaped the approach to cardiac pacing by eliminating transvenous leads and subcutaneous pockets. Since the introduction of the first leadless pacemakers, leadless pacing has advanced from single-chamber ventricular systems to atrioventricular synchronous pacing, standalone atrial pacing, and fully leadless dual-chamber platforms, enabling patient-tailored pacing therapy. The modular leadless pacing concept allows a standalone leadless pacing device to be implanted initially, with additional leadless devices added sequentially as pacing requirements evolve, minimizing unnecessary hardware while preserving the option for future pacing upgrades. This modular approach has expanded to include integration with subcutaneous implantable cardioverter-defibrillators and leadless cardiac resynchronization therapy, moving towards a fully leadless cardiac rhythm management ecosystem. This review summarizes the evolution of leadless pacing, compares currently available devices and their clinical applications, explains leadless pacemaker implantation and end-of-life management, and explores the modular leadless pacing concept as the foundation for future fully leadless cardiac rhythm management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941811$q$, $q$Against the backdrop of global population growth, intensifying climate change, and limited arable land, ensuring food security and promoting sustainable agriculture have become critical issues. The limitations of conventional agricultural systems are becoming increasingly apparent, creating an urgent need for innovation driven by science and technology. In this context, plant multi-omics technologies serve as the foundational data and technical cornerstone of systems biology and precision agriculture. Integrating multi-level data from genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics, epigenomics, and microbiomics offers substantial advantages for systematically understanding and improving plants. Unlike previous reviews that mainly catalog omics technologies or focus on individual crops, this review adopts a unified discovery-to-translation framework and critically evaluates the strength of evidence across applications. This review focuses on cutting-edge applications of multi-omics in three key areas: improving the traits of cereal crops (rice, wheat and maize), remediating soil health, and innovating products such as biofertilizers and biostimulants for agriculture. We systematically explore the roles of multi-omics in accelerating breeding processes, deciphering complex agronomic traits, restoring soil ecosystems, and constructing intelligent decision-support systems. The review also examines current challenges, including the complexity of data integration and the difficulties of predicting genotype-by-environment interactions. Furthermore, it outlines future directions involving deeper convergence with artificial intelligence, high-throughput phenomics, and digital twins. In summary, plant multi-omics technology is a vital tool for unravelling the mysteries of plant life and is also a key enabler of intelligent, precise and sustainable agricultural transformation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42941855$q$, $q$Rice husk-derived biochars with progressively modified surface properties were investigated for adsorbing flucloxacillin sodium (FLX-Na) from aqueous solution. Four materials-rice husk biochar (RHB), nitrogen-functionalized RHB (N-RHB), magnetic nitrogen-functionalized RHB (MN-RHB), and magnetic nitrogen/sulfur-functionalized RHB (MN,S-RHB)-were prepared and characterised using morphological, structural, textural, and surface-chemical techniques. The sequential preparation routes produced materials with different surface areas, pore structures, and surface functionalities. FLX-Na adsorption increased in the order RHB -1 ). Because chemical activation and a higher carbonisation temperature were introduced only for MN,S-RHB, the higher uptake is interpreted as an effect of the combined modification route rather than being attributed specifically to N/S co-doping. Kinetic and equilibrium data were described using several empirical models, with the pseudo-second-order and Sips models giving the highest coefficients of determination among the tested fits. The adsorption capacity of MN,S-RHB decreased from 84.60 ± 1.30 mg g -1 in deionised water to 60.30 ± 1.80 mg g -1 in a mixed inorganic/humic-acid matrix, corresponding to 71.3% capacity retention. Spectroscopic changes after adsorption were consistent with changes in the surface chemical environment, while the matrix experiments indicated that coexisting inorganic ions and dissolved organic matter influenced adsorption performance. These results show that the combined physicochemical modification route altered the adsorption behaviour of rice husk-derived biochar under the investigated conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42942614$q$, $q$Kazakhstan combines a large agricultural-residue base with an expanding grain-processing sector, creating a credible platform for biodegradable polymers if feedstock availability is matched to polymer chemistry, pretreatment intensity, and realistic end-of-life routes. This critical review integrates Kazakhstan-specific production data with the chemistry of starch, cellulose, hemicelluloses, lignin, and rice-husk silica and evaluates the conversion pathways most relevant to domestic implementation. Collectible dry residues from wheat, barley, maize, and rice are estimated at about 15.1 Mt yr -1, while potato and cereal starch streams provide directly processable carbohydrates. Near-term options are thermoplastic starch (TPS), cellulose/nanocellulose reinforcement, lignin-containing functional composites, and hybrid materials using rice-husk-derived silica. PLA and PHA offer higher-value routes but require fermentation, purification, polymerization or recovery, and dedicated end-of-life infrastructure. The review emphasizes molecular limitations-hydrogen-bonded polysaccharide networks, retrogradation, interfacial polarity mismatch, lignocellulosic recalcitrance, filler agglomeration, and environment-dependent biodegradation-and links them to processing and performance tests. A staged strategy is proposed: compound and reinforce existing starch streams first, build regional fractionation and standardized intermediate platforms next, and develop PLA/PHA fermentation only after low-cost carbon streams, product markets, and life-cycle advantages are demonstrated.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42942623$q$, $q$The persistence of microplastics in aquatic environments has created a need for effective methods of their removal from wastewater. The physicochemical differences among the polymers can influence their behaviour during wastewater treatment and that poses a polymer-specific removal challenge. This study comparatively evaluates the removal behaviour of polyethylene (PE) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) microplastics from synthetic wastewater using aluminium-copper electrodes and investigates the effect of electrolysis time on treatment performance. Batch experiments were conducted in a 500 mL reactor at 3 V and 0.012 A for 30, 60, 90 and 120 min. Removal and sludge capture were evaluated gravimetrically, while FESEM-EDS was employed to characterize the recovered microplastics and identify elemental deposition on their surfaces. FESEM-EDS analysis revealed the deposition of aluminium-containing coagulation products on the recovered PE and PVC surfaces following electrocoagulation. Maximum floc removal was obtained at 60 min, reaching 49.72% sludge removal for PE and 27.18% for PVC, with corresponding sludge capture efficiencies of 65.21% and 63.14%, respectively. Electrolysis treatment times below and above 60 min produced unstable flocs that in turn reduced the removal efficiency. The specific energy consumption at the studied condition was ~0.07 kWh m -3 for both polymers. Overall, the results demonstrate polymer-dependent removal behaviour and highlight the influence of electrolysis time on the treatment performance of PE and PVC microplastics under investigated conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42942669$q$, $q$Plastic waste and sewage sludge (SS) are abundant, heterogeneous waste streams whose utilization and recycling are constrained by contamination, variable composition, and limited resource recovery. This review evaluates their integrated thermochemical conversion, focusing on co-incineration, co-pyrolysis, co-gasification, hydrothermal co-carbonization, hydrothermal co-liquefaction, and the use of SS-derived char (SSC) and sewage sludge ash (SSA) as catalytic materials. Co-processing benefits arise from complementary feedstock properties: plastics provide high carbon, hydrogen, volatile matter (VM), and heating value, whereas SS contributes oxygenated intermediates and mineral phases containing Si, Al, Fe, Ca, Mg, and P. Among the reviewed routes, co-pyrolysis shows the strongest evidence of non-additive interactions, including hydrogen transfer, altered decomposition kinetics, and changes in liquid composition. Co-gasification can enhance H 2 formation and tar conversion, while hydrothermal routes are especially attractive for wet SS but remain comparatively underexplored. The review further distinguishes SSA, dominated by mineral acid-base and redox functionality, from SSC, which combines these mineral effects with adsorption and surface reactions on a porous carbon matrix. Key barriers include catalyst deactivation, chlorine and sulfur poisoning, heavy-metal migration, feedstock variability, and limited pilot-scale validation. Future development requires mechanistic controls, catalyst stability testing, techno-economic analysis, life-cycle assessment, and integrated nutrient recovery to establish practical circular waste-management systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42942924$q$, $q$Background/Objectives: Medical nutrition therapy and structured lifestyle self-management are fundamental to diabetes care, yet conventional mobile health tools typically track diet, physical activity, and glucose measurements in isolation, with limited connection to clinical oversight. This study presents AIDCare, an integrated mobile health platform that connects camera-based nutritional assessment, wearable physical activity tracking, physiological monitoring, and clinician-supervised conversational artificial intelligence (AI) to support personalized dietary and lifestyle self-management in diabetes. Methods: AIDCare links a patient mobile application with wearable sensors, backend analytical services, and an interactive clinician portal. Dietary assessment uses smartphone camera imaging to identify foods and estimate meal contents, specifically carbohydrates, protein, and dietary fiber, against nutritional reference data. Physical activity and energy expenditure are captured through smartphone and smartwatch motion sensors. To support contextual lifestyle guidance, an open-access medical conversational language model was adapted using 24,612 structured digital health and diabetes educational articles. A clinician-in-the-loop mechanism allows healthcare providers to inspect AI-generated recommendations and adjust personalization parameters. System feasibility was evaluated through benchtop and system-level testing without patient recruitment, using synthetic patient profiles across 400 data-acquisition pipeline trials and an intent-classification benchmark spanning nutrition, activity, glucose monitoring, and physician consultation. Results: Across the 400 software-pipeline trials, the platform achieved data acquisition, transmission, and processing completion rates of 97.5%, 96.5%, and 95.5%, respectively, with backend response times under 1.8 s. In the recommendation-intent benchmark, the medical language model achieved 81.0% accuracy, 83.5% precision, 79.0% recall, and an F1-score of 81.2%. The platform successfully linked image-based meal nutrient estimates (carbohydrate, protein, and dietary fiber) with physical activity metrics and physiological tracking within a unified interface under clinician supervision. Conclusions: AIDCare establishes the technical feasibility of a unified digital platform that integrates dietary nutrient estimation, physical activity tracking, and clinician-guided AI decision support for diabetes self-management. By bridging daily nutritional intake with metabolic tracking and professional oversight, the architecture provides a functional framework for personalized lifestyle care. Prospective clinical trials in patient cohorts are required to establish nutritional estimation accuracy against weighed food records, long-term user adherence, and clinical efficacy on glycemic control.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943039$q$, $q$The integrated effects of biochar and humate on soil nutrient status, shoot nutrient accumulation, and grain composition and quality remain underexplored. This field study evaluated four treatments-control, rice husk biochar (RHB; 15 t/ha), potassium humate (KH; 6.3 kg/ha), and RHB + KH-on soil properties, shoot nutrient accumulation, grain nutrient composition, yield, and grain quality in a premium Thai rice production system. Compared with the control, RHB significantly increased soil pH and shoot accumulation of nitrogen (N; +93%), phosphorus (P; +321%), and potassium (K; +75%) and resulted in a greater grain P concentration and milled grain yield (+56%). RHB also increased volumetric expansion (+42%), with a significant RHB main effect detected in the factorial analysis. KH significantly increased soil total N (+11%) and ammonium (+19%), shoot N (+79%) and P (+363%) accumulation, and grain N concentration relative to the control, while also increasing milled grain yield (+144%) and grain protein content. KH also produced numerical increases in volumetric expansion (+21%) and grain elongation (+4%), although these responses were not significant in the factorial analysis. The RHB + KH treatment significantly increased soil total N (+18%), shoot N (+70%) and P (+423%) accumulation, and the grain K concentration relative to the control and produced treatment-specific changes in the amino acid profile. The amendment treatments generally decreased residual soil nitrate and available P concentrations, although the factorial response of nitrate included a significant RHB effect and RHB × KH interaction but no significant KH main effect. Grain carbohydrate content decreased under the amendment treatments, whereas TPC showed a treatment-specific response characterized by a significant KH effect and RHB × KH interaction. Principal component analysis revealed associations among soil properties, shoot nutrient accumulation, grain nutrient composition, yield, and grain quality attributes. These findings demonstrate distinct effects of RHB and KH, individually and in combination, on soil properties, shoot nutrient accumulation, and grain composition and quality. Overall, the amendments showed potential for modifying soil nutrient dynamics and shoot nutrient accumulation while altering selected grain nutritional and market-relevant quality attributes, although responses varied among individual parameters.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943048$q$, $q$This review synthesizes evidence from long-term field experiments, meta-analyses, and high-throughput sequencing studies on combined organic-inorganic fertilization, with a focus on sustainable sugarcane production in Yunnan, China. Prolonged use of inorganic fertilizers degrades soils through acidification, loss of organic matter, and reduced microbial diversity. Integrated nutrient management sustains crop productivity while maintaining soil health, outperforming inorganic-only applications. Organic amendments, including farmyard manure and sugarcane by-products such as pressmud, vinasse, and mill ash, improve soil structure, nutrient availability, and water retention while counteracting acidification. These amendments also reshape soil microbial communities, enriching beneficial taxa, suppressing pathogens, and increasing network stability, alongside functional shifts in N fixation, P and K solubilization, mycorrhizal symbioses, and plant growth promotion. Enhanced enzyme activities and functional microbial abundance drive improved C, N, and P cycling. Based on site-specific long-term trials, sugarcane industry by-products can replace 25-50% of inorganic fertilizer under conditions where environmental risks are appropriately managed. Future research should prioritize region-specific amendment strategies, precision agriculture tools, functional microbial characterization, standardized soil health indicators, and socioeconomic assessments of adoption constraints. This review provides a framework for transitioning to soil health-centric nutrient management, with implications for China's sugar security and sustainable agriculture in Yunnan's diverse landscapes and beyond.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943066$q$, $q$Vegetation restoration aims to recover ecosystem functions in degraded landscapes, yet differences in soil microbial functioning between restored vegetation and primary forests remain poorly understood. In this study, we investigated extracellular enzyme activities (EEAs) and ecoenzymatic stoichiometry in plantation, secondary grassland, and primary forest on the Loess Plateau and evaluated the relative contributions of multiple environmental factors to their variations. The results showed that grassland exhibited higher EEAs than plantation and primary forest. Vector lengths (1.34-1.38) indicated greater microbial C limitation in plantation than in grassland and primary forest, whereas vector angles (40.60-43.21°) indicated predominantly greater N than P limitation across vegetation types. Soil organic carbon, slope gradient, and alkaline phosphatase were the main predictors of EEAs, microbial C limitation, and microbial N/P limitation, respectively. In addition, microbial biomass stoichiometric homeostasis varied among vegetation types, with comparatively weak homeostasis in plantation and strict homeostasis in grassland. Overall, grassland was characterized by high enzyme investment and relatively low microbial nutrient limitation, suggesting that natural secondary succession following cropland abandonment may support favorable soil microbial functioning on the Loess Plateau.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943069$q$, $q$Excessive nitrogen fertilization accelerates soil acidification and threatens soil fertility and crop productivity, highlighting the need for management strategies that reduce nitrogen inputs while maintaining soil function and crop production. This study investigated the effects of nitrogen reduction combined with calcium-based amendments on soil acidity, nutrient availability, and maize productivity in an acidified aquic brown soil. A field experiment was conducted with three nitrogen-reduction levels (10%, 20%, and 30%) and four amendment treatments (no amendment, dolomite, quicklime, and fused calcium-magnesium phosphate fertilizer). The results showed that, under non-amended conditions, greater nitrogen reduction resulted in higher soil pH and measured total exchangeable base cations (TEBC), whereas nitrogen-reduction level had no significant effect on soil pH buffering capacity (pHBC). Among the calcium-based amendments, dolomite and quicklime increased pHBC by 25.3% and 21.4%, respectively, relative to the non-amended treatment, while dolomite generally showed higher measured concentrations of exchangeable Ca 2+ and Mg 2+ and higher TEBC. Amendment effects on nutrient availability differed among treatments: fused calcium-magnesium phosphate fertilizer produced the highest available phosphorus, whereas dolomite increased alkali-hydrolyzable nitrogen and generally resulted in higher NH 4 + -N and available potassium. Maize grain yield was maintained when nitrogen reduction increased from 10% to 20% but declined significantly under 30% nitrogen reduction. Across nitrogen-reduction levels, dolomite was associated with the highest grain yield, exceeding the non-amended and quicklime treatments by 4.1% and 5.4%, respectively. Overall, moderate nitrogen reduction maintained maize productivity, while dolomite showed favorable responses across multiple indicators of soil acidity, exchangeable base-cation status, nutrient availability, and grain yield. These findings highlight the importance of coordinating nitrogen-reduction intensity with amendment-specific effects to balance soil amelioration and maize productivity in acidified croplands.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943076$q$, $q$Rice quality mainly includes milling, appearance, nutrition, eating, cooking and physicochemical qualities. Rice quality/grade assessment depends on multiple indicators (traits) testing based on the recommended methods in the executing rice standards. Regretfully, the quality/grade assessments for white and pigmented rice do not follow a unified standard. The utilization of a series of independent methods means complex operations, higher time requirements and cost because of the hidden, subtle, and irregular characteristics of quality traits. Destructive husking and polishing are usually the prerequisite steps before quality assessment, leading to a loss of seed activity. Additionally, these methods fail to provide spatial information of the quality traits within intact seeds. In this study, microCT was employed to capture the cross-section images of living rice seeds. We performed contrast and segmentation processes by using image segmentation software. Multiple quality traits were simultaneously determined using one group of cross-section images from one microCT scan both in living white and pigmented rice seeds. This research also expanded the scope of quality-related traits and displayed their 2D and (or) 3D information (e.g., linearity, area, surface area and volume). Our achievements can guide the establishment of a new unified standard for white and pigmented rice at the 3D level, and it also shows great potential for application in phenotyping and breeding.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943090$q$, $q$The date palm ( Phoenix dactylifera L.) is fundamental to food security, livelihoods, and agro-ecosystem sustainability across the Arabian Peninsula, yet its production is increasingly constrained by water scarcity, salinity, climate change, and biotic stresses. Smart agriculture technologies offer opportunities to improve crop monitoring, resource-use efficiency, and decision-making; however, their application in date palm production remains fragmented and insufficiently validated. This study evaluates the development, geographic distribution, technological focus, research approaches, and thematic structure of smart agriculture research on date palms in the Arabian Peninsula and other major date-producing countries. A bibliometric analysis was conducted using peer-reviewed English-language articles and reviews indexed in Scopus and Web of Science from 2016 to 2026, with 2026 representing a partial year. Following screening and duplicate removal, 164 published studies were retained. Publication output increased gradually, although the trend was not statistically significant (β = 1.25 articles year -1, p = 0.068; Mann-Kendall τ = 0.489, p = 0.060). The literature was concentrated in the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) region, with Saudi Arabia contributing 54 publications (32.9%). General agronomy/other topics accounted for 46.3% of publications, while remote sensing/UAVs (17.1%), GIS/geospatial technologies (14.0%), IoT/sensors (13.4%), and AI/machine learning (9.8%) represented the leading specific technology categories. Water and irrigation management dominated thematic applications (46.3%), whereas Smart Farming/IoT accounted for only 11.0%. Observational and monitoring studies comprised 50.0% of publications, compared with only 6.1% involving field experiments, indicating limited field-scale validation. Internationally co-authored studies received significantly more citations than domestically authored studies (16.55 vs. 8.68; p = 0.0007). Thematic analysis identified four major clusters: geospatial monitoring, water management, pest control, and sustainability, with AI/machine learning showing marked growth after 2022. Altogether, the field is expanding but remains fragmented, highlighting the need for integrated IoT and sensor networks, rigorous field trials, AI-driven decision-support systems, and stronger regional and international collaboration to enable scalable, context-specific smart agriculture for date palm production.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943125$q$, $q$Climate change is increasing the frequency and intensity of abiotic stresses, including drought, high temperature, salinity, and waterlogging, which not only directly constrain crop growth and productivity but also reshape plant-pathogen interactions by altering host physiology and immune responses. These climate-driven environmental pressures can accelerate pathogen spread and generational turnover while compromising crop disease resistance through complex interactions between abiotic stress responses and plant immunity. Concurrently, modern cultivars have lost a substantial portion of their genetic diversity through prolonged productivity-focused selection, limiting their capacity to respond to rapidly changing environmental and disease pressures. Consequently, resistance strategies relying primarily on a few major resistance genes and Marker-Assisted Selection (MAS) are becoming increasingly insufficient for maintaining stable crop performance under climate change. This review discusses next-generation strategies for developing climate-resilient disease-resistant crops through three complementary approaches. First, we examine quantitative disease resistance (QDR), which is typically governed by multiple loci with small to moderate effects and can contribute to durable resistance, although its effectiveness depends on the host genotype, pathogen isolate, and environment. We also evaluate the application of genomic selection (GS) to predict quantitative resistance and select individuals with favorable genomic estimated breeding values across major crops. Third, we highlight AI-driven precision breeding that integrates genomic, transcriptomic, metabolomic, phenomic, and environmental information to decipher complex genotype-by-environment (G × E) interactions and improve the prediction of stress-associated disease resistance. Collectively, integrating genetic diversity, genome-wide prediction, multi-omics, and AI provides a framework for breeding toward durable disease resistance and stable productivity under increasingly complex abiotic stress environments, with resistance performance requiring validation across relevant pathogen isolates and environments.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943152$q$, $q$Aiming to solve the problems of excessive chemical fertilizer application and reduced soil fertility in Lonicera caerulea cultivation, this study investigated soil carbon and nitrogen nutrients, enzyme activities, and rhizosphere microbial characteristics using potted seedlings. Seven treatments, including control, single straw application, single nitrogen fertilization, and four C/N ratios (10, 15, 20, 25), were established. The results showed that a soil C/N ratio of 15-25 achieved the optimal soil fertilization effect. The 20:1 C/N ratio produced the highest soil total nitrogen and active organic carbon contents, as well as the maximum nutrient-converting enzyme activity. C/N ratio significantly regulated rhizosphere microbial communities: increasing nitrogen improved bacterial diversity but slightly reduced fungal diversity, and C/N imbalance altered dominant fungal phyla. Soil nitrogen and active organic carbon were positively correlated with bacterial diversity and negatively correlated with fungal diversity. Based on a comprehensive analysis of various indicators, a C/N ratio range of 15:1 to 25:1 improves soil fertility, while a ratio of 20:1 yields the most balanced overall results, enhancing soil carbon and nitrogen reserves, optimizing the microbial community, and reducing nutrient loss. This study provides a reference for fertilizer reduction and efficiency improvement in Lonicera caerulea cultivation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943156$q$, $q$This study evaluated hydrochar and arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi (AMF) for improving coal gangue-amended sandy soil and plant performance. A ryegrass ( Lolium perenne L.) pot experiment compared corncob, corn straw, and rice husk hydrochars applied at 2-8%. Selected 8% hydrochars were then evaluated in a four-month alfalfa ( Medicago sativa L.) rhizobox experiment to examine soil and plant responses across a 30 cm soil profile and the additional response to AMF inoculation. Hydrochar significantly affected soil nutrient status and all seven ryegrass growth and root traits, with 8% rice husk hydrochar (RHH8) showing the strongest overall performance. After cultivation, soil organic matter was 32.85 g kg -1 under RHH8 versus 14.81 g kg -1 under the control (CK). In the rhizobox experiment, compared with 8% rice husk hydrochar alone (RHH), AMF addition to RHH was associated with higher soil nutrient levels, greater dry-sieved aggregation and water retention, and a greater maximum root length in alfalfa (33.46 vs. 25.68 cm). Total soil concentrations of all eight measured metals were below the GB 15618-2018 agricultural-land risk screening values at the measured endpoints. Overall, RHH performed consistently well, with additional improvements following AMF inoculation. These short-term findings support further evaluation for restoring coal gangue-amended sandy soil.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943555$q$, $q$This study addresses the need for reliable methods in wastewater-based antimicrobial resistance monitoring by comparing three sample preparation approaches for analyzing intracellular DNA (iDNA) and extracellular DNA (exDNA) in raw wastewater: a filtration-based method, a centrifugation-based method, and a combination of the two (combined). We evaluated method repeatability, operational time, usability, consumable costs, qualitative workflow characteristics, and descriptive recovery. Selected antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) ( sul1, bla OXA48 group, bla VIM1 group, vanA, tetW, QnrS1 ) and the mobile genetic element intI1 were quantified by digital PCR. Most target signals occurred in the iDNA-enriched fraction (>98%). Filtration and the combined method received higher usability scores than centrifugation, whereas the combined workflow required approximately 15% higher consumable costs. Storage effects were target-, fraction-, and temperature-dependent. No common holding time satisfied the complete two-sided operational acceptance criteria. Considering decreases in relative recovery alone, storage at 5 °C for up to six days did not reduce any mean recovery below its target- and fraction-specific lower limit. Storage at -20 °C produced decreases in iDNA recovery after 24 h. These findings offer practical guidance for PCR-based AMR monitoring in wastewater.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943559$q$, $q$Plant litter decomposition drives soil biogeochemical cycling, yet how warming alters interactions among litter quality, microbial communities, and soil C, N, and P transformations remains unclear. We conducted a 90-day soil microcosm incubation at 15, 25, and 35 °C using single and mixed litter of Calamagrostis angustifolia and Carex lasiocarpa from the Xingkai Lake wetland to assess warming effects on decomposition, microbial communities, and C, N, and P dynamics. Warming significantly accelerated decomposition, with the highest decomposition constant observed for mixed litter at 35 °C. Mixed litter showed greater temperature sensitivity at 25-35 °C, whereas decomposition rates were weakly correlated with initial litter C/N, C/P, and N/P ratios. Warming and decomposition time reshaped microbial communities, with bacterial diversity increasing over time and fungal diversity declining at 35 °C. Despite accelerated mass loss, C, N, and P dynamics remained asynchronous, with net C release in some treatments, net N accumulation, and litter-dependent P responses. Three-way ANOVA, RDA, and partial least squares structural equation modeling were used to examine associations among environmental factors, microbial attributes, litter decomposition, and soil C, N, and P status. Overall, warming modifies relationships among litter quality, microbial communities, and C, N, and P transformations, providing insights into freshwater wetland responses to climate warming.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943602$q$, $q$Organic fertilizers are rich in nutrients but also frequently contain multiple contaminants that pose a potential threat to the agricultural environment. However, comprehensive investigations into commercial organic fertilizers remain scarce. In this study, 54 commercial organic fertilizer samples-including animal manure-based fertilizers, BOF (bio-organic fertilizer), and other types of fertilizers-were systematically investigated. For OM (organic matter), 48.15% of samples fell below the 30% w / w threshold specified by the industry standard NY/T 525-2021. The exceedance rates of Cu and Zn across all fertilizers were 14.81% and 12.96%, respectively, relative to the German Biowaste Ordinance (BioAbfV) limits. Differences in properties among geographic origins were less pronounced than those among fertilizer types. Pig manure-based fertilizers had the highest nutrient contents, especially phosphorus, but also the highest levels of contaminants, including antibiotics, heavy metals, antibiotic-resistant bacteria (ARB), and antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs). BOF exhibited the best overall quality, with a total nitrogen content 2.0-4.4 times that of the manure-based fertilizers, together with higher organic matter and lower contaminant levels. This study establishes a comprehensive baseline for commercial organic fertilizers in China, providing critical empirical evidence for future industry governance and fertilizer management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943685$q$, $q$The heat-induced aggregation behavior of coconut globulin at pH 2.0 and 85 °C was investigated over a continuous heating period of 0-24 h, with a focus on the temporal evolution of protein structure and corresponding changes in techno-functional performance. The aggregation process exhibited three distinct stages: initial unfolding and dissociation (0-6 h), formation of β-sheet-enriched structures (6-12 h), and subsequent over-aggregation (12-24 h). Surface hydrophobicity reached its maximum at 20 h, foaming capacity peaked at 12 h, and emulsifying activity achieved its optimal value at 10 h. These time-dependent functional variations were closely correlated with progressive conformational rearrangement, peptide chain degradation, and interfacial structural changes. The results establish a time-resolved structure-function correlation for coconut globulin under acidic heating conditions, offering practical guidance for the processing of coconut protein as a plant-based functional ingredient.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943786$q$, $q$Contamination of feed with zearalenone (ZEN) poses a threat to animal and human health, which drives interest in low-cost sorbents derived from plant waste. For non-activated biochars, the properties that govern ZEN binding remain not fully studied. A hypothesis was proposed stating that binding efficiency depends primarily on the amount and type of the mineral fraction rather than on porosity or degree of carbonization. Four feedstocks were pyrolyzed under identical conditions (550 °C, 10 °C/min, N 2 ): Amaranthus cruentus (AC) and Amaranthus retroflexus (AR) biomass (Ca- and K-rich ash; 28-34 wt%), rice husk (RH; Si-rich ash; 44 wt%), and buckwheat husk (BH; 7 wt% ash). The resulting biochars were characterized by proximate analysis, X-ray fluorescence (XRF), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), low-temperature nitrogen adsorption (BET/BJH), and iodine adsorption. ZEN-binding capacity was assessed in vitro (adsorption at a nominal pH of 2.0, desorption at pH 8.0; 37 °C; toxin-to-sorbent mass ratio of 1:1000). All biochars had a very low BET specific surface area (0.45-2.30 m 2 /g). ZEN removal efficiency decreased in the order ACB > ARB > RHB > BHB (90.5 > 77.3 > 65.8 > 10.6%), and desorption did not exceed 1.5% of the bound toxin. No statistically significant correlation between any property and ZEN removal efficiency was found for the four samples; the observed order is consistent with the hypothesis that calcite-containing Ca/K ash plays an important role, but does not conclusively prove it. All biochars were non-toxic to the ciliate Stylonychia mytilus (survival > 70%). ACB merits further investigation, including adsorption isotherms, kinetic studies, experiments at different pH values, and in vivo trials.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42943941$q$, $q$This work presents a solar-assisted wearable pupil-tracking microsystem that integrates on-device inference, head pose sensing, energy harvesting, and mobile visualization within a single hat-based platform. The device is fabricated using 3D-printed thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU), providing a flexible and durable structure that supports embedded electronics while maintaining user comfort. The brim houses two ESP32-S3 microcontrollers, two grayscale eye-facing cameras, an inertial measurement unit (IMU), a lithium-polymer battery, a custom-shaped flexible amorphous silicon photovoltaic module, and a charging circuit. Each microcontroller runs an INT8-quantized convolutional neural network (CNN) that performs real-time 2D pupil coordinate regression and eye closure classification from 64 × 64 grayscale images. Pupil coordinates, eye state information, and head pose measurements are transmitted via Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) to a custom iOS application, where a Unity-based 3D avatar mirrors the wearer's relative eye movement and head motion while enabling lightweight data logging for offline analysis. By relocating sensing, computation, and power electronics from eyewear to the hat brim, the proposed system enables unobtrusive, autonomous pupil and eye state monitoring for naturalistic cognitive and behavioral research without requiring personal point-of-gaze calibration.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944030$q$, $q$Zoonotic viral diseases continue to pose major challenges to global health, particularly as environmental, social and economic changes increasingly influence interactions among humans, animals, vectors, and ecosystems. This review explores Rift Valley fever virus (RVFV), Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever virus (CCHFV), and hantaviruses (HTVs) within the framework of One Health, emphasizing the ecological and epidemiological factors that govern their emergence and transmission. Despite their distinct transmission cycles, these viruses share important determinants of zoonotic risk. RVFV is primarily maintained through interactions between mosquitoes, livestock, wildlife, and humans; CCHFV is sustained within complex tick-vertebrate systems involving Hyalomma ticks, domestic animals, and wildlife, while HTVs are predominantly maintained through persistent infections in mammalian reservoir hosts, particularly rodents. Climate variability, habitat alteration, agricultural expansion, livestock movement, urbanization, and other anthropogenic pressures can modify these transmission systems and create new opportunities for pathogen spillover. Effective prevention and control therefore require integrated surveillance that encompasses human disease, animal populations, vectors or wildlife reservoirs, and environmental conditions. Advances in molecular diagnostics, genomic epidemiology, ecological modeling, remote sensing, and climate-based forecasting provide valuable opportunities for improving early detection and outbreak preparedness. To reduce the burden of these zoonotic viral diseases, a sustained collaboration among human and veterinary health professionals, ecologists, entomologists, environmental scientists, and public health authorities is required. Therefore, a coordinated One Health approach is essential for anticipating emerging threats, strengthening preparedness, and protecting human, animal, and ecosystem health.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944228$q$, $q$At present, piezoelectric actuators featuring both multi-directional output capability and autonomous backward motion suppression are scarce, which limits the application of piezoelectric actuation technology. To address this issue, inspired by the rapid opening-closing switching of crab claws during movement, a novel bionic crab-claw stick-slip piezoelectric actuator is designed based on the stick-slip actuation principle. The actuator adopts a symmetric structure and exhibits bi-directional motion characteristics and is equipped with a flexible hinge amplification mechanism for autonomous backward motion suppression. In this paper, the structural reliability is first verified through mechanical theoretical analysis and the finite element method. Then, an experimental platform is built, and a prototype is fabricated for experimental testing. The results show that the proposed actuator not only has high symmetry in bi-directional output performance but also demonstrates the effectiveness of the backward motion suppression mechanism in the designed comparative experiments. The mechanism can achieve high-speed motion at low frequencies, with a maximum speed of 4.58 mm/s, which exhibits a high speed under comparable dimensions and a lower driving frequency, and the maximum load capacity reaches 0.9 N.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944236$q$, $q$Machine vision enables efficient, non-contact industrial quality inspection. Online inspection of printed laminated films is challenging because complex graphics and transport-induced stretching cause appearance variations and spatially nonuniform mismatches. Global registration cannot fully correct these mismatches, whereas unrestricted dense deformation may absorb small defects. We propose an interpretable system based on a qualified multi-template library and residual-driven column-band compensation. Low-resolution retrieval selects geometrically compatible references; intermediate-resolution ORB-RANSAC registration and original-resolution warping then establish global correspondence. Compensation is activated only in residual-selected bands and constrained to the web-motion direction. Template-tolerant multi-scale Frangi comparison and component-level multi-template voting generate the final defect mask. The independent test set comprised 1500 production-line images: 200 defect-bearing images containing 823 annotated defect instances and 1300 defect-free images. Under the defect-instance-level evaluation, the system produced 765 true positives, 58 false negatives, and 31 false-positive predicted components, corresponding to 92.95% recall and 96.11% precision. The mean processing time was 357.06 ms/image. The results indicate that the method can balance residual-mismatch suppression and defect preservation under the evaluated production conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944250$q$, $q$Real-time numerical abundance monitoring in high-density recirculating aquaculture systems (RASs) faces severe bottlenecks due to target occlusion saturation and spatial sampling mismatch. This study establishes an acoustic-statistical framework coupling deep learning detection with spatial statistical theory across sequential detection, correction, and scale expansion stages. To resolve systematic counting underestimation caused by target occlusion and acoustic shadowing, a Poisson coverage model incorporating morphological eccentricity (e) is formulated to statistically correct visual detection biases. To extrapolate localized acoustic observations to whole-pond fish abundance, a spatial-scale expansion model is developed based on localized roaming behavior and spatial stratification, parameterizing horizontal (α) and vertical (ν) distribution coefficients. Under controlled density gradients (20-100 individuals), the framework achieves a minimum relative estimation error of 9.58%. In commercial pond validation trials (6000-6500 individuals), post-correction global relative errors drop to 1.38-16.43%, substantially improving upon uncorrected visual detections (38.77-53.12%). This framework offers an automated, frame-level acoustic sensing workflow for monitoring fish abundance, providing foundational numerical data for feeding optimization and stock tracking in intensive aquaculture facilities.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944258$q$, $q$Driven by the advancement of intelligent security, person re-identification has evolved from single-modality imagery to complex multimodal tasks involving infrared, text, sketches, and paintings. However, most existing methods are restricted to fixed modality pairs and struggle to accommodate the flexible fusion requirements of variable inputs, making the integration of arbitrary modality combinations a critical challenge. To address this, we propose the Unified Embedding Representation Fusion Network (UERF-Net), a framework based on unified embedding representation fusion. This method first establishes a unified embedding space to map heterogeneous modalities into a shared representation, effectively narrowing the modality gap through an explicit pre-alignment strategy. It then introduces a composable fusion mechanism utilizing a joint self-attention module to facilitate deep token-level interactions across diverse modality combinations. Furthermore, a distribution alignment strategy is employed to optimize similarity distributions via Supervised Distribution Alignment (SDA), significantly enhancing feature stability and identity discriminative power. Experimental results on the five-modality ORBench benchmark demonstrate that UERF-Net achieves state-of-the-art performance in Rank-1 accuracy (R1) and mean average precision (mAP), confirming the robustness and superior adaptability of our unified embedding-based modeling in complex, multi-source retrieval scenarios.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944264$q$, $q$Urban drainage systems rely on fixed surveillance cameras that operate continuously under daytime, nighttime-infrared, rainy, and non-rainy conditions, yet most vision-based water-monitoring methods are validated only on daytime imagery and require costly pixel-level annotation. This paper investigates whether a promptable segmentation foundation model can monitor water surfaces and waterlogging zero-shot-without any task-specific training or labeling-across the full illumination-weather spectrum of real surveillance operations. We construct a benchmark of 3651 frames from 25 fixed cameras of an urban drainage monitoring network in Zhenjiang, China, spanning four scene categories (river/canal, road ponding, underpass, pump-station apron), seven days in July-September 2026, and all four illumination-weather quadrants, explicitly including non-rainy negative days. Using SAM 3 with the text prompt "water", we extract per-frame water-area ratio and normalized waterline level, and evaluate (i) quadrant-wise segmentation stability, (ii) rain-event detection, (iii) false-alarm behavior on negative days, and (iv) pixel-level agreement with expert reference masks. Zero-shot segmentation met the scene-specific validity criterion on 96.9% of the 162 diurnal-cycle frames at the canal reference site (100% in both no-rain quadrants, 88.9% in the night-rain quadrant); failures concentrate in heavy-rain-obscured and sediment-turbid dusk frames. On 10 August 2026-a day with two visually verified heavy-rain events-the waterline indicator detected both events (peaks 72.9% and 71.4% vs. a 43-62% control-day band), and the same afternoon event was independently corroborated by severe ponding at road and underpass sites city-wide. A level-only alarm rule produced an 11.8% false-alarm rate on 102 negative-day frames, traced to a camera re-aiming event that shifted the normalized indicator distribution; a compound rule combining area anomaly and rise rate produced no alarms on the 102 examined negative-day frames (vs. 12/102 for the level-only rule) while preserving both event detections-a retrospective, in-sample result that we do not treat as a validated false-alarm rate. We therefore froze the complete pipeline and rules and evaluated them prospectively on 14 additional days (7 days with event-triggered 5-min capture and 7 routine hourly capture days; 1072 frames) that had never been used for threshold or rule design: on the 13 visually verified dry days (784 frames) the frozen compound rule produced only six alarm frames (0.77%), all at illumination-mode switchover transients, and a two-frame persistence variant eliminated all of them (0/13 days; upper 95% confidence bound on the day-level false-alarm rate 20.6%) while detecting both the nighttime rise and the afternoon flood stage of a newly encountered heavy-rain day. Pixel-level verification on an expanded stratified subset of 79 frames from 24 sites with expert reference masks yields a mean IoU of 0.633 (0.652 excluding two failure frames) and near-zero false positives on 15 water-free frames (mean 0.15% of pixels); the reported metrics change by at most 0.003 mIoU under ±5-pixel annotation boundary tolerance. Against two same-protocol baselines-an open-vocabulary detector (YOLOE, mIoU 0.268) and a classic HSV color rule (mIoU 0.453, 68% false-positive area on negative frames)-zero-shot SAM 3 is the only approach that remains accurate in the nighttime-infrared quadrants, and its mIoU varies by less than 0.04 across confidence thresholds 0.15-0.35 at the reported prompt and stays within 0.48-0.66 across four prompt wordings. Reference-mask reliability was independently confirmed by a second annotator (inter-annotator IoU 0.71, Dice 0.80). Inference costs ~46 ms per frame on a consumer laptop GPU (not an edge platform), supporting further evaluation of multi-camera processing. These results establish text-prompted foundation models as a practical, training-free candidate for drainage surveillance across day/night and rain/no-rain conditions-promising for operational evaluation, though not yet operationally validated-and delineate the specific conditions under which they fail.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944266$q$, $q$Autonomous navigation is essential for orchard robots performing spraying, weeding, inspection, harvesting, and transport, yet semi-structured orchards combine repeatable tree-row geometry with canopy occlusion, uneven illumination, weak road boundaries, terrain variation, and intermittent global positioning. This review examines orchard visual navigation through a control-oriented chain of scene information, perception and localization, navigation-reference construction, motion execution, and closed-loop evaluation. Particular emphasis is placed on orchard-specific interfaces: repeated row geometry can stabilize reference extraction but can also create perceptual ambiguity; canopy occlusion can simultaneously degrade visual features and GNSS availability; and headland turning requires explicit switching among row following, turning, relocalization, and task resumption. Passable areas, tree-row/canopy structure, and obstacle/end-of-row semantics are compared according to the control variables they generate, including drivable corridors, lateral and heading errors, curvature, safety clearance, event states, and tracking targets. Traditional vision, deep-learning detection and segmentation, depth vision, visual SLAM, multi-sensor fusion, and path-tracking control are synthesized in terms of operating conditions, failure modes, and downstream control effects. The review further analyzes perception-to-control error propagation, end-to-end latency, cross-season generalization, and the mismatch between image metrics and field navigation performance. A unified perception-representation-localization-planning-control-evaluation framework and full-chain benchmark protocol are presented.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944278$q$, $q$The importance of wind turbine reliability for the performance and cost-effectiveness of wind power systems cannot be overstated, and advanced wind turbine condition monitoring is crucial to the solution. Providing effective condition monitoring for wind turbines is still difficult, as there is not a lot of insight in real time, nor is there any integrated sustainability assessment. While some solutions can be offered by digital twins, most existing solutions are not immersive, and do not merge performance information with environmental information. In this paper, an integrated VR-based Digital Twin framework for real-time simulation and condition monitoring as well as deterministic fault-risk assessment of wind turbines is presented. The model is developed in MATLAB using historical UK wind data and includes a reduced-order physics-based turbine model, aerodynamic behaviour, rotor dynamics, thermal modelling, controlled fault injection, and a healthy-reference/fault-exposed dual-turbine configuration. The temperature and vibration results are aggregated into a Fault-Risk Score (FRS) using predefined warning and fault thresholds, while a Health Index (HI), energy production and consequence metrics related to CO 2 are complementary indicators of turbine condition and performance. The results indicate that there is a significant difference between the healthy turbine and the fault-exposed turbine, where the fault-exposed turbine has a higher health-index degradation, lower energy production, and higher fault-risk score. By the end of the simulation period, the turbine's health index was reduced from >80% to 38% under fault conditions, leading to a 5% penalty in energy generation and a 5.1% elevation in the environmental CO2 impact metric. Additionally, the implementation using MATLAB-Unity showed real-time telemetry transmission and immersive visualisation at 72-90 FPS from the communication response time of less than 50 milliseconds. The system therefore provides an integrated platform for physics-based condition monitoring, controlled fault-risk assessment, sustainability consequence evaluation, and immersive operator visualisation, while offering a basis for future individual components and hardware-oriented validation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944297$q$, $q$The fusion of multi-source remote sensing data and deep learning technology has been increasingly applied in precision agriculture. This review focuses on the perception-decision-execution framework and summarizes the current application status, technical routes, and development trends of multi-source remote sensing data fusion and deep learning in precision agriculture. It examines the complementary advantages of multimodal data such as optical, thermal infrared (TIR), Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), and synthetic aperture radar (SAR) in characterizing crop physiological traits and structural characteristics. At the algorithmic level, this review analyzes the evolution of deep learning architectures from single convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to spatiotemporal fusion networks (STFNs), Transformer-based architectures, and emerging state-space models, with emphasis on their capabilities for heterogeneous feature extraction and cross-modal information integration. This review discusses the transformation mechanism from intelligent perception to executable decision-making and explores the application effectiveness of prescription maps and variable-rate task technology in improving resource utilization. This review proposes forward-looking views on core bottlenecks such as model generalization, physical interpretability, and edge computing. This review not only provides a theoretical analysis of precision agriculture but also provides academic recommendations for the development of intelligent agricultural equipment in the future.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944301$q$, $q$Reliable localization is a prerequisite for autonomous excavation, yet outdoor construction environments introduce GNSS degradation, vibration, and terrain-induced slip that can compromise single-sensor estimates. This study reports the implementation and field evaluation of an EKF-based localization system that fuses Global Navigation Satellite System-Real-Time Kinematics (GNSS-RTK), inertial measurement unit (IMU), and encoder measurements within a robot operating system (ROS) pipeline for an autonomous excavator. The fusion strategy weights complementary sensor measurements according to modeled uncertainty and down-weights GNSS updates classified as degraded. Experimental evaluation on a mapped outdoor route shows that the fused trajectory remains stable under non-ideal operating conditions, including signal obstructions and high-vibration segments. The EKF-fused trajectory achieved a path-deviation RMSE of 0.139 m and a P95 path deviation of 0.213 m relative to the independently surveyed reference path. These results support the feasibility of probabilistic multi-sensor fusion for autonomous excavation tasks in challenging field environments.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944304$q$, $q$Low-cost sensors could increase the spatial granularity of air-quality monitoring at 700-1000 times less cost than reference stations (2026 prices), but few field studies evaluate them for gases. This study evaluates a three-tier architecture (low-cost metal oxide and optical, mid-cost electrochemical, high-cost government reference) over 10 months at two London sites, 1.6 km apart. The low-cost sensors and mid-cost NO2 sensor were calibrated with manufacturer curves, linear regression on temperature and relative humidity, and machine learning (ML). After field calibration, the low-cost PM2.5 sensor explains over half the reference variance at both sites, and linear calibration equals ML. The low-cost NO2 sensor agrees less with its reference than the PM2.5 sensor. The low-cost sensors explain over half the variance of a factory-calibrated mid-cost electrochemical CO sensor at both sites only with ML. The mid-cost NO2 and low-cost O 3 sensors fail under all three methods. Calibrations trained on April-October 2025 stop describing November 2025-February 2026 data for NO2 at both sites and CO at one, whereas PM2.5 stays stable. Transferred between sites, PM2.5 calibrations reach R2≥0.56 and gas calibrations R2≤0.28. Low-cost PM2.5 sensors calibrated at a reference site could serve neighborhood-scale monitoring where reference stations are absent, whereas NO2 needs co-location.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944316$q$, $q$Energy harvesting (EH) is an emerging technology for enabling sustainable wireless communication in sixth-generation (6G) networks, particularly for energy-constrained network nodes that are difficult to maintain or recharge. In this paper, we propose and analyze an adaptive energy-harvesting decode-and-forward (DF) relaying framework over generalized α-μ fading channels. The proposed framework consists of a multi-antenna source, an energy-constrained multi-antenna relay, and a multi-antenna destination, with direct source-to-destination communication assumed infeasible due to severe channel impairments. To sustain relay operation, the relay harvests energy from received radio-frequency (RF) signals using time-switching (TSR) and power-splitting (PSR) protocols, then forwards the decoded information in half-duplex mode. The generalized α-μ distribution provides a unified statistical characterization of wireless propagation across diverse 6G scenarios and encompasses several well-known fading models as special cases, such as Rayleigh, Nakagami- m, Weibull, one-sided Gaussian, and exponential fading. The study considers multiple transmission strategies, including optimal rate adaptation (ORA), optimal power and rate adaptation (OPRA), channel inversion with fixed rate (CIFR), and truncated channel inversion with fixed rate (TIFR). Exact-form expressions for the outage probability, Bit error rate (BER) using the moment-generating-function approach, and channel capacity are derived for the considered adaptive transmission strategies. The diversity order is also characterized to evaluate system performance under different numbers of antenna elements, transmit power levels, energy-harvesting ratios, and fading severities. Numerical results from Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate the performance of the proposed framework under different system configurations, including Amazon Kubernetes Service (AKS). The analytical results closely match the simulation results, validating the derived expressions. Overall, the proposed EH-enabled Decode-and-Forward (DF) relaying framework provides a flexible approach to improving reliability, reducing outage probability and BER, and enhancing channel capacity in energy-constrained 6G wireless networks.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944318$q$, $q$Intelligent transportation systems increasingly rely on electric vehicle fleets operating under uncertain conditions, where routing decisions must adapt to changes in customer demand, service duration, and travel time while satisfying battery, charging, capacity, and time-window constraints. This work extends a genetic programming (GP)-based route generation scheme (RGS) to the stochastic electric vehicle routing problem with time windows (EVRPTW) and positions it as a downstream decision layer for sensor-enabled fleet management: progressively revealed operational conditions update the route state and are translated into online routing actions without repeated optimization. The experiments represent this information flow through controlled stochastic perturbations, enabling reproducible evaluation independently of a particular sensing configuration. The GP representation is extended with stochastic and global-state descriptors, and two capacity-aware RGS variants are introduced. Five RGS variants are evaluated across multiple stochastic scenarios for fleet size, energy consumption, and total tardiness. Limited parallelism performs best for fleet-size minimization, sequential construction for energy minimization, and parallel construction for tardiness minimization. Across the evaluated uncertainty scenarios, the identity of the best-performing RGS family for each objective remained largely unchanged, although absolute performance and dispersion varied non-monotonically. Structural analysis further showed that descriptors related to demand, energy, timing, and fleet state recurred more consistently in the analyzed policies than explicit variability descriptors. With an average online route-construction time of approximately 10 ms per 100-customer instance, the framework provides a computationally lightweight mechanism for converting sensor- and system-derived state updates into adaptive electric-fleet decisions under uncertainty.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944323$q$, $q$Robotics is advancing toward embodied intelligence and high-level autonomy, requiring the seamless integration of sensing, computation, communication, control, and human interaction to be designed as a coherent system. This Editorial synthesizes ten contributions to this Special Issue, entitled "Advanced Intelligent Robotics and Autonomous Control Systems-Celebrating the 75th Birthday of Professor Imre J. Rudas", and relates their findings to computational intelligence and autonomous systems. Spanning robot navigation, human-robot safety, laboratory automation, digital pathology, manufacturing, medical robotics, and archeology, the collection highlights the complementary roles of hybrid reasoning, uncertainty management, network-aware control, digital twins, and reproducible sensor validation. Emerging research priorities include integrating learning methods with physical models and explicit constraints, coordinating distributed computation and control, and extending verification from individual components to system behavior. Multimodal AI offers further opportunities for task interpretation and adaptation, while reinforcing the need for auditable execution and deterministic safety mechanisms. The central perspective is that reliable autonomy depends on system integration and verifiable behavior alongside algorithmic performance.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944342$q$, $q$Virtual reality (VR) is evolving from a platform for cognitive exercises into an instrumented rehabilitation environment capable of simultaneously delivering treatment and quantifying behavior. This critical narrative review examines how behavioral, ocular, kinematic, physiological, and neurophysiological signals can be transformed into clinically meaningful digital measures, candidate biomarkers, adaptive interventions, and real-world functional outcomes. PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, and IEEE Xplore were searched from database inception to 20 September 2026, supplemented by backward and forward citation searching and targeted searches. Clinical, methodological, validation, and directly relevant technical studies were considered, including studies in healthy participants when methodologically informative. Of 690 records identified, 145 duplicates were removed; 545 unique records were screened and 303 formed the candidate evidence pool for critical narrative appraisal. Behavioral and interaction-derived measures are the most established, while eye tracking, kinematic sensing, autonomic monitoring, EEG, and fNIRS provide complementary information on attention, strategy, workload, and engagement. Quantitatively, combined hand- and eye-tracking features distinguished mild cognitive impairment from healthy controls with 93.3% accuracy, 100% sensitivity, and 83.3% specificity, while immersive VR life-skills training produced real-world functional gains of d = 1.00 post-training and d = 1.12 at 1-week follow-up. However, most sensor-derived variables remain digital measures or candidate biomarkers rather than clinically validated biomarkers; physiology-informed closed-loop adaptation is uncommon, and evidence that such adaptation produces sustained ADL or assisted-living benefits is lacking. Progress now depends on standardized reporting, external and multicenter validation, clinically interpretable adaptive models, and objective demonstration of real-world transfer.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944343$q$, $q$To address the reliance of damping-network-based Schuler oscillation suppression on external aids in pure inertial navigation systems, as well as the edge effects and low computational efficiency of time-frequency transform methods, this paper proposes a moving-window weighted least-squares edge optimization (MWLS-EO) algorithm. By exploiting a priori knowledge of the Schuler carrier frequency and the slow variation in the complex envelope modulated at the Foucault frequency, the proposed method reformulates Schuler oscillation suppression as an envelope estimation problem at a known carrier frequency. On a sparse grid, the slowly varying component is estimated via weighted least squares, interpolated onto the original sampling grid, and subtracted from the original signal. At the boundaries, measured samples are used instead of zero padding. The proposed method is validated through static simulations and a 227-h shipborne field experiment conducted aboard the research vessel "Dayang". The shipborne trajectory includes moderate turns and speed variations, which represent typical long-endurance marine navigation conditions. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves velocity accuracy comparable to that of the Normal Time-Frequency Transform (NTFT) in the middle segment of the trajectory. At the trajectory boundaries, it improves velocity accuracy by 64.7-96.1% compared with pure INS and by 42.7-73.4% compared with NTFT. Moreover, the latitude and longitude errors are reduced by 36.6% and 3.0%, respectively, relative to INS, and by 29.0% and 2.4%, respectively, relative to NTFT. Moreover, the computation time for envelope estimation and Schuler component extraction is reduced from 18.7 h to 87 s, while edge effects are significantly attenuated. This demonstrates an effective trade-off between accuracy and efficiency for long-endurance autonomous navigation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944354$q$, $q$Fire detection in warehouse scenarios is challenged by complex background interference, weak features of small flame targets, and indistinct flame textures, which can reduce detection performance and constrain model deployment. To address these issues, we propose FC-YOLO (Fire Complexity-YOLO), a lightweight fire detection algorithm based on YOLOv11n. A dynamic upsampling module (DySample) is introduced into the neck network to adaptively adjust feature sampling locations and reduce information loss. An adaptive downsampling module (ADown) is employed in the backbone and neck networks to reduce computational complexity while enabling efficient multi-scale feature extraction. A residual multi-scale attention module (IEMA) is constructed in the neck network to enhance feature representation and focus on critical regions. Finally, an additional small-object detection layer (P2) is introduced to improve small-flame detection. On a self-built flame dataset, FC-YOLO achieves 91.8% precision, 84.7% recall, 90.1% mAP@0.5, and 50.0% mAP@0.5:0.95 with 2.2M parameters. Compared with YOLOv11n, these metrics improve by 1.8%, 4.6%, 2.4%, and 3.7%, respectively, while parameters decrease by 14%. These results demonstrate that FC-YOLO improves detection performance and robustness in complex fire scenarios while maintaining a lightweight architecture, indicating strong potential for deployment on edge devices.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944360$q$, $q$Hydropower vibration monitoring requires early warnings with manageable inspection costs despite scarce verified fault labels. HydroPrior combines offline large language model (LLM) priors with lightweight residual-based warning. The LLM converts channel descriptions and training-set statistics into frozen operating-context features, which enter a compact forecasting correction head. Residual scoring, calibrated thresholds, and contextual confirmation then produce budgeted alerts. Evaluation uses rapid vibration transitions as proxy events and the Balanced Warning Score (BWS) to summarize event coverage and alarm burden. On a public hydropower bearing-vibration dataset, HydroPrior obtains the lowest average forecasting error among the evaluated models. Under the proxy-warning protocol, its mean BWS is 0.7492 (standard deviation: 0.0405), with a transition-hit rate of 0.9106, 0.15 false alarms per 1000 non-transition points, and 0.0199 alert coverage. Comparisons using a common confirmation mechanism and coverage budget also favor HydroPrior. The findings support the feasibility of frozen operating priors for lightweight vibration forecasting and focused warning on the evaluated unit record.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944361$q$, $q$This study presents the design and experimental validation of an embedded edge artificial intelligence (AI) camera system for intelligent vision applications. The developed system integrates a multicore edge computing processor and a dedicated AI engine into a compact smart camera platform, enabling on-device image processing, intelligent recognition, and localized decision-making. The hardware implementation incorporates circuit design, impedance-controlled high-speed signal routing, and printed circuit board (PCB) stack-up design. The signal integrity of the MIPI Display Serial Interface (MIPI-DSI) is evaluated through scattering-parameter simulations and measurements, while the conducted radio frequency (RF) transmitter performance and radiated emissions of the fabricated prototype are experimentally assessed. In an outdoor and indoor parking-lot field test, the integrated system processed images at approximately 2 frames per second (FPS) while operating in multi-task mode at an image resolution of 1280 × 960 pixels. When configured exclusively for license plate recognition, the system achieves a processing throughput of up to 45 FPS. For all 13 tested vehicles, comprising two buses, two trucks, and nine cars, both the predicted vehicle type and complete license plate number matched the ground truth, yielding an integrated recognition accuracy of 12/13 for this test sample. Representative measurements of the prototype's DC input power were approximately 2.20-6.32 W at a supply voltage of 12 V. These results demonstrate the feasibility of the developed edge AI camera for fixed-location parking-management applications and real-time edge inference capability.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944369$q$, $q$Detecting fire and smoke across a wide range of object sizes requires both spatial detail and contextual information. However, incorporating these features into a compact detector remains a challenge. This study presents DSF-YOLO, a fire and smoke detector based on YOLO11n. C3K2-MFB introduces wavelet processing into C3K2 to represent coarse structures and fine details in separate frequency subbands. DyFusion Block combines local convolutional features with transformer-based context to support complementary feature processing. ADown is also introduced to reduce the cost of downsampling. For bounding-box regression, AIM-MSAIoU combines auxiliary-box overlap at a fixed scale of 0.7 with a shape-weighted corner penalty normalized by target size. The model is evaluated on the Dual-Scale Fire (DSF) dataset, which contains 7572 images and four scale-based fire and smoke classes. Compared with YOLO11n, DSF-YOLO increases mAP @0.5 from 0.905 to 0.919 and mAP @[0.5:0.95] from 0.631 to 0.680. At the same time, parameter count decreases by 8.14% to 2.37 M, and FLOPs decrease by 4.76% to 6.0 G FLOPs. Reported throughput increases from 164.0 to 175.0 FPS on an RTX 4090D at a batch size of 16. The results show that DSF-YOLO improves detection accuracy while reducing computational cost under the reported conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944370$q$, $q$Online semantic Gaussian SLAM typically updates three-dimensional semantic maps using frame-wise predictions from two-dimensional semantic segmentation networks. However, occlusions, viewpoint changes, and object boundaries may introduce transient misclassifications, which can further cause cross-frame semantic inconsistency and error propagation. To address this issue, we propose HCSG-SLAM (History-Consistent Semantic Gaussian SLAM), a lightweight history-consistent semantic fusion method for Gaussian SLAM that introduces a compact Gaussian-level historical semantic state without modifying the main RGB/depth tracking and reconstruction pipeline of SGS-SLAM. Each Gaussian maintains only a candidate semantic label, a voting state, and an effective historical observation count. A streaming voting strategy forms the historical semantic consensus, while a strictly causal target-before-current-update strategy prevents the current observation from contributing to its own correction target. Experiments on Replica and ScanNet used segmentation-network predictions rather than ground-truth semantics as input. On Replica, the mean mIoU increased from 0.300830 to 0.307010, with positive gains in all 15 paired runs. On ScanNet, the evaluation was extended to five scenes, where the overall mean mIoU increased from 0.366279 to 0.372166, with positive gains in 22 out of 25 paired scene-seed runs. Per-class analysis shows that the gains were concentrated in several structural and furniture categories, while a small number of classes remained unchanged or slightly decreased. Positive gains were also observed under all nine controlled transient-corruption settings, and additional persistent class-confusion and structured boundary-shift tests yielded positive or comparable performance across the evaluated scenes. Compared with FD-EMA, HCSG-SLAM achieved comparable semantic accuracy while requiring only 12 B of additional temporal state per Gaussian compared with approximately 202 B for FD-EMA. HCSG-SLAM introduced approximately 2.05% additional mapping time relative to the Baseline while leaving tracking essentially unchanged. These results indicate that compact Gaussian-level temporal semantic states can improve semantic consistency and robustness with modest online fusion overhead.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944399$q$, $q$Reliable quality monitoring at the front end of sustainable cold chains requires direct observations of product condition in addition to environmental measurements. This study developed an energy-efficient edge vision system for six coffee fruit ripeness and visible-condition classes on an RK3576 neural processing unit (NPU). YOLO11 was reconfigured by combining a Branch-Integrated Feature Aggregation module for fine-grained representation, a YOLOv10-derived Cross-Stage Partial Network with Compact Inverted Block for efficient multi-scale fusion, and a Simplified Regression Head with reduced regression channels. On a public benchmark derived from 996 source images, YOLO11 and YOLO11BCS yielded mean average precision values at an intersection-over-union threshold of 0.5 (mAP50) of 81.5% and 83.2%, respectively, under the matched 320-pixel protocol. At a 640-pixel input, the computational complexity decreased from 6.3 to 5.5 giga floating-point operations. The largest class-level mAP50 difference was 9.7 percentage points for overripe fruit. Under controlled synthetic degradations, the model produced higher point estimates than YOLO11 for illumination variation and rain, whereas severe Gaussian noise and motion blur remained limiting. Board-input measurements showed throughput increases of 24.2% and 22.1% at 640- and 320-pixel inputs, respectively. The 320-pixel system reached 50.3 frames per second and 10.06 frames per second per watt. Within the supplied benchmark, the matched evaluation supports a comparative assessment of the models, while source-image correspondences across partitions define the scope of the reported absolute metrics. Within these boundaries, the results support low-power visual quality monitoring near the point of harvest.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944427$q$, $q$Accurate estimation of the instantaneous frequency (IF) of multi-component non-stationary signals is crucial for the fault diagnosis of wind turbine transmission systems under variable speed. Current methodologies exhibit several key limitations, such as unsatisfied accuracy of instantaneous parameter estimation due to the inadequate time-frequency representation, poor time-frequency energy concentration and multimode separation effects caused by a single post-processing technique, and the inability of non-adaptative window parameters to dynamically accommodate the local time-varying frequency characteristics of signals. These deficiencies degrade time-frequency plane interpretability and the accuracy of extracting time-varying fault features. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a novel Improved Short-Time Fourier Transform-based Adaptive Synchrosqueezing Transform (ISTFT-ASST) and demonstrates its application in wind turbine bearing fault diagnosis. Firstly, to achieve the precisely parameter estimation of multi-component non-stationary signals, an improved Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) sparse observation model integrated with the Finite Rate of Innovation (FRI) is innovatively constructed. Secondly, Synchrosqueezing Extraction Transform (SET), a time-frequency post-processing technique, is incorporated to further enhance the time-frequency concentration. Finally, drawing on the idea of local Rényi entropy-based window parameter estimation, an adaptive Synchrosqueezing algorithm is designed to dynamically accommodate the time-varying frequency characteristics. Through the analysis of numerical simulation signals and measured wind turbine vibration signals, the effectiveness of the proposed ISTFT-ASST in extracting time-varying features is verified, establishing a novel approach for wind turbine fault diagnosis.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944432$q$, $q$The rapid growth of urban populations and consumption patterns has significantly increased the volume and complexity of municipal waste streams, including recyclable, organic, hazardous, and electronic waste. Traditional waste management approaches such as manual sorting and fixed collection schedules struggle to handle the diversity and scale of modern waste systems efficiently. Consequently, recent research has explored artificial intelligence (AI), computer vision, and Internet of Things (IoT) technologies to automate waste classification and enable intelligent monitoring and management of waste infrastructures. However, the rapidly expanding literature in this domain is fragmented across different datasets, sensing platforms, and experimental settings, making it difficult to obtain a comprehensive understanding of current technological progress. To address this gap, this study conducts a systematic literature review using the PRISMA methodology, analyzing 50 rigorously selected peer-reviewed studies on automated waste classification and intelligent waste management systems. The protocol was registered in the Open Science Framework (OSF). The reviewed works are examined in terms of model architectures, sensing infrastructures, datasets, and system capabilities. The analysis shows that convolutional neural network architectures such as ResNet, MobileNet, and EfficientNet, together with object detection frameworks including YOLO and Faster R-CNN, dominate current visual waste classification research; however, emerging studies increasingly explore hybrid architectures combining convolutional networks with transformer-based models to improve contextual understanding and scalability. IoT-enabled smart bins equipped with ultrasonic sensors, load cells, and environmental sensors are widely used for real-time waste monitoring. The review also reveals a recent surge of research activity, particularly in Asia, with increasing contributions from major publishers such as Elsevier and MDPI. Despite promising experimental performance, most systems remain limited to small curated datasets and pilot-scale deployments, highlighting a gap between laboratory research and real-world implementation. These findings provide a consolidated overview of current research trends and identify key challenges for developing scalable and practical intelligent waste management systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944440$q$, $q$Soybean harvesting is strongly affected by low pod position, lodging, pod shattering, moisture variation, and changing material flow, making stable low-loss operation difficult across diverse field conditions. This review synthesizes 179 core publications identified through structured literature searches and citation tracking, including 69 direct soybean studies, six adjacent-legume studies, and 104 cross-crop technology-transfer studies. The literature is organized around a sensing-decision-actuation-feedback chain covering harvest-loss monitoring, grain-quality assessment, crop and terrain perception, navigation, and adaptive regulation. Low-loss header, threshing, separation, and cleaning technologies are examined as the physical processes through which sensing and control affect harvest-quality outcomes. The synthesis shows that future progress requires robust multi-source sensing under dust, vibration, illumination variation, and crop occlusion; field-calibrated state estimation; coordinated multi-actuator closed-loop control; standardized operating and quality databases; and validation across cultivars, terrains, and cropping systems. Representative soybean studies reviewed here reported reductions of approximately 30.4% in grain breakage, 62.6% in harvest loss, and 50.0% in cleaning loss under their respective experimental conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944459$q$, $q$Hydroponic crop production in smart greenhouses requires accurate nutrient monitoring and reliable control, especially in recirculating systems. This review critically examines sensing principles, data acquisition, control strategies, actuation, feedback, and system integration. Electrical conductivity (EC) and pH are widely used for dosing, but EC cannot quantify individual nutrient concentrations. Emerging technologies include ion-selective electrodes, solid contact and field effect sensors, optical spectroscopy, multi-sensor arrays, and Internet of Things platforms. Their deployment remains constrained by interference, drift, fouling, reference instability, calibration demands, and matrix effects. Control approaches range from threshold-based dosing and EC/pH feedback to proportional-integral-derivative, rule-based, ion-specific, and model-based strategies. Published ion-specific replenishment and hybrid EC-ion studies demonstrate feasibility under tested conditions. However, differences in nutrient formulations, study duration, and error definitions limit conclusions about commercial control accuracy. This review proposes an integrated framework linking sensing, decision making, actuation, and verification, with performance assessed at analytical, temporal, operational, and system/control levels. Future research should prioritize reliable multi-ion sensing, automated data quality checks, delivered dose verification, safe operation during faults, and communication between components. Validation should include complete crop cycles, maintenance, fault recovery, and independent sites. These requirements support evaluation of nutrient use efficiency, operational reliability, and scalability across crops and hydroponic systems under realistic production conditions.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944466$q$, $q$In heavy snowfall, LiDAR point clouds contain numerous noise points caused by scattering and reflection from snow particles. In particular, snow and non-snow points are often spatially intermingled, making fine-grained pointwise discrimination challenging. However, existing LiDAR de-snowing methods have not sufficiently focused on this problem. Therefore, this study proposes SnowRemovalNet, a supervised LiDAR de-snowing network designed for fine-grained pointwise discrimination in complex snowy scenes. The proposed method incorporates global contextual information extracted from a bird's-eye view (BEV) representation into 3D point feature learning. This enables the network to jointly consider the spatial distribution of snow noise and the local geometric relationships of individual points. To account for label uncertainty near the boundaries between snow and non-snow regions, the snow-noise likelihood of each point is represented as a continuous probability between 0 and 1. This value serves as the probabilistic label during training. On the CADC and WADS datasets, the proposed model effectively distinguishes spatially intermingled snow and non-snow points. Using only 30 training frames per dataset, the model achieves F1-scores of 98.10% on CADC and 97.23% on WADS. The processing time is 17-59 ms per frame.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944469$q$, $q$In fire smoke detection, interference aerosols frequently cause false alarms for photoelectric smoke detectors. Conventional closed-set classification models trained on limited samples cannot reliably handle unknown aerosol types, which further leads to false and missed alarms. To address this issue, we propose FireOSR-Net, an open-set recognition network for fire smoke identification using the signals of a four-channel optical scattering detector, which integrates three modules. First, Conv1d-LSTM is used to extract features from dual-wavelength, dual-angle scattered signals. Next, the out-of-distribution detection module constructs an open boundary between known and unknown aerosols and generates pseudo-unknown features to enhance the detection capability for out-of-domain samples. Finally, the distance of all scattered intensity signals to both the fire and the non-fire domain is calculated, and fire and non-fire proximity scores are output to assess their risk propensity. Experiments are performed on eight-class aerosol data acquired with our self-developed four-channel detector, including six known and two unknown test categories. It achieves a peak F1-score of 0.95 on known samples, while unknown smoldering-wire smoke and oil fumes reach 96.29% fire proximity and 89.68% non-fire proximity correspondingly. Such results demonstrate FireOSR-Net's capacity for aerosol classification, out-of-domain detection and risk evaluation towards interpretable low-false-alarm fire sensing.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944477$q$, $q$This paper presents "Do Punktu", an offline-first Android application designed to support geodetic fieldwork involving the localization, documentation, and management of geodetic control and project points. The system integrates a local SQLite database, digital maps, mobile-device Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) positioning, coordinate transformations, height data, photographic documentation, point navigation, stakeout support, data exchange, Portable Document Format (PDF) reporting, and project backup. The application was implemented in Kotlin using Jetpack Compose UI 1.9.2 and Material 3 1.3.0 and supports World Geodetic System 1984 (WGS84) and the Polish national projected coordinate systems PL-1992 (EPSG:2180) and PL-2000 (EPSG:2176-2179). The project produced an inventory database containing 539 point records. The application was subsequently evaluated through functional testing involving 16 testers and preliminary field testing involving 11 surveyors. Exploratory observations indicated approximate distances of 4-5 m from the target point in open terrain and approximately 10 m in forested areas. These values are reported as preliminary observations rather than as a formal accuracy assessment. The results confirm the feasibility of integrating mobile sensing, geodetic data management, and field documentation in one local application. Further studies should include controlled experiments, external GNSS receivers, quantitative usability assessment, and statistical evaluation of positioning accuracy and point-finding efficiency.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944489$q$, $q$High-performance perception systems for autonomous vehicles (AVs) commonly use LiDAR, RADAR, and multiple cameras. These sensors provide useful information, but their cost and computational requirements can be difficult to justify for small prototypes and low-cost platforms. This work therefore uses a monocular camera and combines three vision methods: Canny edge detection for boundary information, ArUco markers for localization, and YOLOv4 for object detection. The outputs are used to generate navigation commands on a commercially available four-wheel electric vehicle platform equipped with the prototype control system. The experiments compare the individual image-processing methods and evaluate the complete system under different visual conditions. The results indicate that the combined approach can provide useful localization and obstacle detection without requiring a large sensor suite.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944500$q$, $q$Deep learning-driven computer vision has become a fundamental enabling technology for agricultural robotics, providing reliable perception capabilities for precision farming and autonomous field operations. Among various object detection frameworks, the You Only Look Once (YOLO) family has attracted extensive attention due to its favorable balance between detection accuracy, computational efficiency, and real-time deployment capability in complex and dynamic agricultural environments. This paper presents a comprehensive review of YOLO-based vision perception and automation technologies for agricultural robotic systems. The evolution of the YOLO framework from YOLOv1 to recent YOLOv11 variants is systematically reviewed, with particular emphasis on architectural innovations, performance improvements, and adaptations to agriculture-specific challenges, including complex backgrounds, illumination variations, severe occlusion, and multi-scale small object detection. Beyond algorithm-level analysis, this survey investigates the integration of YOLO-based perception modules into robotic systems for practical applications, including autonomous navigation, precision spraying, crop monitoring, disease detection, and robotic harvesting. Furthermore, this review analyzes the complete deployment pipeline of YOLO-based agricultural robots, covering benchmark datasets, evaluation metrics, model optimization strategies, and hardware constraints for edge computing platforms. Emerging research directions are also discussed, including lightweight YOLO architectures, multimodal vision-language models, and cloud-edge collaborative intelligence for next-generation agricultural automation. By bridging advances in computer vision algorithms with robotic perception and execution, this survey provides a comprehensive perspective on the development of intelligent and efficient agricultural robotic systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944507$q$, $q$Recognizing a swarm of unmanned surface vehicles does not guarantee that its physical cardinality can be recovered. This study examines how formation, viewing aspect ψ, defined as the orientation of the formation relative to the radar line of sight (RLOS), and sensing resources affect these two tasks in a multifunction radar. A two-stage simulation framework evaluates group recognition against a single extended target (Stage 1) and cardinality recovery (Stage 2) using regularized linear discriminant analysis, with the aggregate nominal radar cross-section held constant. Certification is assessed using one-sided 95% confidence bounds. At N = 9 and baseline resources, group observability is certified in 12 of 35 formation-aspect conditions. For abreast formations at ψ=60∘ and 90∘, the group selection probability is 0.993, whereas the exact recovery probabilities are 0.108 and 0.067. This cardinality concealment, in which group observability is certified but exact recovery is not, persists over N=6,…,12 for both geometries. Coarse size information nevertheless remains, as 52-56% of estimates over N=3,…,12 lie within one target of the true value, against 28% for uniform guessing. Increasing bandwidth up to tenfold or coherent dwell up to fourfold yields no certified exact recovery. These results are specific to the evaluated simulation model and signal-processing framework and do not establish a general limitation of radar systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944521$q$, $q$Health data generated by Internet of Things (IoT) devices outside institutional boundaries-consumer wearables and home-based clinical monitors-expands the attack surface for unauthorized access and complicates patient-side control over data sharing, a problem that conventional, centrally gated Electronic Health Record (EHR) architectures do not solve. Regulations such as the GDPR and the forthcoming European Health Data Space (EHDS) increasingly require auditable, revocable, patient-controlled access. This paper presents IoT2Wallets, a permissioned blockchain access-control and consent-auditing architecture combining decentralized identifiers (DIDs), W3C verifiable credentials, and two Solidity smart contracts (ConsentManager and AccessLog) on a four-node Hyperledger Besu network running IBFT 2.0 consensus (f = 1 of 4 nodes). We define a STRIDE-organized threat model describing the smart-contract-enforced mitigations in place and the threats left unaddressed. The architecture was implemented and evaluated across patient-controlled and institution-controlled consent scenarios, integrating HL7 FHIR R4 clinical and wearable data from a Parkinson's disease remote-monitoring pilot (18 patients, 20 healthcare professional evaluators). Across 200 test transactions per operation, verifiable credential verification averaged 95 (SD 12) ms, and end-to-end access logging latency averaged 3840 (SD 490) ms, with 0/200 observed failures across consent-verification and revocation tests (Wilson 95% CI upper bound ≈ 1.8%) and complete access log fidelity relative to the independently maintained gateway request log. We discuss the scope and limits of the security guarantees established-access control and auditability rather than comprehensive cryptographic protection, formal verification, or independent smart-contract audit-and outline the hardening work required before production deployment. These results are contingent on the current implementation, which has undergone internal code review only; no independent smart-contract audit or formal verification has yet been performed, and this absence should be read as the primary limit on the security claims that follow, not a minor caveat. In addition, all four ledger nodes in the pilot were operated by the research team, so the pilot does not test the multi-institution trust argument for using a blockchain rather than a signed log.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42944526$q$, $q$Object detection has evolved dramatically over three decades, transitioning from handcrafted features to deep learning, transformers, and now large language models. This paper provides a comprehensive systematic review of this evolution, covering traditional methods (SIFT, HOG, Viola-Jones), CNN-based single- and two-stage detectors (R-CNN family, YOLO v1-v12, SSD), transformer-based architectures (DETR, ViT, Swin), and emerging LLM-driven approaches (GPT-4V, Grounding DINO, YOLO-World). We benchmark and compare these methods using key metrics-mAP, IoU, FPS-and analyze over 50 datasets across generic, autonomous driving, medical, remote sensing, and agricultural domains. Our critical synthesis identifies persistent research gaps: lightweight edge deployment, sensor fusion for real-time systems, bias and fairness in AI, and multimodal vision-language integration. By consolidating three decades of progress and outlining future challenges, this survey serves as a valuable resource for researchers and practitioners seeking to develop accurate, efficient, and reliable object detection systems.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945183$q$, $q$Mpox remains an important public health concern in Africa, particularly following the spread of clade Ib in several countries. In Burundi, limited information is available on population knowledge, risk perception, preventive behaviours, and willingness to accept mpox vaccination. This study assessed social and behavioural factors associated with mpox preparedness among the general population. We conducted a nationwide cross-sectional survey between March and December 2025 across five provinces of Burundi. A total of 5,177 participants aged 15 years and older were interviewed using a structured questionnaire adapted from the Africa CDC behavioural and social sciences framework for mpox. Sampling weights were not applied; therefore, findings should be interpreted as estimates among surveyed participants rather than nationally weighted population estimates. Univariate analyses were conducted to describe key variables, while bivariate and multivariable analyses using modified Poisson regression models with generalized estimating equations were performed to identify factors associated with mpox knowledge and prevention and control practices. Overall, most participants had heard about mpox (94.7%). Many correctly identified skin rash (94.4%) and fever (84.3%) as common symptoms, and most recognized direct contact as a mode of transmission (86.6%). However, 21.4% of participants incorrectly identified bacteria as the causative agent and 24.5% did not know the causative agent. Radio was the main source of information (82.0%) and the most trusted source (87.0%). Higher income was associated with better mpox knowledge. Prevention and control practices varied by knowledge level, income, marital status, and HIV status. Although most participants (86.1%) were unaware of the availability of a vaccine, 76.5% reported willingness to accept vaccination if available. Despite high awareness and willingness to accept vaccination, important gaps in mpox knowledge remain, particularly among lower-income groups. Strengthening accessible and targeted risk communication strategies will be essential to improve mpox preparedness in Burundi.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945187$q$, $q$Background: To prevent wildfires, electric utilities may preemptively de-energize power lines (public safety power shutoffs [PSPS]). These PSPS events may unintentionally harm health, particularly for those with pre-existing conditions like chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) or who rely on electricity-dependent medical equipment. Health impacts may worsen further if a wildfire occurs despite the power shutoff. To assess the effect of PSPS events, wildfire fine particulate matter (PM2.5) exposure, and their co-occurrence on cause-specific healthcare utilization. Methods and findings: We identified PSPS events in California from 2013 to 2019, categorizing magnitude based on number of people without power by zip code. We leveraged zip code-level average daily wildfire PM2.5 estimates and respiratory, COPD-specific, cardiovascular, and psychiatric emergency department visits and unscheduled hospital admissions among adults aged 20+ from the California Department of Health Care Access and Information. Using a time-stratified case-crossover design, we estimated the impact of same-day and lagged PSPS events alone, wildfire PM2.5 alone, and their co-occurrence on healthcare utilization, adjusting for daily temperature. Severe PSPS events were associated with elevated odds of same-day respiratory encounters (OR = 1.06, 95% CI: 1.02, 1.11) and lag0-3 effects were similar. These associations were stronger for COPD. Compared to no PSPS or wildfire exposure, joint PSPS and 10 μg/m3 higher wildfire PM2.5 was associated with higher odds of respiratory encounters (1.16 [95% CI: 1.10, 1.22]), with evidence of multiplicative interaction. Associations were null for cardiovascular and psychiatric encounters. Limitations include exposure misclassification, because we assigned exposure at the residential zip code level, aggregating circuit-level data and not accounting for individual behavior or the potential for PSPS events to impact wildfire PM2.5 exposure. Conclusions: PSPS events may increase risk of respiratory events requiring acute care utilization, especially when they co-occur with wildfire PM2.5.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945224$q$, $q$En-route sector congestion poses a critical challenge to air traffic safety, operational efficiency, and resource-efficient airspace operation in high-density airspace. Because sector operations are spatially networked, locally emerging congestion may propagate through adjacent or upstream grid areas, leading to large-scale delays, increased fuel consumption, and elevated controller workload. This study proposes an operational spatiotemporal prediction framework for 15-minute-ahead sector congestion warning. First, processed grid-level operational states are converted into three congestion levels using K-means++ clustering based on six direct observable operational-state indicators. Second, a hybrid GNN-BiLSTM-Cross-Attention model predicts future congestion levels and local T2 hotspots from historical 5-minute grid-window sequences, without using future observed features. Third, SHAP/SHEP-style post-hoc grouped attribution is applied after inference to distinguish external triggering risk from congestion-propagation risk and to decompose external contributions from weather, restricted areas, controller workload, and wake intensity. A case study using 2023 processed grid-level operational data from the ZGGGAR15 high-altitude en-route sector achieves TEST@VAL Weighted F1 = 0.9773, L2-F1 = 0.9602, Precision = 0.9545, Recall = 0.9660, and AP = 0.9767 for rare high-congestion warning.The results indicate that the proposed framework provides accurate and spatially interpretable predictions with potential decision-support value for proactive air traffic flow management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945235$q$, $q$Modern library areas require constant environmental adjustment to preserve user comfort and promote learning efficiency. This investigation offers a Multi-Sensor Internet of Things (IoT) Data Source and Artificial Intelligence-Based Adaptation Control Model for Library Indoor Systems, which is intended to transform heterogeneous sensing data into intelligent real-time environmental management decisions. A Multi-Sensor IoT Library Indoor Environment Dataset containing 2,745 time-stamped observations was developed, thereby capturing dynamic indoor environmental variations. Normalization and noise smoothing processes were performed before the analysis of the collected data for improving the quality of the analysis. In order to detect the most useful features of the environment, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was used to perform dimension reduction and simplify calculations. The proposed approach includes edge sensing, intelligent data processing, and actuator cooperation for implementing an efficient closed-loop environmental management system. To classify indoor environmental conditions, a Colony Predation Algorithm enhanced with Random Adaptive Boosting (CPA-RAdaBoost) was developed, where CPA maximizes feature weights and model hyperparameters, Random Forest (RF) enhances ensemble variety, and Adaptive Boosting strengthens the identification of complicated and border environmental conditions. The adaptive control instructions for HVAC, lighting and ventilation are formulated considering the prevailing environmental conditions to facilitate indoor environment control. Experimental implementation using Python demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed framework, reaching a MAE of 3.9 and control accuracy of 97.1%, indicating its capacity for intelligent, responsive, and energy-efficient smart library environmental management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945305$q$, $q$Despite the increasing growth of sentiment analysis (SA) in several languages, Bangla is largely unexplored as a result of the low availability of annotated datasets, class imbalance, and challenging linguistic features such as inflectional morphology and code mixing. Existing work has concentrated mostly on limited or in-domain corpora, which leaves a void for general multi-domain sentiment resources based on real-world usage. The aim of the research work is to present such a balanced three-class sentiment dataset from Bangla social media text messages, with each post annotated by native speakers as positive, negative, or neutral. A robust preprocessing pipeline, including denoising, abbreviation expansion, and tokenization, was applied to improve data quality. Features were extracted using Count Vectorizer and Word2Vec embeddings, followed by the implementation of several machine learning (SVM, Random Forest, XGBoost) and deep learning (GRU, LSTM) models. In addition, fine-tuning was also applied to the BanglaBERT transformer model for comparison. The results show that BanglaBERT outperforms all other pretrained with approximately 80% accuracy, indicating good generalizability over sentiment classes. These observations support the success of transformer-based architectures trained on low-resource languages. The release of this dataset is expected to facilitate further advances in Bangla sentiment analysis and provide a scalable foundation for multilingual and cross-lingual NLP research.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945351$q$, $q$Soil nutrient analysis is essential for sustainable agriculture and precision farming. Traditional chemical tests for nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium are reliable but costly, time-consuming, and often unavailable in remote areas. The objective of this study is to develop a novel approach that combines laboratory chemical extraction, controlled image acquisition, image processing, machine learning, and deep learning to classify soil nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium levels. 50 independent soil samples were analyzed, chemically extracted, and classified as Low, Medium, or High for each nutrient. A specially designed black-box prototype was developed to capture high-quality images of soil extract samples under consistent lighting conditions. The images were used to extract color and texture information, which was evaluated using multiple classical machine-learning algorithms, such as K-nearest neighbours, support vector machines, logistic regression, random forest, extra trees, gradient boosting, and Gaussian naive Bayes. A convolutional neural network-based model was also used for comparison. To minimize data leakage, all validation procedures were performed at the physical-sample level, and augmented images were restricted to the training data. The best models obtained using nested cross-validation for nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium were KNN, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression, respectively, with accuracies of 60%, 82%, and 48%. The nitrogen model correctly identified the High category, and the phosphorus model correctly identified the dominant Low category each time. For potassium, Logistic Regression made predictions of all three classes and outperformed the majority class baseline. The Classical machine-learning models slightly outperformed the compact CNN for nitrogen and potassium, while both achieved the same accuracy for phosphorus; however, overall performance remained limited by the small and imbalanced dataset. The findings suggest that the proposed framework could contribute to image-assisted decision-making in smart farming and support improved soil management practices, particularly in regions where laboratory testing facilities are limited.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945358$q$, $q$Background: Hypertension requires lifelong treatment and regular follow-up to prevent complications; however, lost to follow-up (LTFU) remains a major challenge in long-term hypertension care, particularly in resource-constrained settings. Objective: This study explored patient perspectives on barriers contributing to LTFU in hypertension management. Methods: A qualitative study was conducted among 32 hypertensive patients who were LTFU at a tertiary care hospital in Coastal Karnataka, India. Participants were aged 31-69years, with 21 (65.6%) men and 11 (34.4%) women. Participants were identified through hospital records and interviewed using a semi-structured interview guide. Interviews were conducted from February to April 2026 Interviews were audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim, and analysed thematically following COREQ guidelines. Results: Six major themes emerged.: (1) illness perception and self-management practices, (2) care-seeking and follow-up behaviour, (3) structural and socioeconomic barriers, (4) health system and continuity gaps, (5) patient-perceived strategies to improve continuity of care, and (6) health information continuity and interoperability needs. Participants described symptom-driven healthcare seeking, medication non-adherence, occupational responsibilities, and competing family priorities contributing to irregular follow-up. Financial burden, including treatment costs, transportation expenses, and wage loss, substantially affected continuity of care. Long travel distances, dependence on family members, overcrowding, rushed consultations, and inadequate follow-up mechanisms further influenced disengagement from care. Participants also reported fragmented healthcare utilisation, repeated investigations, dependence on paper-based records, and poor continuity of medical information across healthcare facilities. Several participants perceived that integrated digital health information systems, decentralised services, appointment reminders, and improved counselling could strengthen long-term hypertension follow-up. Conclusions: LTFU in hypertension care is influenced by interconnected individual, socioeconomic, and health system-related barriers. A notable finding was the fragmented nature of continuity of care and poor health information continuity across healthcare facilities. Strengthening patient-centred follow-up systems, decentralised chronic disease services, financial protection mechanisms, and interoperable health information systems may improve continuity of hypertension care and reduce long-term disengagement from chronic disease management.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42945374$q$, $q$Background: Type 2 diabetes (T2DM) is one of the leading chronic diseases worldwide, requiring effective long-term self-management (SM) to improve health outcomes and reduce complications. Although structured diabetes pathways exist in Belgium, national reports indicate that adherence to the recommended diabetes follow-up and routine monitoring remains suboptimal. Digital health technologies have shown promise in supporting SM; however, little is known about how stakeholders within the Flemish health care system perceive opportunities to improve SM and implement digital health interventions. Objective: This study aimed to explore stakeholders' perspectives on improving diabetes SM and the potential role of digital tools in supporting people with T2DM in the Flemish health care system. Methods: A qualitative study was conducted using 2 online semistructured focus group discussions with 19 stakeholders, including health care professionals (HCPs) from primary, secondary, and tertiary care settings; researchers; digital health experts; and an individual living with T2DM. Participants were recruited using maximum variation purposive sampling followed by snowball sampling. The discussion guide was informed by the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research and current evidence on diabetes SM and digital health. Discussions were audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim, translated from Dutch into English, analyzed using Braun and Clarke's 6-phase reflexive thematic analysis, and supported by NVivo (QSR International). Results: Five key themes were identified: (1) Empowerment through education, (2) Tailoring education to individual needs, (3) Multidisciplinary and cross-sectoral collaboration, (4) Opportunities for digital technology in self-management, and (5) Regional and national systemic limitations. Participants consistently emphasized that effective diabetes SM may extend beyond patients' individual behaviors and depends on tailored education, coordinated multidisciplinary care across the health care sectors, supportive organizational structures, and supportive health policies. Digital health technologies were perceived as valuable tools to facilitate patient education, communication, information sharing, and continuity of care. However, participants also highlighted that successful implementation depends on addressing broader organizational and system-level barriers, including fragmented digital infrastructures, limited interoperability, and regulatory constraints within the Belgian health care system. Conclusions: Stakeholders viewed improving diabetes SM as a shared responsibility between patients, HCPs, and the health care system. While digital health technologies may strengthen SM support, their value depends on how well they complement existing care processes and address contextual implementation challenges. These findings provide context-specific insights that may inform the development and implementation of digital interventions for T2DM in Belgium and similar health care settings. Further qualitative research involving a larger and more diverse sample of people living with T2DM is warranted to better understand patient experiences and support needs.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42947671$q$, $q$Background: Healthcare Education and Research Training (HEART) is an engaged learning initiative that supported undergraduate students in acquiring and applying healthcare research skills over the course of an academic year. The project was offered through a Saskatchewan, Canada-based university in conjunction with provincial health system and patient-oriented research organizations. Designed to serve a dual aim, this pilot project provided undergraduate students with meaningful healthcare research skills while sharing qualitative data from underserved communities with health system partners in an effort to advance a more person-centered Learning Health System in Saskatchewan. Methods: Upper-year undergraduate students were recruited through various pathways. Over 14 weeks, the project ran in two phases: research training in Term 1 and collecting patient-reported experience data in Term 2. Results: Twenty-eight undergraduate students from differing academic backgrounds completed training in key concepts of research ethics, privacy, confidentiality, and other essential preparation for engaging in patient- and community-oriented healthcare research. Students conducted 35 telephone interviews with health system users regarding care experiences and facilitated four focus groups with new mothers across diverse community settings. Following data collection, students completed thematic analysis and developed knowledge translation outputs, including reports that were shared with health system partners to inform system learning and quality improvement. Conclusion: This pilot project demonstrated a feasible, mutually beneficial strategy for embedding qualitative data into a Learning Health System cycle by using supervised undergraduate student researchers to collect, synthesize, and translate findings into actionable outputs. HEART generated timely, equity-relevant insights to inform decision-making and potential practice change for health system partners while simultaneously building foundational competencies for future healthcare researchers and care providers.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42947850$q$, $q$Background: Disease-modifying therapies (DMTs) are the cornerstone of remission-phase management for multiple sclerosis (MS), a prevalent autoimmune neuroinflammatory disorder of the central nervous system. However, their immune-modulating mechanisms carry inherent risks of systemic and neurological adverse events (ADEs), which remain a major barrier to optimal long-term care. This study aimed to comprehensively characterize the real-world safety profiles of all major MS DMT classes and identify clinically actionable safety signals. Methods: We analyzed 15-year (2009Q1-2023Q4) ADE reports for 8 classes of FDA-approved MS DMTs from the FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS) database. Four disproportionality algorithms (ROR, PRR, BCPNN, MGPS) were used for signal detection. Time-to-onset (TTO) analysis with Weibull distribution and multivariate logistic regression were performed to assess temporal risk patterns and patient-specific risk factors for important medical events (IMEs). Results: A total of 498,847 ADE reports (1,048,575 events) were included, with nearly 50% involving IMEs. The most common ADEs were systemic symptoms, infections, liver injury and alopecia. Notably, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML, ROR = 15.59) and spontaneous abortion (ROR = 2.54) were identified as high-risk severe events. Drug-specific analysis revealed distinct safety signatures: anti-CD20 antibodies carried the highest infection risk, natalizumab was strongly associated with PML (ROR = 45.62), and teriflunomide had the highest alopecia risk (ROR = 12.73). Approximately 70% of IMEs occurred within 2 years of treatment initiation. Multivariate analysis identified older age, male sex and longer treatment duration as independent risk factors for IMEs, while obesity was a protective factor. Conclusions: This long-term FAERS-based analysis identified potential safety signals associated with major MS DMTs, providing hypothesis-generating evidence to support clinical vigilance and prioritize further validation, but not to estimate incidence, comparative risk, or causality.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42947888$q$, $q$Introduction: Fertilization can alter both soil properties and crop canopy characteristics, with potential implications for hydrological nutrient export, yet field evidence on the relationship between canopy rainfall redistribution and nutrient-loss patterns remains limited. Methods: During the 2023 tobacco-growing season, we conducted a field experiment in a flue-cured tobacco-faba bean rotation system in the Erhai Lake Basin, China. We compared conventional fertilization (CF), organic substitution (OS, in which chemical-fertilizer N under CF was fully replaced on an equal-N basis with commercial organic fertilizer), and optimized fertilization (OF, with chemical-fertilizer N and P inputs reduced by 25% and K input increased by 25% relative to CF). Soil properties, aggregate stability, tobacco growth and nutrient status, canopy rainfall partitioning during three late-season rainfall events, and seasonal N and P losses through runoff and leaching were quantified. Results: Compared with CF, OS reduced soil bulk density by 8.53% and increased soil organic matter, porosity, and moisture content by 9.71%, 11.61%, and 15.35%, respectively, while also improving aggregate stability. OS increased leaf N concentration, maximum leaf area, and yield by 25.44%, 17.25%, and 11.14%, respectively, despite nonsignificant differences in leaf area index and canopy coverage. Fertilization regime significantly affected canopy interception and throughfall, with OS showing the numerically highest interception and lowest throughfall across the three events. OS reduced surface runoff by 7.04%, runoff-mediated TN and TP losses by 22.46% and 20.63%, and leaching-mediated TN and TP losses by 24.70% and 30.61%, respectively. OF showed more moderate soil and plant responses but lowerrunoff-mediated TP and leaching-mediated TN and NH 4 + -N losses. Exploratory analyses indicated numerically stronger associations of canopy interception with runoff-mediated than with leaching-mediated nutrient losses. Discussion: Contrasting fertilization regimes were associated with coordinated soil, plant, canopy-hydrological, and nutrient-export responses. Rainfall partitioning differed despite similar LAI and canopy cover, indicating that bulk canopy-size metrics do not fully capture canopy hydrological functioning. The stronger association of canopy interception with runoff-mediated losses suggests a closer relationship between canopy rainfall redistribution and surface nutrient transport, providing a process-oriented basis for evaluating nutrient-loss mitigation.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42947972$q$, $q$Introduction: Phosphorus (P) has low mobility in soil, so crop P acquisition depends on both P availability and its spatial accessibility to roots. We hypothesized that moderate P reduction under split fertigation could maintain maize productivity by improving the coordination between available P and root distribution. Methods: A two-year field experiment was conducted in irrigated spring maize in Inner Mongolia, China. Split monoammonium phosphate fertigation was applied at 105 (CP1), 127.5 (CP2), and 150 kg P 2 O 5 ha -1 (CP3), together with a no-P treatment (CP0) and conventional basal diammonium phosphate application at 150 kg P 2 O 5 ha -1 (CK). Root length density and Olsen P were measured in paired soil blocks within the 0-40 cm soil profile at silking, together with plant P accumulation, biomass, grain yield, and P-use efficiency. Results: CP2 increased Olsen P availability and root proliferation in the middle and deeper soil layers and showed closer spatial correspondence between root length density and Olsen P at 20-40 cm. These changes were associated with greater post-silking P accumulation and biomass. Reducing P input by 15% maintained grain yield at the level of CP3 and increased yield by 4.07% relative to CK, while apparent P recovery efficiency and P agronomic efficiency increased by 77.36% and 54.62%, respectively. Further reducing P input to 105 kg P 2 O 5 ha⁻¹ decreased P accumulation, biomass, and yield, whereas 150 kg P 2 O 5 ha -1 provided no additional yield benefit. Discussion: Under the conditions of this study, moderate P reduction under split fertigation maintained maize yield while reducing fertilizer input and enhancing P-use efficiency, and this response was associated with closer spatial correspondence between root distribution and soil available P.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948002$q$, $q$Field-scale information on rice seedling distribution is crucial for transplanting quality assessment, site-specific seedling-stage management, and precision agricultural operations. However, seedlings in UAV orthomosaics are small, closely spaced, and easily confused with soil, shallow water, plastic mulch, and shadows, which makes accurate localization challenging. Meanwhile, under high-density planting, existing segmentation-based networks often generate adhesive responses between neighboring seedlings, leading to missed detections and biased counting. To address these issues, this study proposed PPNet, a deep learning framework for rice seedling localization and distribution mapping from UAV orthomosaics. The framework uses root-based point annotation and integrates a rice-point perception module (RPPM), an adjacent decoupling module (ADM), and an aggregated attention module (AAM). These modules enhance seedling-point responses, separate adjacent seedlings, and introduce row-structure information. Experiments were conducted on a UAV orthomosaic dataset containing 3,013 image tiles and 220,553 seedling point annotations from different rice-growing regions. PPNet achieved an F1 score of 0.9029, an AP of 0.8881, a counting MAE of 6.99, and an R2 of 0.9553. The method also generated field-scale seedling point maps and density maps under different planting densities and field backgrounds. The results demonstrate that PPNet can provide reliable seedling localization and counting information for crop establishment assessment and targeted seedling management, supporting intelligent agricultural machinery decision-making in precision farming operations.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948041$q$, $q$Cardiovascular diseases remain the leading global cause of mortality and morbidity, necessitating continuous advancement in secondary prevention strategies. Cardiac rehabilitation (CR), a cornerstone of comprehensive cardiovascular care, has been significantly enhanced by innovations in exercise medicine, evolving towards more personalized and accessible paradigms. This review synthesizes the current frontiers in exercise-based CR, focusing on the synergistic integration of three transformative domains: technological innovation, precision medicine, and population-specific strategies. We first delineate the established physiological benefits and evidence-based efficacy of exercise therapy within CR frameworks. Subsequently, we examine the application of digital and intelligent technologies-including wearable devices, remote monitoring, artificial intelligence (AI), and big data analytics-which enable dynamic, data-driven patient management and remote care delivery. The paper further explores the paradigm of precision exercise prescription, guided by biomarkers, genetic profiling, and other individual biological data, to tailor interventions for optimal safety and therapeutic yield. Special emphasis is placed on addressing the heterogeneous needs of key patient populations, such as the elderly, women, and those with specific comorbidities like heart failure or arrhythmias, by outlining individualized strategies that improve engagement and effectiveness. Finally, we discuss future directions, highlighting multimodal technology platforms and in-depth precision medicine applications as key drivers for the next generation of CR, while critically evaluating the evidence readiness of emerging technologies and addressing implementation barriers. This review aims to inform clinical practice and guide future research towards developing more personalized, technology-enabled, and equitable cardiac rehabilitation models.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948044$q$, $q$Maternal mental health remains an overlooked dimension of global women's health, particularly in conflict-affected and low-resource settings where psychological distress is intensified by poverty, displacement, violence, gender inequality, and fragile health systems. Although maternal health agendas have achieved progress in reducing mortality, survival-focused approaches often fail to capture the psychological burdens that shape women's well-being, caregiving capacity, and intergenerational outcomes. This Perspective argues that maternal mental health should be reframed as a core component of maternal health rather than a secondary concern. Drawing on a life-course and gender-responsive perspective, we propose a conceptual framework linking conflict and structural inequality with social determinants, maternal psychological outcomes, and consequences for mothers and children. We highlight the need for integrated health-system responses that combine mental health screening, psychosocial support, community-based care, and gender-responsive policies to achieve equitable and sustainable improvements in women's health.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948211$q$, $q$Medical consumables constitute a core object of hospital asset management, and the traditional extensive management model can no longer meet the requirements of refined management in modern hospitals. This narrative review aims to construct a conceptual theoretical framework based on information ecology theory, to analyze the patterns of information flow in the field of medical consumables management, and build a digital asset management ecosystem centered on the four elements of "information platform-digital technology-responsible actor-social environment". As a narrative review, it synthesizes selected literature and policy documents rather than providing a systematic or exhaustive synthesis of evidence. The conceptual contribution of this framework is to address the "technology-heavy, system-light" limitation of existing research by shifting toward ecological governance through the coordination of the four elements, and proposing an operating mechanism in which the information chain links the closed loop of asset management. The framework is illustrated through a worked example of high-value consumable use during cesarean section. The framework is illustrated through a worked example of high-value consumable use during cesarean section. It provides a conceptual reference and a hypothesis-generating roadmap for future research and local adaptation, rather than a validated operational solution. All thresholds, targets, automated responses, and organizational arrangements mentioned in this article are proposed design principles for future validation, not empirically confirmed standards.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948215$q$, $q$The share of building energy consumption in global total energy consumption continues to rise. Transforming building envelopes into media for low-grade heat harvesting and self-powered sensing has therefore become one of the key technological pathways toward achieving the "dual carbon" goals. In recent years, ionic thermoelectric (i-TE) materials have attracted widespread attention due to their operational capability under low temperature gradients, readily available raw materials, and high thermoelectric potential. Endowing commodity building materials with i-TE functionality offers an effective strategy for low-grade waste heat harvesting in buildings and their low-carbon transition. Cement-based materials, owing to their natural porous structure, abundant pore solution ions, and multiphase solid-liquid interfacial environment, exhibit unique potential as i-TE media. This paper systematically reviews the research progress on the i-TE performance of i-TE cement-based composites (i-TECs). The core mechanisms of the thermodiffusion effect and thermogalvanic effect are explained, and a framework for evaluating key performance parameters is established. The paper examines how the intrinsic properties of the cement matrix govern i-TE behavior and summarizes the strategies currently employed to optimize performance. The applications of i-TECs in building energy harvesting, self-powered temperature sensing, and structural supercapacitor energy storage are surveyed, highlighting the multifunctional potential of these materials. Finally, this review outlines the key challenges in guiding the rational development of durable and efficient i-TECs, and provides an outlook on the development prospects and engineering application potential.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948253$q$, $q$Chronic pain is among the most prevalent and burdensome health conditions in the United States, disproportionately affecting rural populations who face compounding barriers to specialty care, including geographic distance, transportation burden, specialist shortages, and limited access to multidisciplinary services. These structural inequities contribute to persistent disparities in pain prevalence, pain-related disability, and opioid utilization. Telemedicine is a promising strategy for reducing this access gap, with adoption accelerating markedly following the COVID-19 pandemic. This narrative review examines telemedicine for chronic pain management in rural populations across synchronous video visits, telephone-based encounters, asynchronous electronic consultations, telerehabilitation, tele-delivered cognitive behavioral therapy, and mobile health applications. The evidence base is strongest for remotely delivered cognitive behavioral therapy and selected telerehabilitation interventions, whereas evidence for specialty video programs, electronic consultations, Veterans Health Administration programs, and mobile health remains more heterogeneous and frequently observational. Telemedicine can improve access, psychological outcomes, functional status, and patient convenience while reducing travel burden, but it does not consistently improve pain intensity and may worsen inequities when broadband, device access, digital literacy, or privacy are inadequate. Important implementation challenges include limitations of the remote physical examination, variable patient preferences, technology anxiety, and regulatory uncertainty governing telemedicine prescribing of controlled substances. A hybrid model that uses structured triage, low-bandwidth alternatives, technology-navigation support, standardized opioid monitoring, and clearly defined triggers for in-person evaluation represents the most pragmatic framework for rural chronic pain care in the U.S. context.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948287$q$, $q$Microalgae are third generation feedstock and an alternative for the development of biodiesel through sustainable application which are environment friendly. This paper delves in utilization of microalgae for biodiesel and other value-added products. The microalgae cultivation procedure can become cost effective by utilizing wastewater from domestic and industrial origin for the purpose of biomass and subsequent lipid production. This paper addresses all the breakthrough of microalgae in biotechnological applications and also how machine learning and artificial intelligence can be applied to make the process more favourable and technoeconomic analysis of harvesting and other process has also been discussed. The growth of microalgae and different methods and equipment to achieve high amount of biomass is explored efficiently. This paper emphasizes the use of heterogenous catalyst instead of homogenous because of its reusability and the easy availability of precursors and calcium oxide sources in the environmental wastes like oyster shells, egg shells and waste animal bones. Overall, this chapter delves into the advantages and challenges of utilizing microalgae for the process of biodiesel production and provides alternative methods to address and minimize those challenges for sustainable and economically feasible procedures.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948329$q$, $q$Breast hematomas resulting from traumatic injuries are poorly characterized and variably managed. Traumatic hematomas are less common than iatrogenic hematomas and lack a clear algorithm for assessment and management. Hematoma management in trauma scenarios is primarily guided by concern for ongoing blood loss and resultant variation in vital signs. As a result, management decisions are less likely to incorporate concerns about scarring and cosmesis, though these are commonly critical parts of breast care. Through a series of patients evaluated at a level one trauma center, this article explores the appropriate assessment, management, and follow-up for patients with breast trauma and resultant hematomas. We propose several strategies to address care of immediate traumatic injuries while also optimizing long-term breast health.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948573$q$, $q$Background and aims: Childhood immunization is a cost-effective public health intervention, and Bangladesh has achieved South Asia's highest complete immunization rate, yet village-level effectiveness of local awareness programs remains poorly evaluated. This study evaluated whether exposure to a village-level vaccination awareness program was independently associated with full immunization coverage among mothers in Jashore District, Bangladesh, alongside other maternal determinants. Methods: A community-based, comparative cross-sectional study surveyed 1,000 mothers of children aged 0-5 years, sampled by convenience from villages with and without an implemented awareness program, between June and August 2025. Bivariate associations with full immunization status were tested using Chi-square tests and 95% confidence intervals; multivariable logistic regression estimated adjusted odds ratios (AOR) for all predictors simultaneously. Results: Of 886 mothers with recorded status, 62.1% (95% CI 58.8%-65.2%) were fully immunized. Maternal education showed the strongest association, rising from 53.6% among mothers with no education to 80.9% among graduates (χ 2 = 52.60, p p p = 0.98) or after adjustment (AOR = 0.98, p = 0.90). Distance to health facilities (43.2%), fear of side effects (30.9%), and husband disapproval (25.9%) were the leading reported barriers. Mothers with unrecorded EPI status were more often less-educated than those with recorded status ( p = 0.004), a missingness pattern addressed explicitly in the Discussion. Conclusion: Maternal education, not awareness-program exposure, independently predicted full immunization. Program investment should shift from generic exposure toward literacy-sensitive content, husband-inclusive counseling, and outreach-site expansion to help close Bangladesh's remaining immunization gap.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948649$q$, $q$Crypto-asset disputes create distinctive barriers to effective remedies because they combine technical opacity, cross-border legal fragmentation, pseudonymous actors, irreversible transactions and uncertain enforcement pathways. These barriers are especially significant for vulnerable and non-expert users, who may be unable to identify the legally relevant harm, organize blockchain-based evidence, select an appropriate forum or obtain practical enforcement. This article develops a conceptual, normative and legal-design proposal for a hybrid Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) architecture that integrates off-chain artificial intelligence (AI) assistance with a minimized on-chain smart-contract layer. The model assigns AI systems a supporting role in legal information, triage, claim classification, evidence organization, explanation and negotiation, while reserving contested legal determinations for human review. A permissioned distributed ledger records only hashes, timestamps, procedural events, escrow states and execution proofs; personal data, communications, evidence and AI processing remain off-chain. Smart contracts are used as bounded enforcement tools for registration, escrow and execution of validated settlement terms, rather than as autonomous adjudicators. The article applies the architecture to three illustrative scenarios: asset seizure and recovery, exchange failure and mass claims, and crypto scams involving cross-border fraud. It then evaluates the model against European requirements concerning artificial intelligence, crypto-asset services, data protection, procedural fairness, cybersecurity and accountability. The contribution is twofold. First, it advances the concept of access to remedies by design, under which comprehension, participation, contestability, evidentiary integrity and enforceability are embedded into the architecture of digital dispute resolution. Second, it provides a technically specified evaluation framework for future prototyping and empirical validation. The article does not claim measured improvements in accessibility or efficiency; rather, it identifies testable design requirements and performance indicators for subsequent pilots, user studies and regulatory sandboxes.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948717$q$, $q$Background: Wildfire smoke has been associated with cardiovascular outcomes, yet annual counts of fire detections capture a different exposure pattern from the short-term smoke concentrations on which much of that evidence rests. We examined whether fire detection density was associated with hospital admissions for circulatory diseases across Brazil between 2013 and 2024. Methods: The ecological panel covered 27 federative units over 12 years, giving 324 unit-year observations. Admissions for International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision, Chapter IX diseases, came from the hospital information system/unified health system; fire detections from the Brazilian National Institute for Space Research; and population and territorial area from the Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics. We calculated Spearman correlations and then fitted negative binomial models of admission counts with a population offset and state fixed effects, adding year effects, temperature, poverty, and primary care coverage in successive specifications. Robustness was examined through three temporal subsets and a state-clustered standard-error analysis. Results: The period comprised 13,801,934 admissions and 2,318,883 fire detections. Ischemic heart disease was the largest individually specified diagnostic group, with 3,301,354 admissions (23.9%). All three correlations lay close to zero: between states ρ = -0.03 (p = 0.87), in the pooled panel ρ = -0.02 (p = 0.70), and after within-state centering ρ = 0.01 (p = 0.89). Across five regression specifications, rate ratios per additional 1,000 detections/100,000 km²/year ranged from 0.979 to 1.020, with the full model at 0.998 (conventional 95% confidence interval (CI), 0.970-1.028; p = 0.913). CIs included unity in every temporal subset and under state-clustered inference as well. Conclusions: No statistically significant association was detected at the annual state level. These findings do not establish the absence of cardiovascular effects from wildfire smoke; rather, the indirect exposure measure, temporal aggregation, crude outcome rates, and methodological changes in population and healthcare indicators each limit what the data can show.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948764$q$, $q$Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming healthcare and nursing education globally. Despite its growing relevance, limited research has explored how orientation sessions influence nursing students' knowledge and attitudes toward AI, particularly in maternity care settings in Egypt. This study evaluated the effect of structured orientation sessions on maternity nursing students' knowledge and attitudes regarding AI applications in obstetric nursing at Ain Shams and Beni Suef universities. A quasiexperimental pre-post design was conducted among convenient sample of (786) third-year maternity nursing students. Data were collected using a validated knowledge questionnaire and a Likert attitude scale before and after AI-focused orientation sessions. Sessions included interactive lectures and culturally adapted educational materials. Statistical analyses assessed within- and between-group differences. Preintervention, both groups demonstrated low-to-moderate knowledge and mixed attitudes toward AI. Postintervention, significant improvements were observed in knowledge and positive attitudes in both groups ( p < 0.001). Ain Shams students showed a higher postintervention awareness level (58.8%) compared to Beni Suef (19.8%). Attitudinal shifts were also more pronounced among Ain Shams students across multiple domains, including AI's role in education, clinical practice, and student assessment. The study concluded that the orientation sessions improved knowledge, enhanced attitudes toward AI in obstetric nursing, and produced significant differences between the two study settings. Integrating AI education into nursing curricula is essential to prepare future professionals for technology-integrated healthcare environments. Further research is recommended to explore long-term impacts and scalability across diverse educational settings.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948795$q$, $q$Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly reshaping healthcare globally and may offer opportunities to strengthen health systems in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), including Cambodia. AI-enabled technologies, including machine learning, deep learning, predictive analytics, computer vision, wearable devices, and generative AI, could help address persistent health-system challenges such as shortages and maldistribution of healthcare professionals, limited specialist services, unequal access to care, and constrained resources, particularly in rural and underserved communities. However, translating these technological opportunities into meaningful health benefits requires more than access to AI tools. Cambodia faces important barriers related to digital infrastructure, health-information systems, data quality and availability, workforce capacity, cybersecurity, algorithmic bias, affordability, and regulatory and ethical governance. At the same time, ongoing digital transformation, the expansion of electronic health information systems, emerging AI research capacity, and national efforts toward AI governance provide an evolving foundation for responsible adoption. This perspective examines the current and potential role of AI in Cambodia's healthcare system, focusing on applications in disease diagnosis, clinical decision support, telemedicine, public health surveillance, healthcare administration, and health-system management. Particular attention is given to the gap between technological feasibility and sustainable health-system implementation, including local validation, interoperability, economic evaluation, workforce preparedness, and long-term financing. AI should therefore be introduced through a phased, evidence-based, and patient-centered approach in which technology complements rather than replaces healthcare professionals. Investments in digital infrastructure, human capacity, locally relevant research, ethical governance, and sustainable implementation will be essential to ensure that AI contributes to improved healthcare access, quality, equity, and patient outcomes in Cambodia.$q$),
    ($q$epmc:MED:42948825$q$, $q$The Expanded Food and Nutrition Education Program (EFNEP), which is an outreach program provided by the Land-grant Universities through their Cooperative Extension System, was established in 1969. This makes it the longest operating, evidence-based nutrition education program in the country. It provides food and nutrition education to adults and youth from limited-income communities around the nation. In the state of Delaware, the University of Delaware provides EFNEP to New Castle and Sussex counties while Delaware State University provides programming in Kent County. Currently classes are taught in English and Spanish both in person and online. A series of classes teaches participants about eating a healthier diet, being more physically active, keeping food safe and using food resources wisely. In Fiscal Year 2025, the University of Delaware's EFNEP alone reached over 250 adults and over 1,200 youth within New Castle and Sussex Counties. Over 90% of adults and 80% of youth showed an improvement in their nutritional practices.$q$)
  ) as v(external_id, abstract)
 where s.external_id = v.external_id
   and s.abstract like '%…';

alter table public.studies force row level security;
commit;

select count(*) filter (where abstract like '%…') as still_shortened, count(*) as total_studies from public.studies;
